5200:
** ὑγρός**, ά, όν:
_comparative_ ὑγρότερος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 162b, etc.}}: _superlative_ **-ότατος**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 7.7, etc.}}:—**wet, moist, fluid** (opposed to ξηρός), ὑγρὸν ἔλαιον, i. e. olive-oil, opposed to fat or tallow, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.281, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.79}}; ὑ. πίσσα, νᾶπυ, **raw** pitch, **liquid** mustard, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“SIG” 1171.14 (Lebena, 1st c.BC), “IG” 42(1).126.22 (Epid., 2nd c.AD)}}; τὸ ὑ. ξύλον, opposed to τὸ ξηρόν, {{NT+8th c.BC+|NT.Luke.23.31; ὑγρὸν ὕδωρ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.458}}; ἄνεμοι ὑγρὸν ἀέντες winds blowing **moist** or **rainy,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5.478, 19.440, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 625, “Th.” 869}}; ὑ. ἅλς, πέλαγος, θάλασσα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.69. “P.” 4.40, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 259}}; ὑγρὰ νύξ a **wet** night, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 112a}}; ἐφ᾽ ὑγροῖς ζωγραφεῖν paint on **a wet ground,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.759c.}}
**2** ὑγρά, _Ionic dialect_ ὑγρή, ἡ, the **moist,** i.e. the **sea,** ἐπὶ τραφερήν τε καὶ ὑγρήν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.308; ἠμὲν ἐφ᾽ ὑγρὴν ἠδ᾽ ἐπ᾽ ἀπείρονα γαῖαν 24.341, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.97; ἐφ᾽ ὑγρᾷ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 678; πουλὺν ἐφ᾽ ὑγρήν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.27}}; so ὑγρὰ κέλευθα the **watery** ways, i. e. the sea,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|1.312, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.71.}}
**3** τὸ ὑ. and τὰ ὑ. **wet, moisture,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.142, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τόπων τῶν κατὰ ἄνθρωπον” 9, “Liqu.” at Title Page}}; **Liquid,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.172; γῆ ὑγρῷ φυραθεῖσα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 147c}}; ἐξερρύα συχνὸν ὑγρόν a quantity of **fluid,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 42(1).122.4 (Epid., 4th c.BC)}}; μετρεῖν τὰ ὑγρά **liquids,** {{Refs|[prev. work]22.1013.10}}; ἐπὶ ὑγροῖς οὐκ ἐξὸν δανείζειν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PGnom.” 232 (2nd c.AD).}}
**4** μέτρα ὑγρὰ καὶ ξηρά **liquid** and dry measure, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 746e.}}
**5** θῆρες ὑ. **water**-animals, opposed to πεζοί, [{{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.18 (\{1st c.BC-1st c.AD\}: Germanicus Caesar Epigrammaticus); οἱ ὄρνιθες οἱ ὑ. 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Philostrati majoris imagines” 1.9}}]; ὑ. ἀοιδός, of a frog, {{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 6.43 (from Plato).}}
**6** of the bowels or faeces, **loose,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 2.20, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 617a1.}}
**7** ὑ. σφυγμός a **damp** pulse, defined by {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 19.405.}}
**II** **soft, pliant, supple,** of the eagle's back, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.9}}; of the limbs and body, ὑγραῖς ἐν ἀγκάλαις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 941, compare 2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 34.7}}; ὑγρὸς τὸ εἶδος, of Ἔρως, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 196a}}; νεώτερος καὶ ὑγρότερος, opposed to σκληρός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Tht.” 162b; χορῷ.. ἔτερπον κέαρ ὑγροῖσι ποσσί 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 16.108; ὑ. ὀρχηστής 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 4.96, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 655a24 (_comparative_)}}; ὑγρὰ ἔχειν τὰ σκέλη, of a horse, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 1.6}}; of a horse's neck, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyn.” 4.1 (so in _adverb_ of colts, γόνατα ὑγρῶς κάμπτειν, ὑγρῶς τοῖς σκέλεσι χρῆσθαι, [prev. author] “Eq.” 1.6, 10.15)}}; of the hare, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyn.” 5.31}}; of the jackal, ταχυτῆτι διαφέρει διὰ τὸ ὑγρὸς εἶναι καὶ πηδᾷ πόρρω {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 580a30}}; also of plants, ὑ. ἄκανθος {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.55; ὑ. χολάδες 2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 1.10}}; σῶμα ὑγρὸν κείμενον lying **in an easy position,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προγνωστικόν” 3; ὑγρὸν χύτλασον σεαυτόν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1213}}; κέρας ὑ., of a bow, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 25.206.}}
**II.2** **languid, feeble,** of one dying, ἐς ὑγρὸν ἀγκῶνα.. παρθένῳ προσπτύσσεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1236; κἀπιθεὶς ὑγρὰν χέρα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1439.}}
**II.3** of substance, **flaccid, flabby,** σάρκες {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 598a9, 603b32, +others}}
**II.3.b** **tender,** νεοττοί {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 7.9; βρέφος 4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 1.4.}}
**II.4** **moist with wine, tipsy,** ὑγρὴν τὴν ψυχὴν ἔχειν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 117; ἡ διάνοια ὑ. γεγενημένη 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.713a; οἰνοβαρὴς.. ὑγρὸν ἀείδων, οὐ μάλα νγφάλιον κλάζων μέλος 2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 2.412.}}
**II.5** of the eyes, **melting, languishing,** ὑ. βλέμμα {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anacreontea” 15.21; ὑγρὰ δερκομένοισιν ἐν ὄμμασιν “Anthologia Graeca” 7.27 (Antip. Sid.); ἐπ᾽ ὄμμασιν ὑγρὰ δεδορκώς “APl.” 4.306 (1st c.AD: Leonidas Alexandrinus Epigrammaticus); τῶν ὀφθαλμῶν τὸ ὑ. ἅμα τῷ φαιδρῷ 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Im.” 6}}; also πόθος ὑ. a **languishing, longing** desire, {{Refs|“hymnus ad Panem” 33}}. _adverb_, ὑγρῶς βλέπειν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Epistulae” 33}}: _superlative_, ὑγρότατα καὶ πένθιμα μελῳδεῖν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 1.106.}}
**II.6** of language, **smoothly flowing,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Demosthene” 20.}}
**II.7** _metaphorically_ of persons or their tempers, **facile, pliant, easy,** ὑγρός τις καὶ δημοτικός {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Marius” 28; κόλαξ ὑγρὸς ὢν μεταβάλλεσθαι [prev. author] 2.51c}}; τὸ Κίμωνος ὑ. his **easy temper,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Per.” 5}}; **pleasure-loving,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}; ὑγρότατος ἐς ταῦτα **prone** to.., {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 5.8; ὑ. τῷ γελοίῳ 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Brutus” 29 (_comparative_).}}
**II.7.b** **soft, dainty, luxurious, voluptuous,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] 2.751a; ὑ. πρὸς τὴν δίαιταν [prev. author] “Sol.” 3; βίου.., ὃν πάντες εἰώθασιν ὀνομάζειν ὑγρόν 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 203; compare ὑγρότης 11.2.}}
**II.8** of the vowels α ι υ, **sometimes long and sometimes short,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 1.100.}}
**II.8.b** of στοιχεῖα, **liquid** (_i.e. _ λ μ ν ρ), {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 632.9, 7th c.AD: Hephaestio Grammaticus 1.3, +others}}
**III** _adverb_ ὑγρῶς, see above {{Refs|11.1 and 5}}; also ὑγρότερον δαπανᾶν spend **more freely,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ οἰκονομίας” p.73J.}}

5201:
** ὑδρ-ία**, ἡ,
(ὕδωρ) **water-pot, pitcher,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 926, “Ec.” 678 (_anapaest meter_), LXX.Eccl.12.6, “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2855.10 (from Branchidae), NT.John.2.6, etc.}}; ὑδρίης πέρι δῆρις (compare ἀμφορίτης) {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.1767: proverbial, ἐπὶ θύραις τὴν ὑδρίαν}} to break the **pitcher** at the door, 'there's many a slip 'twixt cup and lip', {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1363a7.}}
**II** **vessel** of any kind, e. g. **wine-pot,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 136}}; **a pot of money,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] “Av.” 602 (_anapaest meter_) (ἐν ὑδρίαις γὰρ ἔκειντο οἱ θησαυροί Scholia (at [prev. work]) (603)),compare “IG” 11(2).161 B 100 (Delos, 3rd c.BC)}}; ὑ. χαλκῆ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 47.52}}; ὑ. χρυσῆ, ἀργυρᾶ, {{Refs|“IG” 22.204.35}}; ὑδρίαι ἄρτων πέντε bread-**pans,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|POxy. 155.4 (6th c.AD).}}
**II.2** **balloting urn,** especially in lawcourts, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 9(1).334.45 (_Locrian dialect_, 5th c.BC), 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 17.33, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Tiberius Gracchus” 11.}}
**II.3** **cinerary urn,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Phil.” 21, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Dem.Enc.” 29, etc.}}
**II.4** **water-clock,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 5.75, 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Caesares” 325c. [ῑ in 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus [same place], where ὑδρείης is variant]}}

5204:
** ὕδωρ** [ῠ,
see near the end], τό, _genitive_ ὕδατος: an _Epic dialect_ _dative_ ὕδει in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 61, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 961}}; later _nominative_ ὕδος {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 475}}; _Boeotian dialect_ **οὕδωρ** probably in {{Refs|“IG” 7.3169 (Orchom.)}}:—**water,** of any kind, but in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} rarely of seawater without an epithet, ἄνεμός τε καὶ ὕ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.300, 7.277; but ἁλμυρὸν ὕ. 9.227, +others, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.26}}; of rivers, ὕ. Αἰσήποιο, Στυγός, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.825, 8.369, +others}}; so in Lyric poetry and Trag., ὕ, Ἀσώπιον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.3; ὕ. τὸ Νείλου 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 561 (Lyric poetry)}}: frequently in _plural_ (but only once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, ὕδατ᾽ ἀενάοντα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.109), Καφίσια ὕδατα}} **the waters** of Cephisus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 14.1; ῥυτῶν ὑδάτων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1599; ὕδασιν τοίς Ἀχελῴου [prev. author] “fragment” 271 (_anapaest meter_)}}: **spring-water, drinking-water,** οἶνον ἔμισγον καὶ ὕ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.110; ἀφυσσάμεθ᾽ ὕδωρ 9.85; ὕδατα καὶ.. δῖτοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 404a; πότιμον ὕ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.2.19}}; ὕ. πίνων **a water**-drinker, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 6.30, compare 19.46, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 349; ὕ. δὲ πίνων οὐδὲν ἂν τέκοι σοφόν 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 199, compare 4th c.BC: Aristopho Comicus 10.3, 3rd c.BC: Bato Comicus 2.9, +others: ὕδωρ κατὰ χειρός}} **water** for washing the hands, see at {χεί; φέρτε χερσὶν ὕ}. [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.171; ὕ. ἐπὶ χεῖρας ἔχευαν 3.270, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.146, +others; λοέσσας ὕδατι λευκῷ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.282:—on γῆν καὶ ὕδωρ αἰτεῖν and διδόναι, see at {γῆ} 1.2b:}}] —a curse was invoked upon those who refused fire (i.e. the right to borrow burning embers) or water or to direct a traveller on his way, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 62, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 2.15:—proverbial, ὅρκους ἐγὼ γυναικὸς εἰς ὕ. γράφω 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 811, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 25; ἐν ὕδατι γράφειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 276c}}; ὅταν τὸ ὕδωρ πνίγῃ, τί δεῖ ἐπιπίνει; if **water** chokes, what more can be done ? of a desperate case, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1146a35, compare ἐπιρροφέω 1.}}
**2** **rain-water, rain,** ὅτε λαβρότατον χέει ὕ. Ζεύς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.385; ὗσαι ὕδατι λαβροτάτῳ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.87; ἐγίνετο ὕ. ἄπλετον [prev. author] 8.12; πολύ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.70, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.99; ὕ. ἐπιγενόμενον πολύ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.6.28; τὸ ὕ. τὸ γενόμενον τῆς νυκτός 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.5, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.13: more definitely, ὕδωρ ἐζ οὐρανοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.2.2, 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor “Orationes” 50(26).35 (but ἐζ οὐρανοῦ}} is a gloss in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.77): _plural_, ὕ. ὄμβρια 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 11(10).2}}; τὰ Διὸς, or παρὰ τοῦ Δ., ὕ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 761a, 761b; τὸ ἐκ Διὸς ὕ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 2.6.5}}; καινὸν ἀεὶ τὸν Δία ὕειν ὕδωρ, ὕδωρ τὸν θεὸν ποιῆσαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1280, “V.” 261 (Lyric poetry), compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 3.4: _absolutely_, ἐὰν πλείω ποιῇ ὕ. [prev. author] “CP” 1.19.3: κεραύνια ὕ.}} thunder-**showers,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.664f; ὕ. πολλά, συνεχέα μαλθακῶς 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 1.1.}}
**3** for ἐν ὕδατι βρέχεσθαι, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.104, see at {βρέχω}.}}]
**4** in the law-courts, τὸ ὕδωρ was **the water of the water-clock** (κλεψύδρ), and hence **the time it took in running out,** ἂν ἐγχωρῇ τὸ ὕδωρ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 44.45; οὐχ ἱκανόν μοι τὸὕ. [prev. author] 45.47}}; ἐν τῷ ἐμῷ ὕ., ἐπὶ τοῦ ἐμοῦ ὕ., in the time allowed me, {{Refs|[prev. author] 18.139, 57.61}}; οὐκ ἐνδέχεται πρὸς ταὐτὸ ὕ. εἰπεῖν one cannot say (all) in one speech, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 27.12; τὸ ὕ. ἀναλῶσαι 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 2.6; πρὸς ὕ. σμικρὸν διδάζαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 201b; ἐν μικρῷ μέρει τοῦ παντὸς ὕ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 29.9}}; ἐπίλαβε τὸ ὕ. stop the **water** (which was done while the speech was interrupted by the calling of evidence and reading of documents), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 45.8; ἐγχεῖται τὸ μὲν πρῶτον ὕ. τῷ κατηγόρῳ.., τὸ δὲ δεύτερον ὕ. τῷ φεύγοντι 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.197}}; ἀποδιδόναι, παραδιδόναι τινὶ τὸ ὕ., to give him the turn of speaking, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.162, 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.114.}}
**5** generally, **liquid,** ὕδατος εἴδη τὰ τοιάδε· οἶνος, οὖρον, ὀρός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 382b13, compare 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ καρδίης” 12.}}
**II** part of the constellation Aquarius, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 399.}}
**II.2** a name for the winter solstice, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Paulus Alexandrinus Astrologus “A.” 4.}}
**III** Ὕδατα, τά, as the name of **places with hot** or **mineral waters,** Ὕ. Σέζτια, Latin _Aquae Sextiae,_ Ὕ. Νεαπολιτανά, etc., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Geographia” 2.10.8, 3.3.7, etc.}} [ῠ by nature, ὕ?~Xδωρ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.347, +others (usually with ὕ?~X when not at end of line), ὕ?~Xδατος 16.229, +others, ὕ?~Xδατι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.363, +others, ὕ?~Xδατ᾽ 13.109}}, and so always in _Attic dialect_ (except sometimes in dactylic verse, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1339); 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus frequently has ὕ ¯ δωρ}} (always at end of line except in phrase Στυγὸς ὕδωρ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.37), also ὕ ¯ δατος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.300, 312, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.475, ὕ ¯ δατι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.282, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.439}}; later _Epic dialect_ admits ὕ ¯ δωρ more freely, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.601}}, so that we find ῡ in the second half of the foot in {{Refs 7th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Cererem” 381, Batrachomyomachia 97, 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.290, etc.; also in 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 11.8}}.] (Cf. Sanskrit _udán-,_ _genitive_ **udn-ás** 'water', O{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **woeter,** O Norse **vatn;** I.-{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **u(e)d-** with suffix **r** alternating with **n** (ὕδ-ṇ-τος): _cognate_ with Sanskrit _u-ná-t-ti_ (root **ud-**), _3rd.pers._ _plural_ **u-n-d-ánti** 'moisten', cf. Latin _unda._)

5205:
** ὑετ-ός** [ῡ], ὁ,
(ὕὠ **rain,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.133, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 545; ποιεῖν ὑετόν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 263 (Lyric poetry)}}; especially **a heavy shower** (whereas ὄμβρος is continuous rain, ψεκάς or ψακάς drizzle), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.22, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 5.4, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 347a12, “Mu.” 394a31, 3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.203}}: _plural_, **rains,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Diogenes Cynicus Philosophus Apoll.3, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 653a4.}}
**II** as _adjective_ in _superlative_, ἄνεμοι ὑετώτατοι the **rainiest** winds, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.25 (where θυετιώτατοι codex 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, ὑετιώτατοι Hude). [ῡ 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus}}, _Attic dialect_; later ῠ in ῠετοῖο {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 273.]}}

5207:
** υἱός**, ὁ
(written ϝηιός in Ἀρχ. Ἐφ. {{Refs 6th c.BC+|1931.103 (Nemea, 6th c.BC)), declined regular υἱοῦ, υἱῷ, υἱόν, but in _Attic dialect_ Inscrr. only after 4th c.BC (except υἱός “IG” 12.529,530, 598, 625; ὑός [prev. work] 585, 828; ὑόν[prev. work]70.8), and then always so:}} —in earlier _Attic dialect_ and other Inscrr. inflected as a ῠ-stem (like πῆχυς), _nominative_ υἱύς (written **huihus**){{Refs|Klein “Vasen mit Meister-signaturen” 72 (“Brit.Mus.Cat.” 701) (ὑύς “IG” 12.571, 670, 686; _contraction_ ὕς [prev. work]663); _genitive_ υἱέος (ὑέος “IG” 22.4883)}}; _dative_ υἱεῖ: dual υἱεῖ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.46}}, written ηυιε in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12.775 (corrupted to υἱέε in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 20a codex B), υἱέοιν: _plural_ υἱεῖς (ὑεῖς “IG” 12.115.14, +others), υἱέων, υἱέσι (5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 571, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1001 (_anapaest meter_)), ὑέ[σιν] (“IG” 12.54.14), υἱεῖς (ὑεῖς “IG” 22.1.73): but _genitive_ υἱέως, and _accusative_ υἱέα, υἱέας, which are formed as though from _nominative_ Υἱεύς}} are rejected by {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 48,49}}, Thom.Mag.p.367 R., as not _Attic dialect_, though the two latter forms are used by later writers (as υἱέα {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euphorio Epicus 5, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Cynegeticus” 16, ὑέα “IG” 42(1).244.4 (Epid., 2nd c.BC), but υἱέως}} is _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.13, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 18.2.4, etc.): _dative_ _plural_ υἱεῦσιν}} is mentioned as a form that would be regular by {{Refs|12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1348.27:}} —Homer uses _nominative_ υἱός (very frequently); _genitive_ υἱοῦ only in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.238}}, elsewhere υἱέο; _dative_ υἱέϊ or υἱε; _accusative_ υἱέα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.350 (compare “IGRom.” 4.360.29 (Pergam., hexameters)), elsewhere υἱόν (very frequently)}}: _plural_, _nominative_ υἱέες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.10, +others, or υἱεῖς 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.248, 24.387, 497; _genitive_ υἱῶν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.587, 22.44, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.223}}; _dative_ υἱοῖσι (ν) only {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.418, υἱάσι (ν) 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.463, +others (never υἱέσι); _accusative_ υἱέας [prev. work] 149, +others:— he also uses the shorter forms, _genitive_ υἷος, υἷι, υἷα, dual υἷε (distinguished from the _vocative_ _sing._ υἱέ by the accent), _plural_ υἷες, υἷα; but these were confined to _Epic dialect_: their accentuation (in which codices agree with Hdn.Gr. 1.409) may preserve a trace of their Aeolic origin (see. below). The declension υἱῆος, υἱῆϊ, υἱῆα, υἱῆες, υἱήεσσι, υἱῆας (like βασιλῆος, etc., as though from υἱεύς)}}, belongs solely to later _Epic dialect_ poets, as {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.1093, 1119, 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “fragment” 110, “Anthologia Graeca” 9.23 (Antip.), etc. Dialect Inscrr. have the following archaic forms, _nominative_ υἱύς “IG” 5 (1).720 (_Laconian dialect_), “Leg.Gort.” 12.17}} (υιυις lapis); _accusative_ υἱύν {{Refs|“Inscription Olymp.” 30, “Leg.Gort.” 10.15}}; _genitive_ υἱέος {{Refs 6th c.BC+|[prev. work]6.3, “Schwyzer” 105 (Methana, 6th c.BC)}}; but υἱοῦ {{Refs 7th c.BC+|“IG” 9(1).867 (Corc., 7th c.BC)}}; _nominative_ _plural_ υἱέες {{Refs|“Leg.Gort.” 7.25}}; _accusative_ _plural_ υἱύνς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 4.40, “IG” 12.407 (Cretan dialect or Argive); _dative_ _plural_ υἱάσι “Leg.Gort.” 4.37 (as in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, influenced by θυγατράσι, πατράσι}} which have ρα = ṛ, cf. Sanskrit _pitṛ[snull]u_); ὑέεσσι {{Refs|“IG” 14.10 (Syrac.)}}; υἷος in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 55 (Thessaly, 5th c.BC) is perhaps the _Aeolic dialect_ _genitive_ (ὑός}} is _nominative_ rather than _genitive_ in {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“IG” 12.828); _accusative_ ὗα “Schwyzer” 625 (Mytil., 2nd-1st c.BC)}}; a _nominative_ ὑϊς (scanned - ?~X) {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“IG” 12.472 (Boeotia, 6th c.BC),compare 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 249 (see. below); _nominative_ _plural_ ὗες “IG” 22.3632.24 (hexameters, Eleusis, 2nd c.AD). The initial syllable is both υἱ-and ὑ}}-in _Attic dialect_ Inscrr. down to {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC (e. g. ὑεῖς “IG” 12.115.14, ὑέ[σιν] [prev. work]54.14, ὑόν see above), afterwards ὑ-, but υἱός}} reappears under the Empire; in Plato codex A usually has ὑιος, which is found also in T, codex B always has υἱός, editors restore ὑό; _accusative_ υἱόν is recommended by {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista}} [same place]; in Inscrr. of Pergamon, Magnesia, and Delphi, and in non-literary Papyri, ὑός is at all times less common than υἱός:—ὁ υεἱός {{Refs|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” (add.) 3857p; _dative_ υεἱῷ [prev. work]3846z82 (both Phrygia),compare “BCH” 11.471}}:—**son,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.366, etc.}}; υἱὸν ποιεῖσθαί τινα to adopt as **a son,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.28}}; υἱεῖς ἄνδρες grown-up **sons,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.88: _metaphorically_, Κόρον Ὕβριος υἱόν}} Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.77}}: rarely of animals, {{NT|NT.Matt.21.5.}}
**2** _periphrastic_, υἷες Ἀχαιῶν, for Ἀχαιοί, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.162, +others; compare παῖς 1.3.}}
**3** generally, **child,** and so υἱ. ἄρρην male **child,** {{NT+3rd c.AD+|NT.Rev. 12.5, “PSI” 9.1039.36 (3rd c.AD).}}
**4** frequently in {{Refs|LXX in periphrases (Hebraisms with various meanings), υἱὸς ἐτῶν ἑκατόν 100}} _years old, _{{LXX|LXX.Gen.11.10, +others}}; υἱοὶ ἀδικίας {{Refs|“2 Ki.” 7.10; υἱοὶ θανατώσεως “1 Ki.” 26.16; υἱοὶ τῶν συμμίζεων}}**hostages,** {{LXX+NT|LXX.2Ki.14.14; so υἱὸς εἰρήνης NT.Luke.10.6.}}
**5** in some dialects, including the _Ionic dialect_ Prose of {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, υἱός is replaced by παῖς: υἱός}} is rare in Trag., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 609, “fragment” 320, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1689 (_anapaest meter_), +others, and 7 times in 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus: 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus has both words in this sense.}}
**6** as a general term of affection, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PGiss.” 68.2 (2nd c.AD), “POxy.” 1219.2 (3rd c.AD)}}; υἱέ, an author's address to the reader, {{LXX|LXX.Pro.1.8, +others}}
**7** δάμου υἱός, υἱὸς πόλεως, Ἑλλάδος, as titles of honour, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“SIG” 804.10 (Cos, 1st c.AD), 813A,B (Delph., 1st c.AD), 854 (Eleusis, 1st c.AD).}}
**8** υἱοὶ ἀνθρώπων **sons** of men, _periphrastic_ for **men** (compare above 2,4), {{LXX|LXX.Psa.89(90).3}}; οἱ υἱοὶ τῶν ἀ.{{LXX+NT|LXX.Gen.11.5, NT.Mark.3.28; υἱὸς ἀνθρώπου}}**man,** {{LXX|LXX.Eze.2.1, 3, +others}}; of the Messiah,{{LXX+NT|LXX.Dan.7.13, NT.Rev.14.14}}; used by Jesus of himself, {{NT|NT.Matt.8.20, +others}} (by Stephen recalling the words of Jesus, {{NT|NT.Act.7.56).}}
**9** υἱοὶ Θεοῦ **sons** of God, implying **inheritors of the nature** of God {{NT|compare(above 4), NT.Matt.5.9, compare 45, NT.Luke.6.35}}; implying **participants in the glory** of God, {{Refs|[prev. work] 20.36.}}
**9.b** of Jesus, τὸ γεννώμενον κληθήσεται υἱὸς Θεοῦ {{Refs|[prev. work] 1.35}}; ὁ Χριστός, ὁ υἱὸς τοῦ Θεοῦ, {{NT|NT.Matt.26.63, cf. NT.John.1.34.}}
**9.c** Θεοῦ υἱός, = Latin {{Refs|“Divi” (i.e. “Caesaris”)}} **filius,** patronymic of Augustus, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“BGU” 543.3 (1st c.BC), “PTeb.” 382.21 (1st c.BC), “IG” 12(3).174.2 (Epist. ad Cnidios, 5 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator)}}. [Hom.sometimes has the first syllable short in _nominative_, _vocative_ and _accusative_ _singular_, οὐδὲ Δρύαντος υἱός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.130; Ἀμφιτρύωνος υἱός 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.270; Ποδῆς υἱὸς Ἠετίωνος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.575, compare 590; Ἀνθεμίωνος υἱόν 4.473; Σελάγου υἱόν 5.612; Ἕκτορ, υἱὲ Πριάμοιο 7.47; and Πηλῆος υἱός, Μηκιστῆος υἱός seem to be the better readings in 1.489, 2.566: in these places some other form ought perhaps to be restored, but none of the known forms has a short ῠ: ὑός has ῡ in “IG” 12.585 (6th c.BC), 828 (5th c.BC), 2.2338, 22.4319 (both 4th c.BC)}}; Simon. [prev. cited] seems to have used a _monosyllable_ _nominative_ υἷς, and Hdn.Gr. may have read it as ὕις (??), but this is uncertain, as in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|Scholia 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.266 he seems to say that ὕις (υἷις codex)}} does not occur.] (Prob. from ***sū-yú-s,** cf. Sanskrit _sūte_ 'procreate', Tocharian (A-_dialogue_) **se,** (B-_dialogue_) **soyä** 'son'; different suffix in ***sū-nu-s,** Sanskrit _sūnūs,_ etc., and in ***s[ucaron]-nu-s,** O{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **sunu,** etc. (all = **son**); ***sūyú-** perhaps became ***s[ucaron]wyú-,** then ***suiwú-;** υἱός and υἱόν perhaps by dissimilation from υἱύς υἱύν, since the o-stem forms appear first where υ-υ would otherwise be repeated; ὗϊς (ὑΐς) may be another dissimilation; the precise origin of υἷος υἷι υἷες etc. is uncertain.)

5208:
** ὕλη** [ῡ], ἡ,
**forest, woodland,** {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.155, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.316, NT.Jam.3.5, etc.; γῆν.. δασέαν ὕλῃ παντοίῃ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.21; ἀπ᾽ ὕλης ἀγρίης ζώειν [prev. author] 1.203}}; ὕλα ἀεργός virgin **forest,** {{Refs|Berl.Sitzb. 1927.167 (from Cyrene)}}; τὰ δένδρα καὶ ὕλη fruit-trees and **forest-trees,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.69 (compareδένδρον)}}; not only of **forest-trees,** but also of **copse, brushwood, undergrowth** (compare ὕλημα), directly opposed to to timber-trees, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.1, “Oec.” 16.13, 17.12, “PSI” 6.577.8 (3rd c.BC), 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.40}}: also in _plural_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Cererem” 386, 4th-5th c.BC: Hecataeus Milesius Historicus 291 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, 2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus 3.88, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.7.10, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 3.48, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Thucydide” 6, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 5.1.12, 15.1.60, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pyrrhus” 25, “Cat. Ma.” 21, “Comp. Cim.Luc.” 3, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Prom.” 12, “Sacr.” 10, “Am.” 12, 2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 12.2, +others, 4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 3.69, 252, 16.91, 36.70, etc.}}
**II** **wood cut down,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.257 (cf. III)}}; **firewood, fuel,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.418, 23.50, 111, +others, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.234, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.164, 6.80}}; **brushwood,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 7.36, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.75, etc.}}; **timber,** ὕ. ναυπηγησίμη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 705c; ναυπηγήσιμος καὶ οἰκοδομική 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 5.7.1, compare “IG” 42(1).102.50 (Epid., 4th c.BC)}}; also, **twigs** for birds' nests, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 559a2.}}
**III** **the stuff** of which a thing is made, **material,** (perhaps so of wood), {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.257}}; rarely of other **material,** as metal, οἱ παρ᾽ ἄκμονι.. ὕλην ἄψυχον δημιουργοῦντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.802b (compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 844, but ὕλη is Plutarch's word)}}: generally, **materials,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|PMasp. 151.91 (6th c.AD).}}
**III.2** in Philosophy, **matter,** first in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus (5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 93b, +others is later)}}; defined as τὸ ὑποκείμενον γενέσεως καὶ φθορᾶς δεκτικόν, {{Refs|“GC” 320a2}}; as τὸ ἐζ οὗ γίγνεται, {{Refs|“Metaphorical” 1032a17; οὐσία ἥ τε ὕ. καὶ τὸ εἶδος καὶ τὸ ἐκ τούτων [prev. work]1035a2; opposed to as δυνάμει τόδε τι to τόδε τι ἐνεργεία, [prev. work] 1042a27; opposed to ἐντελέχεια, [prev. work]1038b6:}} in later philosophical writers, mostly opposed to to the intelligent and formative principle (νοῦς), {{Refs 4th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Inst.” 72, etc.; ὕ. τῶν ἀριθμῶν 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de communi mathematica scientia” 4.}}
**III.3** **matter** for a poem or treatise, ὕ. τραγική, ποιητικαὶ ὗλαι, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.16.14, 3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 13.4, compare 43.1, 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 172.1, etc.}}; ἡ ὑποκειμένη ὕ. the subject-**matter,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1094b12, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.124 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}
**III.4** ὕ. ἰατρική **materia** medica, Dsc.at Title Page; so ὕλη alone, **materia medica,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] 1 “Prooem.”, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 17(2).181}}; ὗλαι τῆς τέχνης {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. passage], compare 6.77, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.83, 110, 2.15, 28; ἡ ὕ. τῶν ὁπλομαχικῶν ἐνεργειῶν 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.157.}}
**III.4.b** ἡ μέση ὕλη the middle **range of diet,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.46, 2.15; τροφιμωτέρα ὕλη [prev. author] 1.95, compare 36.}}
**III.5** _plural_, **material resources,** βασιλικαὶ ὗλαι {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.640.}}
**IV** **sediment,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 879, cf. Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1086, 1088 (hence ὑλίζω}} (ἀφ-, δι-), ὑλώδης {{Refs|11)}}; **mud, slime**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 70.9 (2nd c.BC)}}; ὕλῃ, ὕλει, and ἰλυῖ are conjecture for ὕδει in {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 961.}}
**IV.2** **matter** excreted from the human body, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.22, 23, 25, +others; ἡ ὕ. τῶν ἐμπυημάτων 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(2).256}}; **phlegm, catarrh, PMed.** in {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“Arch.Papyrus” 4.270 (3rd c.AD).}}

5209:
** σύ** [ῠ],
**thou**: _pronoun_ of the second _person_:—_Epic dialect_ _nominative_ **τύνη** [ῡ] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.485, +others}} (_Laconian dialect_ **τούνη** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ **σύ** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 16.6, 21.9}}; _Doric dialect_ **τύ** [ῠ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.85, 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 34, +others, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 3.33, etc.}}; _Boeotian dialect_ **τού** [short syllable] {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica “Supp.” 2.83, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 55.6}} (also τούν {{Refs|[prev. work] 50.27, 55.6): Nom.}}**σύ**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.31, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1035, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 29, etc.; _vocative_, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.193, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 165, “Pl.” 1069.}}—Gen. **σοῦ**, {{Refs|“Hymni Homerici” 29.4}}, elsewhere only _Attic dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 302, etc.}}; _enclitic_ σου, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 761, “OT” 538, etc.; never in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, who uses σεῦ, lines 3.206, +others, σέο [prev. work] 446, +others}} (also in Lyric poetry, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus (?)in “PLit.Lond.” 54, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 3.65), σεῖο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.137, +others; also σέθεν 1.180, +others}} (which also occurs in Lyric poetry, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 33, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 10.9, and Trag., 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 264, +others)}}, and as _enclitic_ σευ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.811, +others, σεο 1.396: 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus has only σέο 1.124}}, σεο (_enclitic_) {{Refs|[prev. work] 9, σεῦ [prev. passage], 3.42, 85, 7.38}} σευ (_enclitic_) {{Refs|3.36, 134, 7.49}}:—_Doric dialect_ τεῦ, τευ, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.19, 10.36, etc.}}; rarely τέο, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 17; lengthened τεοῦ 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 145, 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 84, and τεοῖο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.37, 468}}; _Boeotian dialect_ τεῦς {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 24}}; _Doric dialect_ τεοῦς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 59; also τιοῦς 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 74.27; τεῦς 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.126}}; τοι variant in {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.25}}; _enclitic_ τεος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 83; Cretan dialect τέορ 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}; other _Doric dialect_ forms are τίω, τίως, both {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Rhinthon Comicus 13, τίος 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 75.24.}}—Dat. **σοί**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.158, 167, 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 88, 6th c.BC: Mimnermus Lyricus 8, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 7,99, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 3, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.42, 6.86.ά, 7.52}}, etc.; _Doric dialect_ τοί {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 86 (oxytone)}}; _Doric dialect_, Lesb., and _Ionic dialect_ _enclitic_ τοι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|Alem. 33, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Frr.” 2.2,8, 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 79, 6th c.BC: Hipponax Iambographus 20, 6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 44, 75.3, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.76, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 10.104, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.115, 3.35, 63, 85; in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, Lesbian Lyric poetry, and _Ionic dialect_ Lyric poetry and Prose τοι is always _enclitic_, σοί never _enclitic_ (τοί and σοι are not found except σοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.359, 11.381, ς (οι) 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.170}}, and in codices of {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.270, 9.55}}; rarer than τοι in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, 3.69, +others)}}; in _Attic dialect_ both σοί and σοι (_enclitic_) are used (σοί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 361, etc., σοι [prev. work] 87, etc.)}}, τοί and τοι are not used; σοι is never elided except in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.170, τοι is elided in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.60, 347, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 55, [prev. author] “Oxy.” 1788 “fragment” 15 ii 9, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 28.2}}; _Epic dialect_ and Lyric poetry also **τεΐν**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.201, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.619, epigram cited in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.60, 61, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 930}}; also **τίν** [ῐ], {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 16, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.29, 8.68 (uncertain reading)}}; **τίν** [ῑ], {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] “I.” 6(5).4, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.20 (τίν}} before a consonant, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).93); also τεΐ 7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 53; τίνη 3rd c.BC: Rhinthon Comicus 13.}}—Acc. **σέ**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.256, +others}}; _enclitic_ σε,{{Refs 7th c.BC+|1.26, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 1.2, “Supp.” 23.9, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.42, etc.}}; in late Gr. σέν, {{Refs|“Anatolian Studies” p.76}}; _Doric dialect_ τέ {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 52, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.48, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.5, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 114}}; τ᾽ variant (codex R) in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 779 (on the accent see 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 54.14, 83.4)}}; τρέ (to be read τϝέ) {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}; or (_enclitic_) τυ {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“IG” 42(1).121.69 (Epid., 4th c.BC), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1225, “Ach.” 730 (uncertain in “Ach.” 779), 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.56, 78, etc.:—also τίν 6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 4, 3rd c.BC: Cercidas Iambographus 7.6, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 11.39, 55, 68.}}
**2** in combination with γε, **σύ γε,** **σέ γε**, etc. (compare ἔγωγε), **thou at least, for thy part**, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} and _Attic dialect_; _Doric dialect_ τύγε [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus [272], 5th c.BC: Timocreon Lyricus 1 (variant{τύ γα}); τύγᾰ 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.69, 71}}]; _Boeotian dialect_ **τούγα** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 55.6}}: _dative_ **σοί γε** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.557}}: _accusative_ **σέ γε** {{Refs|10.96}}, etc.:—also **σύ περ** {{Refs|24.398.}}
**3** σύ with _infinitive_ (as _imperative_), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.134, 4.163.}}
**II** Dual _nominative_ and _accusative_ **σφῶϊ**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.336, 4.286, +others}}, **you two, both of you**; **σφώ** (not σφῴ,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|compare 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 85.17), 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.574, 11.782, 13.47, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 344, 1543, etc.}}—Gen. and Dat. **σφῶϊν**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.341, +others}}; _contraction_ σφῷν once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.62, and always in _Attic dialect_ (Hdn.Gr. 1.475) and Trag., e.g. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 12, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 342, “OT” 1495, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 892e (codices 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus have σφῶϊν in “Tht.” 193c, +others, σφῶν “Euthd.” 273e, +others)}}. None of these forms are _enclitic_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 38.9, 85.12; codex A of 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 658c, 673e, 689a wrongly makes σφῳν}} _enclitic_; Ζεὺς σφὼ is prescribed in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.146 by Hdn.Gr. 2.93.}} —σφῶϊ is never _dative_; in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.286}} it is the _accusative_ depending on κελεύ; σφῶϊν is never _accusative_; in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.52 it is dative commodi.}}
**III** Plur. _nominative_ **ὑμεῖς**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.75, +others (before a vowel, 4.246, 7.194, +others), 6th c.BC: Pherecydes Syrius Philosophus 11, 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 29a, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.72, etc.}}, **ye, you**; _Aeolic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ ὔμμες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.274, +others, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 24, 25}}; _Doric dialect_ ὑμές {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 60, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 760, 761, 862; ὑμέν “SIG” 685.118 (Crete, 2nd c.BC), “GDI” 5155.6 ([prev. passage], 2nd c.BC)}}; _Boeotian dialect_ οὐμές {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 6}}; a resolved form ὑμέες, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Parthenius Mythographus “fragment” 14, is a poeticlicence (so 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 93.3)}} rather than genuine Ionic {{Refs 5th c.BC+|(variant in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.22).}}— Gen. **ὑμῶν**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 143, etc.}}; ὑμέων (_disyllable_) [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.159, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.7, +others, 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 74.6 (ὑμῶν codices), 6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 11.5 (variant{ὑμῶν})}}]; ὑμέων also {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.73, 6.130, +others; as _trisyllable_, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 2.27; ὑμείων 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.348, 7.195, +others}}; _Doric dialect_ ὑμέων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 46}}; also ὑμῶν, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 95.23}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ὑμμέων {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 96}}; _Boeotian dialect_ οὐμίων {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 22.}}—Dat. **ὑμῖν**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.46, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.126, etc.}}; _Ionic dialect_ _enclitic_ ὗμῐν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 97.28}} also _Doric dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 91}}; _Doric dialect_ (not _enclitic_) ὑμίν [ῐ] {{Refs|[prev. author] 92}}; ὑμίν [ῐ] also in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 864, 1242, “OT” 991, 1402, “Ant.” 308, “El.” 804, +others (but ὗμιν}} should perhaps be _restored_ where the sense needs an enclitic on the principle stated by {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 35.6, 36.5, “Synt.” 130.23)}}; ὕμιν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|(as _enclitic_) is prescribed by Hdn.Gr. (2.124) in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.33, by “Etymologicum Magnum” 432.34 in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.376, 2.141, etc.:—_Aeolic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ ὔμμῐ, ὔμμῐν, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.316, 11.336, +others, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 328, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 14, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 26.9, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 11(10).17.}}—Acc. **ὑμᾶς**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 325}}, etc. (**-υ** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Argonautica” 820, variant in 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 222}}; ὗμας or (more probably) ὕμας is required by the metre in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 9.9, 47.11)}}; _Ionic dialect_ ὑμέας (_disyllable_) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.198, +others}}; _enclitic_ ὕμεας (_disyllable_) {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 2.60 (Papyrus)}}; ὑμέας also {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.126, +others}}; _Aeolic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ ὔμμε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.412, +others, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 6(5).19; also in 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 620 (trimeter), and 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 846 (Lyric poetry)}}; _Doric dialect_ ὑμέ {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 3, 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 52, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 737, “Lys.” 1076, “SIG” 528.3 (Cretan dialogue, 3rd c.BC), 622 B 8 (Cretan, 2nd c.BC).}}—The _plural_ is sometimes used in addressing **one** person, when others are included in the speaker's thought, as {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.81, 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 89. (With σύ}} cf. Latin _tu_, Gothic _pu_; with τοι Sanskrit _genitive_ and _dative_ **te**; the origin of σφῶϊ is doubtful; with ὑμεῖς cf. Sanskrit _accusative_ _plural_ **yusmān.**)

