**thou**: _pronoun_ of the second _person_:—_Epic dialect_ _nominative_ **τύνη** [ῡ] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.485, +others}} (_Laconian dialect_ **τούνη** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ **σύ** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 16.6, 21.9}}; _Doric dialect_ **τύ** [ῠ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.85, 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 34, +others, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 3.33, etc.}}; _Boeotian dialect_ **τού** [short syllable] {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica “Supp.” 2.83, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 55.6}} (also τούν {{Refs|[prev. work] 50.27, 55.6): Nom.}}**σύ**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.31, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1035, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 29, etc.; _vocative_, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.193, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 165, “Pl.” 1069.}}—Gen. **σοῦ**, {{Refs|“Hymni Homerici” 29.4}}, elsewhere only _Attic dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 302, etc.}}; _enclitic_ σου, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 761, “OT” 538, etc.; never in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, who uses σεῦ, lines 3.206, +others, σέο [prev. work] 446, +others}} (also in Lyric poetry, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus (?)in “PLit.Lond.” 54, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 3.65), σεῖο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.137, +others; also σέθεν 1.180, +others}} (which also occurs in Lyric poetry, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 33, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 10.9, and Trag., 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 264, +others)}}, and as _enclitic_ σευ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.811, +others, σεο 1.396: 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus has only σέο 1.124}}, σεο (_enclitic_) {{Refs|[prev. work] 9, σεῦ [prev. passage], 3.42, 85, 7.38}} σευ (_enclitic_) {{Refs|3.36, 134, 7.49}}:—_Doric dialect_ τεῦ, τευ, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.19, 10.36, etc.}}; rarely τέο, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 17; lengthened τεοῦ 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 145, 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 84, and τεοῖο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.37, 468}}; _Boeotian dialect_ τεῦς {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 24}}; _Doric dialect_ τεοῦς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 59; also τιοῦς 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 74.27; τεῦς 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.126}}; τοι variant in {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.25}}; _enclitic_ τεος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 83; Cretan dialect τέορ 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}; other _Doric dialect_ forms are τίω, τίως, both {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Rhinthon Comicus 13, τίος 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 75.24.}}—Dat. **σοί**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.158, 167, 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 88, 6th c.BC: Mimnermus Lyricus 8, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 7,99, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 3, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.42, 6.86.ά, 7.52}}, etc.; _Doric dialect_ τοί {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 86 (oxytone)}}; _Doric dialect_, Lesb., and _Ionic dialect_ _enclitic_ τοι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|Alem. 33, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Frr.” 2.2,8, 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 79, 6th c.BC: Hipponax Iambographus 20, 6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 44, 75.3, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.76, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 10.104, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.115, 3.35, 63, 85; in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, Lesbian Lyric poetry, and _Ionic dialect_ Lyric poetry and Prose τοι is always _enclitic_, σοί never _enclitic_ (τοί and σοι are not found except σοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.359, 11.381, ς (οι) 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.170}}, and in codices of {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.270, 9.55}}; rarer than τοι in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, 3.69, +others)}}; in _Attic dialect_ both σοί and σοι (_enclitic_) are used (σοί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 361, etc., σοι [prev. work] 87, etc.)}}, τοί and τοι are not used; σοι is never elided except in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.170, τοι is elided in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.60, 347, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 55, [prev. author] “Oxy.” 1788 “fragment” 15 ii 9, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 28.2}}; _Epic dialect_ and Lyric poetry also **τεΐν**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.201, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.619, epigram cited in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.60, 61, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 930}}; also **τίν** [ῐ], {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 16, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.29, 8.68 (uncertain reading)}}; **τίν** [ῑ], {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] “I.” 6(5).4, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.20 (τίν}} before a consonant, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).93); also τεΐ 7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 53; τίνη 3rd c.BC: Rhinthon Comicus 13.}}—Acc. **σέ**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.256, +others}}; _enclitic_ σε,{{Refs 7th c.BC+|1.26, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 1.2, “Supp.” 23.9, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.42, etc.}}; in late Gr. σέν, {{Refs|“Anatolian Studies” p.76}}; _Doric dialect_ τέ {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 52, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.48, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.5, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 114}}; τ᾽ variant (codex R) in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 779 (on the accent see 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 54.14, 83.4)}}; τρέ (to be read τϝέ) {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}; or (_enclitic_) τυ {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“IG” 42(1).121.69 (Epid., 4th c.BC), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1225, “Ach.” 730 (uncertain in “Ach.” 779), 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.56, 78, etc.:—also τίν 6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 4, 3rd c.BC: Cercidas Iambographus 7.6, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 11.39, 55, 68.}}
**2** in combination with γε, **σύ γε,** **σέ γε**, etc. (compare ἔγωγε), **thou at least, for thy part**, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} and _Attic dialect_; _Doric dialect_ τύγε [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus [272], 5th c.BC: Timocreon Lyricus 1 (variant{τύ γα}); τύγᾰ 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.69, 71}}]; _Boeotian dialect_ **τούγα** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 55.6}}: _dative_ **σοί γε** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.557}}: _accusative_ **σέ γε** {{Refs|10.96}}, etc.:—also **σύ περ** {{Refs|24.398.}}
**3** σύ with _infinitive_ (as _imperative_), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.134, 4.163.}}
**II** Dual _nominative_ and _accusative_ **σφῶϊ**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.336, 4.286, +others}}, **you two, both of you**; **σφώ** (not σφῴ,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|compare 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 85.17), 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.574, 11.782, 13.47, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 344, 1543, etc.}}—Gen. and Dat. **σφῶϊν**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.341, +others}}; _contraction_ σφῷν once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.62, and always in _Attic dialect_ (Hdn.Gr. 1.475) and Trag., e.g. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 12, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 342, “OT” 1495, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 892e (codices 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus have σφῶϊν in “Tht.” 193c, +others, σφῶν “Euthd.” 273e, +others)}}. None of these forms are _enclitic_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 38.9, 85.12; codex A of 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 658c, 673e, 689a wrongly makes σφῳν}} _enclitic_; Ζεὺς σφὼ is prescribed in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.146 by Hdn.Gr. 2.93.}} —σφῶϊ is never _dative_; in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.286}} it is the _accusative_ depending on κελεύ; σφῶϊν is never _accusative_; in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.52 it is dative commodi.}}
**III** Plur. _nominative_ **ὑμεῖς**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.75, +others (before a vowel, 4.246, 7.194, +others), 6th c.BC: Pherecydes Syrius Philosophus 11, 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 29a, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.72, etc.}}, **ye, you**; _Aeolic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ ὔμμες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.274, +others, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 24, 25}}; _Doric dialect_ ὑμές {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 60, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 760, 761, 862; ὑμέν “SIG” 685.118 (Crete, 2nd c.BC), “GDI” 5155.6 ([prev. passage], 2nd c.BC)}}; _Boeotian dialect_ οὐμές {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 6}}; a resolved form ὑμέες, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Parthenius Mythographus “fragment” 14, is a poeticlicence (so 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 93.3)}} rather than genuine Ionic {{Refs 5th c.BC+|(variant in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.22).}}— Gen. **ὑμῶν**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 143, etc.}}; ὑμέων (_disyllable_) [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.159, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.7, +others, 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 74.6 (ὑμῶν codices), 6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 11.5 (variant{ὑμῶν})}}]; ὑμέων also {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.73, 6.130, +others; as _trisyllable_, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 2.27; ὑμείων 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.348, 7.195, +others}}; _Doric dialect_ ὑμέων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 46}}; also ὑμῶν, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 95.23}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ὑμμέων {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 96}}; _Boeotian dialect_ οὐμίων {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 22.}}—Dat. **ὑμῖν**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.46, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.126, etc.}}; _Ionic dialect_ _enclitic_ ὗμῐν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 97.28}} also _Doric dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 91}}; _Doric dialect_ (not _enclitic_) ὑμίν [ῐ] {{Refs|[prev. author] 92}}; ὑμίν [ῐ] also in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 864, 1242, “OT” 991, 1402, “Ant.” 308, “El.” 804, +others (but ὗμιν}} should perhaps be _restored_ where the sense needs an enclitic on the principle stated by {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 35.6, 36.5, “Synt.” 130.23)}}; ὕμιν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|(as _enclitic_) is prescribed by Hdn.Gr. (2.124) in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.33, by “Etymologicum Magnum” 432.34 in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.376, 2.141, etc.:—_Aeolic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ ὔμμῐ, ὔμμῐν, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.316, 11.336, +others, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 328, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 14, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 26.9, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 11(10).17.}}—Acc. **ὑμᾶς**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 325}}, etc. (**-υ** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Argonautica” 820, variant in 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 222}}; ὗμας or (more probably) ὕμας is required by the metre in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 9.9, 47.11)}}; _Ionic dialect_ ὑμέας (_disyllable_) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.198, +others}}; _enclitic_ ὕμεας (_disyllable_) {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 2.60 (Papyrus)}}; ὑμέας also {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.126, +others}}; _Aeolic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ ὔμμε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.412, +others, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 6(5).19; also in 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 620 (trimeter), and 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 846 (Lyric poetry)}}; _Doric dialect_ ὑμέ {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 3, 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 52, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 737, “Lys.” 1076, “SIG” 528.3 (Cretan dialogue, 3rd c.BC), 622 B 8 (Cretan, 2nd c.BC).}}—The _plural_ is sometimes used in addressing **one** person, when others are included in the speaker's thought, as {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.81, 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 89. (With σύ}} cf. Latin _tu_, Gothic _pu_; with τοι Sanskrit _genitive_ and _dative_ **te**; the origin of σφῶϊ is doubtful; with ὑμεῖς cf. Sanskrit _accusative_ _plural_ **yusmān.**)

5211:
** ὑμέναι-ος** [ῠ], ὁ,
(Ὑμήν) **the wedding** or **bridal song,** sung by the bride's attendants as they led her to the bridegroom's house, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.493, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 274, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 707 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1036 (Lyric poetry, assuming variant): _plural_, παμφώνων ἰαχὰ ὑμεναίων 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.17, cf. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 922 (_anapaest meter_)}}, etc.: _Aeolic dialect_ **ὐμήνᾰος** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 91, “epigram Gr.” 418.7 (from Cyrene)}}: a form **ὑμήναιος** in {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Aetia” 3.1.43.}}
**2** **wedding,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 422, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1475 (Lyric poetry): _plural_, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 813 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 123 (Lyric poetry), 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Mus.” p.68K.}}
**II** ={Ὑμήν}, **Hymen,** the god of marriage, addressed in wedding-songs, frequently in Trag. and Comedy texts (Lyric poetry), Ὑμὴν ὦ Υμέναι᾽ ἄναζ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 314}}; Ὑμὴν ὦ Ὑμέναι᾽ Ὑμήν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 331; Ὑμὴν Ὑμέναι᾽ ὦ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1335; Ὑμὴν ὦ, Ὑμέναι᾽ ὦ [prev. author] “Av.” 1736, 1742}}; _Doric dialect_ Ὑμὰν ὦ Ὑμέναιε {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 18.58}}; hence the two are used as one word, ὑμὴν ὑμέναιον ἀείδων {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 1.341.}}

5212:
** ὑμέτερος** [ῡ], α, ον,
_Doric dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ **ὑμός** (which see): (ὑμεῖς):— **your, yours,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc.}}; with a _pronoun_ added in _genitive_, ὑμέτερος ἑκάστου θυμός the courage **of** each of **you,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.226}}; ὑμέτερος θυμὸς αὐτῶν **your** own mind, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.138; ὑμέτερόνδε}}**to your house,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.86: τὸ ὑ.}} **your part, your business,** ἢν μὴ τὸ ὑ. αἴτιον γένηται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.140.ά, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 522c}}; τὸ δ᾽ ὑ. πρᾶξαι **your character** is to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.70; τὰ ὑ.}}**your goods,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.2.12}}: in Prose sometimes with the Article, ταῖς ὑμετέραις πόλεσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 836c}}; and objectively, αἱ ὑ ἐλπίδες hopes **raised by you,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.69}}; ἐπὶ τῇ ὑ. παρακελεύσει for the purpose of advising **you,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 36d.}}
**II** _poetry_ (never in _Attic dialect_) sometimes for σός, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 19.2, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Delum” 204, 227, 4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 5.340, “Anthologia Graeca” 5.292 (Paul. Sil.).}}

5213:
** σύ** [ῠ],
**thou**: _pronoun_ of the second _person_:—_Epic dialect_ _nominative_ **τύνη** [ῡ] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.485, +others}} (_Laconian dialect_ **τούνη** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ **σύ** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 16.6, 21.9}}; _Doric dialect_ **τύ** [ῠ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.85, 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 34, +others, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 3.33, etc.}}; _Boeotian dialect_ **τού** [short syllable] {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica “Supp.” 2.83, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 55.6}} (also τούν {{Refs|[prev. work] 50.27, 55.6): Nom.}}**σύ**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.31, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1035, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 29, etc.; _vocative_, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.193, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 165, “Pl.” 1069.}}—Gen. **σοῦ**, {{Refs|“Hymni Homerici” 29.4}}, elsewhere only _Attic dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 302, etc.}}; _enclitic_ σου, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 761, “OT” 538, etc.; never in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, who uses σεῦ, lines 3.206, +others, σέο [prev. work] 446, +others}} (also in Lyric poetry, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus (?)in “PLit.Lond.” 54, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 3.65), σεῖο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.137, +others; also σέθεν 1.180, +others}} (which also occurs in Lyric poetry, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 33, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 10.9, and Trag., 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 264, +others)}}, and as _enclitic_ σευ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.811, +others, σεο 1.396: 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus has only σέο 1.124}}, σεο (_enclitic_) {{Refs|[prev. work] 9, σεῦ [prev. passage], 3.42, 85, 7.38}} σευ (_enclitic_) {{Refs|3.36, 134, 7.49}}:—_Doric dialect_ τεῦ, τευ, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.19, 10.36, etc.}}; rarely τέο, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 17; lengthened τεοῦ 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 145, 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 84, and τεοῖο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.37, 468}}; _Boeotian dialect_ τεῦς {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 24}}; _Doric dialect_ τεοῦς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 59; also τιοῦς 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 74.27; τεῦς 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.126}}; τοι variant in {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.25}}; _enclitic_ τεος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 83; Cretan dialect τέορ 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}; other _Doric dialect_ forms are τίω, τίως, both {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Rhinthon Comicus 13, τίος 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 75.24.}}—Dat. **σοί**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.158, 167, 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 88, 6th c.BC: Mimnermus Lyricus 8, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 7,99, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 3, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.42, 6.86.ά, 7.52}}, etc.; _Doric dialect_ τοί {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 86 (oxytone)}}; _Doric dialect_, Lesb., and _Ionic dialect_ _enclitic_ τοι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|Alem. 33, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Frr.” 2.2,8, 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 79, 6th c.BC: Hipponax Iambographus 20, 6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 44, 75.3, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.76, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 10.104, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.115, 3.35, 63, 85; in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, Lesbian Lyric poetry, and _Ionic dialect_ Lyric poetry and Prose τοι is always _enclitic_, σοί never _enclitic_ (τοί and σοι are not found except σοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.359, 11.381, ς (οι) 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.170}}, and in codices of {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.270, 9.55}}; rarer than τοι in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, 3.69, +others)}}; in _Attic dialect_ both σοί and σοι (_enclitic_) are used (σοί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 361, etc., σοι [prev. work] 87, etc.)}}, τοί and τοι are not used; σοι is never elided except in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.170, τοι is elided in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.60, 347, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 55, [prev. author] “Oxy.” 1788 “fragment” 15 ii 9, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 28.2}}; _Epic dialect_ and Lyric poetry also **τεΐν**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.201, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.619, epigram cited in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.60, 61, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 930}}; also **τίν** [ῐ], {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 16, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.29, 8.68 (uncertain reading)}}; **τίν** [ῑ], {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] “I.” 6(5).4, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.20 (τίν}} before a consonant, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).93); also τεΐ 7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 53; τίνη 3rd c.BC: Rhinthon Comicus 13.}}—Acc. **σέ**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.256, +others}}; _enclitic_ σε,{{Refs 7th c.BC+|1.26, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 1.2, “Supp.” 23.9, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.42, etc.}}; in late Gr. σέν, {{Refs|“Anatolian Studies” p.76}}; _Doric dialect_ τέ {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 52, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.48, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.5, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 114}}; τ᾽ variant (codex R) in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 779 (on the accent see 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 54.14, 83.4)}}; τρέ (to be read τϝέ) {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}; or (_enclitic_) τυ {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“IG” 42(1).121.69 (Epid., 4th c.BC), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1225, “Ach.” 730 (uncertain in “Ach.” 779), 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.56, 78, etc.:—also τίν 6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 4, 3rd c.BC: Cercidas Iambographus 7.6, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 11.39, 55, 68.}}
**2** in combination with γε, **σύ γε,** **σέ γε**, etc. (compare ἔγωγε), **thou at least, for thy part**, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} and _Attic dialect_; _Doric dialect_ τύγε [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus [272], 5th c.BC: Timocreon Lyricus 1 (variant{τύ γα}); τύγᾰ 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.69, 71}}]; _Boeotian dialect_ **τούγα** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 55.6}}: _dative_ **σοί γε** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.557}}: _accusative_ **σέ γε** {{Refs|10.96}}, etc.:—also **σύ περ** {{Refs|24.398.}}
**3** σύ with _infinitive_ (as _imperative_), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.134, 4.163.}}
**II** Dual _nominative_ and _accusative_ **σφῶϊ**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.336, 4.286, +others}}, **you two, both of you**; **σφώ** (not σφῴ,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|compare 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 85.17), 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.574, 11.782, 13.47, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 344, 1543, etc.}}—Gen. and Dat. **σφῶϊν**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.341, +others}}; _contraction_ σφῷν once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.62, and always in _Attic dialect_ (Hdn.Gr. 1.475) and Trag., e.g. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 12, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 342, “OT” 1495, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 892e (codices 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus have σφῶϊν in “Tht.” 193c, +others, σφῶν “Euthd.” 273e, +others)}}. None of these forms are _enclitic_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 38.9, 85.12; codex A of 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 658c, 673e, 689a wrongly makes σφῳν}} _enclitic_; Ζεὺς σφὼ is prescribed in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.146 by Hdn.Gr. 2.93.}} —σφῶϊ is never _dative_; in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.286}} it is the _accusative_ depending on κελεύ; σφῶϊν is never _accusative_; in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.52 it is dative commodi.}}
**III** Plur. _nominative_ **ὑμεῖς**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.75, +others (before a vowel, 4.246, 7.194, +others), 6th c.BC: Pherecydes Syrius Philosophus 11, 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 29a, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.72, etc.}}, **ye, you**; _Aeolic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ ὔμμες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.274, +others, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 24, 25}}; _Doric dialect_ ὑμές {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 60, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 760, 761, 862; ὑμέν “SIG” 685.118 (Crete, 2nd c.BC), “GDI” 5155.6 ([prev. passage], 2nd c.BC)}}; _Boeotian dialect_ οὐμές {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 6}}; a resolved form ὑμέες, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Parthenius Mythographus “fragment” 14, is a poeticlicence (so 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 93.3)}} rather than genuine Ionic {{Refs 5th c.BC+|(variant in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.22).}}— Gen. **ὑμῶν**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 143, etc.}}; ὑμέων (_disyllable_) [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.159, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.7, +others, 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 74.6 (ὑμῶν codices), 6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 11.5 (variant{ὑμῶν})}}]; ὑμέων also {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.73, 6.130, +others; as _trisyllable_, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 2.27; ὑμείων 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.348, 7.195, +others}}; _Doric dialect_ ὑμέων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 46}}; also ὑμῶν, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 95.23}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ὑμμέων {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 96}}; _Boeotian dialect_ οὐμίων {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 22.}}—Dat. **ὑμῖν**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.46, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.126, etc.}}; _Ionic dialect_ _enclitic_ ὗμῐν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 97.28}} also _Doric dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 91}}; _Doric dialect_ (not _enclitic_) ὑμίν [ῐ] {{Refs|[prev. author] 92}}; ὑμίν [ῐ] also in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 864, 1242, “OT” 991, 1402, “Ant.” 308, “El.” 804, +others (but ὗμιν}} should perhaps be _restored_ where the sense needs an enclitic on the principle stated by {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 35.6, 36.5, “Synt.” 130.23)}}; ὕμιν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|(as _enclitic_) is prescribed by Hdn.Gr. (2.124) in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.33, by “Etymologicum Magnum” 432.34 in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.376, 2.141, etc.:—_Aeolic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ ὔμμῐ, ὔμμῐν, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.316, 11.336, +others, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 328, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 14, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 26.9, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 11(10).17.}}—Acc. **ὑμᾶς**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 325}}, etc. (**-υ** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Argonautica” 820, variant in 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 222}}; ὗμας or (more probably) ὕμας is required by the metre in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 9.9, 47.11)}}; _Ionic dialect_ ὑμέας (_disyllable_) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.198, +others}}; _enclitic_ ὕμεας (_disyllable_) {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 2.60 (Papyrus)}}; ὑμέας also {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.126, +others}}; _Aeolic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ ὔμμε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.412, +others, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 6(5).19; also in 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 620 (trimeter), and 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 846 (Lyric poetry)}}; _Doric dialect_ ὑμέ {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 3, 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 52, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 737, “Lys.” 1076, “SIG” 528.3 (Cretan dialogue, 3rd c.BC), 622 B 8 (Cretan, 2nd c.BC).}}—The _plural_ is sometimes used in addressing **one** person, when others are included in the speaker's thought, as {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.81, 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 89. (With σύ}} cf. Latin _tu_, Gothic _pu_; with τοι Sanskrit _genitive_ and _dative_ **te**; the origin of σφῶϊ is doubtful; with ὑμεῖς cf. Sanskrit _accusative_ _plural_ **yusmān.**)

5214:
** ὑμν-έω**,
_Epic dialect_ **ὑμνείω** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 2}}; _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ὑμνεῦσιν {{Refs|“hymnus ad Apollinem” 190}}; _feminine_ _participle_ ὑμνεῦσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 11}}; _Aeolic dialect_ _infinitive_ ὔμνην to be restored for ὑμνεῖν in {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 5.2}}; _Laconian dialect_ _1st pers._ _plural_ _subjunctive_ ὑμνίωμες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1305 (Lyric poetry)}}: _Aeolic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ _imperfect_ ὔμνην {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 20c. 6}}: _future_ ὑμνήσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Parth.” 2.11}}: (ὕμνος):
**I** with _accusative_ of person or thing sung of, **sing of,** first in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 11, 33}}, frequently in **Hymni Homerici (Hymnus ad Apollinem**{{Refs 7th c.BC+|178, +others), Lyric poetry (7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica [same place], 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus [same place], 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.2, +others, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 10.13, +others), and Trag. (5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 367, etc.; θρήνοις.. σ᾽ ὑμνήσομεν [prev. author] “Rh.” 976)}}: also in Prose, **celebrate in a hymn, commemorate,** Ὦπιν {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.35; τὰς τούτων ἀρετάς 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.2, compare 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 5.33; [Παλαμήδη] ὑμνοῦσιν ὡς.. ἀπόλλυται 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.2.33, etc.; τὸν θεόν NT.Act.16.25}}; of the hymn itself, οὔτε.. μέ τις ὕμνος ὕμνησεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 815 (Lyric poetry): with double _accusative_, ἃ τὴν πόλιν ὕμνησα}} the points wherein **I praised** our city, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.42}}:—_passive_, **to be celebrated in hymns,** of gods, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“OGI” 56.66 (Canopus, 3rd c.BC), 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 9.23.3, etc.}}; also Ἀργεῖοι.. τὰ πολλὰ πάντα ὑμνέαται (_Ionic dialect_ for -ηνται) **are** everywhere **praised,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.67; ὑμνηθήσεται πόλις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1590; ἡ ὑμνουμένη χρυσῆ πλάτανος}}**famous,** {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.1.38; αἱ ὑμνούμεναι φιλίαι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1171a15; σοφία ἐν ἐξόδοις ὑμνεῖται LXX.Pro.1.20: _impersonal_, ὑμνεῖτο δ᾽ αἰσχρῶς}} foul **songs were sung,** {{Refs|Comica Adespota 1203.5.}}
**I.2** **descant upon,** in song or speech, ἐν κατηρεφεῖ στέγῃ.. ὑμνήσεις κακά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 382}}; τὰν ἐμὰν ὑμνεῦσαι (_Doric dialect_ for -οῦσαι) ἀπιστοσύναν **ever singing of** my want of faith, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 423 (Lyric poetry)}}:— _passive_, Ἐτεοκλέης ἂν.. ὑμνοῖτο.. φροιμίοις πολυρρόθοις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 7.}}
**I.3** with _accusative_ _cognate_, **sing,** ᾆσμα, ὕμνον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 15, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1191: with double _accusative_, παιᾶνα.. ὑμνοῦσι.. τὸν Αατοῦς γόνον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 688 (Lyric poetry), compare “SIG” 711 L 12 (Delph., 2nd c.BC).}}
**II** **tell over and over again, harp upon, repeat, recite,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 317a, “R.” 549e, “Tht.” 174e, etc.}}; ὡς.. {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 364a; ὑμνοῦσι τὸ γῆρας ὅσων κακῶν αἴτιόν [ἐστι] [prev. work] 329b}}; τὸν νόμον ὑμνεῖν **recite the form of** the law, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 871a:}} —_passive_, ὁ δ᾽ εἶπε πρός με βαί᾽, ἀεὶ δ᾽ ὑμνούμενα ({{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia τὰ πολυθρύλητα) 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 292.}}
**III** _intransitive_, **sing, chant,** ὡς ποιηταὶ ὑμνήκασι περὶ αὐτῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.21}}; ὑμνῶν οὔποτ᾽ ἔληγεν ὡς.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 11.2.}}
**III.2** in _passive_ sense, φῆμαι.. ὑμνήσουσι περὶ τὰ ὦτα **will ring** in their ears, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 463d. [On the quantity, see at {ὕμνος}.]}}]

5216:
** σύ** [ῠ],
**thou**: _pronoun_ of the second _person_:—_Epic dialect_ _nominative_ **τύνη** [ῡ] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.485, +others}} (_Laconian dialect_ **τούνη** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ **σύ** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 16.6, 21.9}}; _Doric dialect_ **τύ** [ῠ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.85, 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 34, +others, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 3.33, etc.}}; _Boeotian dialect_ **τού** [short syllable] {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica “Supp.” 2.83, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 55.6}} (also τούν {{Refs|[prev. work] 50.27, 55.6): Nom.}}**σύ**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.31, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1035, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 29, etc.; _vocative_, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.193, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 165, “Pl.” 1069.}}—Gen. **σοῦ**, {{Refs|“Hymni Homerici” 29.4}}, elsewhere only _Attic dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 302, etc.}}; _enclitic_ σου, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 761, “OT” 538, etc.; never in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, who uses σεῦ, lines 3.206, +others, σέο [prev. work] 446, +others}} (also in Lyric poetry, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus (?)in “PLit.Lond.” 54, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 3.65), σεῖο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.137, +others; also σέθεν 1.180, +others}} (which also occurs in Lyric poetry, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 33, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 10.9, and Trag., 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 264, +others)}}, and as _enclitic_ σευ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.811, +others, σεο 1.396: 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus has only σέο 1.124}}, σεο (_enclitic_) {{Refs|[prev. work] 9, σεῦ [prev. passage], 3.42, 85, 7.38}} σευ (_enclitic_) {{Refs|3.36, 134, 7.49}}:—_Doric dialect_ τεῦ, τευ, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.19, 10.36, etc.}}; rarely τέο, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 17; lengthened τεοῦ 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 145, 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 84, and τεοῖο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.37, 468}}; _Boeotian dialect_ τεῦς {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 24}}; _Doric dialect_ τεοῦς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 59; also τιοῦς 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 74.27; τεῦς 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.126}}; τοι variant in {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.25}}; _enclitic_ τεος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 83; Cretan dialect τέορ 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}; other _Doric dialect_ forms are τίω, τίως, both {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Rhinthon Comicus 13, τίος 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 75.24.}}—Dat. **σοί**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.158, 167, 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 88, 6th c.BC: Mimnermus Lyricus 8, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 7,99, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 3, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.42, 6.86.ά, 7.52}}, etc.; _Doric dialect_ τοί {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 86 (oxytone)}}; _Doric dialect_, Lesb., and _Ionic dialect_ _enclitic_ τοι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|Alem. 33, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Frr.” 2.2,8, 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 79, 6th c.BC: Hipponax Iambographus 20, 6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 44, 75.3, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.76, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 10.104, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.115, 3.35, 63, 85; in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, Lesbian Lyric poetry, and _Ionic dialect_ Lyric poetry and Prose τοι is always _enclitic_, σοί never _enclitic_ (τοί and σοι are not found except σοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.359, 11.381, ς (οι) 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.170}}, and in codices of {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.270, 9.55}}; rarer than τοι in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, 3.69, +others)}}; in _Attic dialect_ both σοί and σοι (_enclitic_) are used (σοί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 361, etc., σοι [prev. work] 87, etc.)}}, τοί and τοι are not used; σοι is never elided except in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.170, τοι is elided in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.60, 347, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 55, [prev. author] “Oxy.” 1788 “fragment” 15 ii 9, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 28.2}}; _Epic dialect_ and Lyric poetry also **τεΐν**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.201, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.619, epigram cited in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.60, 61, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 930}}; also **τίν** [ῐ], {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 16, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.29, 8.68 (uncertain reading)}}; **τίν** [ῑ], {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] “I.” 6(5).4, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.20 (τίν}} before a consonant, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).93); also τεΐ 7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 53; τίνη 3rd c.BC: Rhinthon Comicus 13.}}—Acc. **σέ**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.256, +others}}; _enclitic_ σε,{{Refs 7th c.BC+|1.26, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 1.2, “Supp.” 23.9, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.42, etc.}}; in late Gr. σέν, {{Refs|“Anatolian Studies” p.76}}; _Doric dialect_ τέ {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 52, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.48, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.5, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 114}}; τ᾽ variant (codex R) in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 779 (on the accent see 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 54.14, 83.4)}}; τρέ (to be read τϝέ) {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}; or (_enclitic_) τυ {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“IG” 42(1).121.69 (Epid., 4th c.BC), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1225, “Ach.” 730 (uncertain in “Ach.” 779), 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.56, 78, etc.:—also τίν 6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 4, 3rd c.BC: Cercidas Iambographus 7.6, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 11.39, 55, 68.}}
**2** in combination with γε, **σύ γε,** **σέ γε**, etc. (compare ἔγωγε), **thou at least, for thy part**, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} and _Attic dialect_; _Doric dialect_ τύγε [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus [272], 5th c.BC: Timocreon Lyricus 1 (variant{τύ γα}); τύγᾰ 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.69, 71}}]; _Boeotian dialect_ **τούγα** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 55.6}}: _dative_ **σοί γε** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.557}}: _accusative_ **σέ γε** {{Refs|10.96}}, etc.:—also **σύ περ** {{Refs|24.398.}}
**3** σύ with _infinitive_ (as _imperative_), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.134, 4.163.}}
**II** Dual _nominative_ and _accusative_ **σφῶϊ**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.336, 4.286, +others}}, **you two, both of you**; **σφώ** (not σφῴ,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|compare 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 85.17), 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.574, 11.782, 13.47, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 344, 1543, etc.}}—Gen. and Dat. **σφῶϊν**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.341, +others}}; _contraction_ σφῷν once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.62, and always in _Attic dialect_ (Hdn.Gr. 1.475) and Trag., e.g. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 12, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 342, “OT” 1495, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 892e (codices 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus have σφῶϊν in “Tht.” 193c, +others, σφῶν “Euthd.” 273e, +others)}}. None of these forms are _enclitic_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 38.9, 85.12; codex A of 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 658c, 673e, 689a wrongly makes σφῳν}} _enclitic_; Ζεὺς σφὼ is prescribed in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.146 by Hdn.Gr. 2.93.}} —σφῶϊ is never _dative_; in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.286}} it is the _accusative_ depending on κελεύ; σφῶϊν is never _accusative_; in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.52 it is dative commodi.}}
**III** Plur. _nominative_ **ὑμεῖς**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.75, +others (before a vowel, 4.246, 7.194, +others), 6th c.BC: Pherecydes Syrius Philosophus 11, 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 29a, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.72, etc.}}, **ye, you**; _Aeolic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ ὔμμες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.274, +others, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 24, 25}}; _Doric dialect_ ὑμές {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 60, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 760, 761, 862; ὑμέν “SIG” 685.118 (Crete, 2nd c.BC), “GDI” 5155.6 ([prev. passage], 2nd c.BC)}}; _Boeotian dialect_ οὐμές {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 6}}; a resolved form ὑμέες, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Parthenius Mythographus “fragment” 14, is a poeticlicence (so 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 93.3)}} rather than genuine Ionic {{Refs 5th c.BC+|(variant in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.22).}}— Gen. **ὑμῶν**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 143, etc.}}; ὑμέων (_disyllable_) [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.159, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.7, +others, 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 74.6 (ὑμῶν codices), 6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 11.5 (variant{ὑμῶν})}}]; ὑμέων also {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.73, 6.130, +others; as _trisyllable_, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 2.27; ὑμείων 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.348, 7.195, +others}}; _Doric dialect_ ὑμέων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 46}}; also ὑμῶν, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 95.23}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ὑμμέων {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 96}}; _Boeotian dialect_ οὐμίων {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 22.}}—Dat. **ὑμῖν**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.46, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.126, etc.}}; _Ionic dialect_ _enclitic_ ὗμῐν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 97.28}} also _Doric dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 91}}; _Doric dialect_ (not _enclitic_) ὑμίν [ῐ] {{Refs|[prev. author] 92}}; ὑμίν [ῐ] also in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 864, 1242, “OT” 991, 1402, “Ant.” 308, “El.” 804, +others (but ὗμιν}} should perhaps be _restored_ where the sense needs an enclitic on the principle stated by {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 35.6, 36.5, “Synt.” 130.23)}}; ὕμιν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|(as _enclitic_) is prescribed by Hdn.Gr. (2.124) in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.33, by “Etymologicum Magnum” 432.34 in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.376, 2.141, etc.:—_Aeolic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ ὔμμῐ, ὔμμῐν, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.316, 11.336, +others, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 328, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 14, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 26.9, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 11(10).17.}}—Acc. **ὑμᾶς**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 325}}, etc. (**-υ** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Argonautica” 820, variant in 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 222}}; ὗμας or (more probably) ὕμας is required by the metre in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 9.9, 47.11)}}; _Ionic dialect_ ὑμέας (_disyllable_) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.198, +others}}; _enclitic_ ὕμεας (_disyllable_) {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 2.60 (Papyrus)}}; ὑμέας also {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.126, +others}}; _Aeolic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ ὔμμε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.412, +others, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 6(5).19; also in 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 620 (trimeter), and 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 846 (Lyric poetry)}}; _Doric dialect_ ὑμέ {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 3, 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 52, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 737, “Lys.” 1076, “SIG” 528.3 (Cretan dialogue, 3rd c.BC), 622 B 8 (Cretan, 2nd c.BC).}}—The _plural_ is sometimes used in addressing **one** person, when others are included in the speaker's thought, as {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.81, 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 89. (With σύ}} cf. Latin _tu_, Gothic _pu_; with τοι Sanskrit _genitive_ and _dative_ **te**; the origin of σφῶϊ is doubtful; with ὑμεῖς cf. Sanskrit _accusative_ _plural_ **yusmān.**)

5217:
** ὑπάγω** [ᾰ]:
_transitive_, **lead** or **bring under,** ὕπαγε ζυγὸν ὠκέας ἵππους **brought** them **under** the yoke, **yoked** them, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.148, compare 23.291}}; ἴπποις (_accusative_) δ᾽ ἄνδρες ὔπαγον ὐπ᾽ ἄρματα {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 20a. 17, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1194 in “PLit.Lond.” 73 (ἐπῆγε codices)}}; also simply, ἡμιόνους ὕπαγον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.73.}}
**2** **bring under one's power,** [οἱ θεοί] σε ὑπήγαγον ἐς χεῖρας τὰς ἐμάς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.106; ὑ. τινὰς εἰς δουλείαν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Apol.” 3}}:— _middle_, **bring under one's own power, reduce,** πόλιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.46; τοὺς Θρᾷκας 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DDeor.” 18.1, etc.}}
**3** **subsume,** ὑφ᾽ ἓν μέρος λόγου τὰ ἄρθρα καὶ τὰς ἀντωνυμίας {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 88.11, compare 235.7 (_passive_)}}; πάντα τῷ τῆς μανίας ὀνόματι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Abd.” 29.}}
**4** **bring forward in reply,** in _passive_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Conjunctionibus;” 251.9, “Synt.” 73.11.}}
**5** **subject,** τὴν ἀρχομένην [διάθεσιν] τοῖς βοηθήμασιν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.38}}:—_passive_, τῶν -ομένων τῇ διαίτῃ παθῶν {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.2.}}
**II** **bring** a person **before the judgement-seat** (the ὑπό refers to his being set **under** or **below** the judge), ὑ. τινὰ ὑπὸ δικαστήριον **bring** one **before** a court, i.e. **accuse, impeach** him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.93, compare 6.72 (_passive_)}}; ὑ. τινὰ ὑπὸ τοὺς ἐφόρους{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 82; οἱ -όμενοι εἰς ὑμᾶς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.3.28; ὑ. τινὰ ἐς δίκην 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.70; simply, ὑ. τινὰ ὡς ἐπιβουλεύοντα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.3.33; ὑ. τινὰ θανάτου on a capital charge, [prev. work] 2.3.12, 5.4.24}}; θανάτου ὑπὸ τὸν δῆμον Μιλτιάδεα **impeached** him before the commons on a capital charge, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.136: with _dative_, ὑ. τινὰς δικαστηρίοις 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Fug.” 11}}:—_middle_, τάνδ᾽ ὑπάγεται Δίκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 1155 (Lyric poetry, uncertain reading, δίκαν codices)}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.140 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus: with _dative_, τοῖς τῆς.. πεπρωμένης.. νόμοις ὑπαχθέντα “IG” 12(7).240.24 (Amorgos, 3rd c.AD)}}; ὁ πένης ὑπάγεται τῷ νόμῳ {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Declamationes” 36 at Title Page}}
**III** **lead on by degrees,** τὰς κύνας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 5.15, compare 10.4}}; **draw** or **lead on** by art or deceit, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.94; τινὰ ἐπὶ κῶμον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 507 (Lyric poetry)}}; ὑ. τοὺς πολεμίους εἰς δυσχωρίαν **draw** them **on** by pretended flight, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.37}}; ὑ. τοὺς πολεμίους ὑποφεύγοντες{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 3.2.8; τὸν ἐρῶντα τῷ ἐρωμένῳ ἀκολουθεῖν.., ὅπῃ ἂν ἐκεῖνος ὑπάγῃ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 14c; τίν᾽ ὑπάγεις μ᾽ ἐς ἐλπίδ; 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 826; ὁ θεὸς ὑπῆγεν αὐτόν, ἵνα ἀφικόμενος.. δοίη δίκην 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 6.19}}; ἡ πέρδιξ.. ἀπὸ τῶν ῳῶν ὑπάγει (i.e. ἄνθρωπον) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 613b32: with}}_infinitive_, σ᾽ ὑπήγαγον εἰς χεῖρας ἐλθεῖν **so as to** come, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 428}}:—_middle_, **lead on for one's own advantage,** but frequently much like the _active_, **lead on,** ἐλπίσιν ὑπαγαγέσθαι τινά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.91, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.4.3}}; ὑ. Θετταλοὺς εἰς δουλείαν **reduce** them, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.62}}; ὑ. τινὰς ἐς μάχην, ἐς φιλίαν, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 36.4, 42.39; ἐς φόρου συντέλειαν 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 6.2.1}}; **give** one **a lead** in speech, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 906, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.1.18}}:—_passive_, κατὰ μικρὸν ὑπαχθείς [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.1; [ἐλπίσικαὶ θενακισμοῖς] ὑπαχθέντες 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 5.10 (variant{ἐπ-}}}] (; ὑπὸ τῆς ἀπάτης καὶ τῶν ἀλαζονευμάτων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.178, etc.; εἰς ἔχθραν ὑπηγμένος ὑπότινος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.188; ἐκλοιδορίας εἰς πληγάς [prev. author] 54.19. (In this sense, ἐπάγω is frequently variant)}}
**IV** **take away from beneath, withdraw,** τινὰ ἐκ βελέων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.163; ὕπαγε τὰς ἀκροβελίδας 5th-6th c.BC: Archippus Comicus 10}}:—_passive_, ὑπαγομένου κάτωθεν τοῦ χώματος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.76.}}
**IV.2** **draw off,** τὸ στράτευμα {{LXX|[prev. author] 4.127; ὑπήγαγεν Κύριος τὴν θάλατταν LXX.Exo.14.21.}}
**IV.3** **carry off below,** ὑ. τὴν κοιλίην **purge** the bowels, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 3.17, 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “ὀξέων νούσων θεραπευτικόν” 1.10; ὑ. τὴν γαστέρα 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 279, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.353, +others}}; see below {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 111.}}
**IV.4** **bring down** a bandage, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus “de fasciis - Opera Hippocratis et Galeni” 2}}: with _dative_, **bring under,** τῷ κοίλῳ τοῦ ποδός {{Refs|[prev. work] 59.}}
**B** _intransitive_, **go away, withdraw, retire,** ὑπάγω φρένα τέρψας {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 921, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1017, “Anthologia Graeca” 9.341 (Glaucus Atheniensis Epigrammaticus)}}; of an army, **draw off** or **retire slowly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.120, 122, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.126}}; of the lion, ὑπάγει βάδην {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 629b17}}; ἂν φυτεύῃ καὶ ὑπάγῃ if he.. **goes away,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 12(7).62.54 (Amorgos, 4th c.BC)}}; ὑπάγει αὔριον he **is going (= leaving, setting out**) to-morrow, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“POxy.” 1291.11 (1st c.AD)}}; ὑπάγοντι εἰς Ἑρμοῦ πόλιν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PLond.” 1.131.155, 218, +others (1st c.AD).}}
**B.II** **go forwards, draw on,** ὕπαγ᾽ ὦ, ὕπαγ᾽ ὦ **on with you!** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 52 (Lyric poetry); ὕπαγε, τί μέλλει; 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1298; ὑπάγεθ᾽ ὑμεῖς τῆς ὁδοῦ [prev. author] “Ra.” 174; ὑ. εἰς τοὔμπροσθεν 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 79}}: also of an army, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.4.48, 4.2.16.}}
**B.II.2** later, in _present_, simply **go,** opposed to ἔρχομαι 'come', ὕπαγε Σατανᾶ {{NT|NT.Matt.4.10}}; ὕπαγε, δεῖξον.. {{NT|NT.Mark.1.44}}; ἦσαν οἱ ἐρχόμενοι καὶ οἱ ὑπάγοντες πολλοί{{NT+3rd c.AD+|[prev. work] 6.31; ποῦ ὑπάγει; NT.John.16.5; ἐν πλοίῳ ὑπάγοντι ἰς Ταπόσιριν “Sammelb.” 7357.8 (3rd c.AD); ὕπαγε ἰς πάντα τόπον [prev. work] 7452.7, 19 (3rd c.AD)}}; καθ᾽ ἡμέρα[ν] ὑπάγω παρὰ Σεραπιάδα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 38.17 (2nd-3rd c.AD)}}: the _aorist_ is ἀπῆλθον, ὕπαγε.. καὶ ἀπῆλθε {{NT|NT.Matt.9.6:—αὐτόματα ὑπάγοντα}} automata **which go** (from place to place), opposed to στατά (those which perform actions while standing still), {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “Automatopoetica” 1.2:—rare in LXX}} (and only in codex א), {{LXX|LXX.Tob.8.21, +others, LXX.Jer.43(36).19.}}
**B.III** in Medicine texts, of the bowels, **to be open,** κοιλίη ὑπάγουσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Acut.(Sp.”) 2, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.756}}; see above {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus IV. 3.}}
**B.IV** **sink down, squat,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 540a7; compare ὑπαγωγή 111.2.}}

5219:
** ὑπᾰκού-ω**,
_future_ **-ακούσομαι**{{LXX|LXX.Gen.41.40 (see. entry near the end)}} later **-ακούσω** {{Refs|“Mim.Oxy.” 413.222:}}
**I** **hearken, give ear,** θεοὶ δ᾽ ὑπὸ πάντες ἄκουον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.4; ὁ δ᾽ ἄρ᾽ ἐμμαπέως ὑπάκουσε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.485, compare “hymnus ad Venerem” 180: with _genitive_, ὄττις.. πλάσιον ἆδυ φωνείσας ὐπακούει 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 2.4; ὑμῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 318 (Lyric poetry); τῆς κρίσεως 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.56 (assuming variant).}}
**I.2** **answer** (by voice or act) when called, ἢ ἐξελθέμεναι ἢ ἔνδοθεν αἶψ᾽ ὑπακοῦσαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.283, compare 10.83, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 400 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 273 (Lyric poetry), 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 13.59: in Prose, ὁ κῆρυξ ἐκήρυττε τίς τὴν ἱκετηρίαν καταθείη, καὶ οὐδεὶς ὑπήκουεν 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.112; τῷ παιδίῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 878, compare “Nu.” 360 (_anapaest meter_), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 3.4, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.49, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.266.}}
**I.2.b** in a dialogue, **answer** when questioned, σοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 217d; τοῖς λόγοις [prev. author] “Lg.” 898c.}}
**I.3** **listen to, heed, regard,** with _genitive_ _of things_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Tht.” 162d, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.1.20; ὑ. νόμων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 708d}}; ὑ. διαίτῃ **submit** to a regimen, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “R.” 459c; λόγῳ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1333a18}}; ὑ. τῷ ξυμφόρῳ τινός **comply** with his interest, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.98}}; δείπνῳ ὑ. **accept an invitation** to dinner, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 6.247d}}: _absolutely_, **give way, submit, comply,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.148, 4.119, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 325a, “PCair.Zen.” 367.15 (3rd c.BC): with a _neuter_ _pronoun_, μάλα γε τοῦτο ὑπήκουσεν}} in this matter he **obeyed,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.2.3; οὐδὲν τούτων ὑπήκουον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.29, compare 139, 140, etc.}}; ὑ. τινός τι or τινί τι, **obey** one **in** a thing,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 26, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 774b.}}
**II** Special senses:
**II.1** of porters, **answer** a knock at the door, ὑ. τινί {{NT+4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Cri.” 43a: _absolutely_, [prev. author] “Phd.” 59e, NT.Act.12.13; [παρὰ] τὴν θύραν 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 4.9, 28.3}}; ὁ ὑπακούσας the porter, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 1.11, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 47.35.}}
**II.2** of a judge, **listen** to a complainant, τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.1.18}}; also of the parties in legal proceedings, **appear before the court,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 4.28, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.257,290; ὑ. εἰς τὸ δικαστήριον 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “pro Euxenippo” 2, compare “PSI” 10.1100.10 (2nd c.AD), “Sammelb.” 7369.10 (2nd-3rd c.AD).}}
**II.3** of dependants, subjects, etc., **obey, submit to,** Δαρείου οὐδαμὰ ὑ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.101; Ἀθηναίων 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.56, compare 6.82; τοῖς πέλας [prev. author] 2.61.}}
**II.3.b** Astrology texts, **to aspect from South to North,** of the southernmost of two zodiacal signs equidistant from an equinoctial point, opposed to προστάσσειν, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 35}}; ={ἀκούω} see, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Paulus Alexandrinus Astrologus “E.” 2.}}
**II.4** **answer one's expectations, succeed,** ὑπήκουέ μοι τὸ πρᾶγμα {{Refs 1st c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Icar.” 10; τῆς μεταλλείας ἀσθενῶς ὑπακουούσης 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 9.1.23.}}
**II.5** ὑ. αὐγαῖς ἁλίου **to be subject** to the sun's rays, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 3.24; ταῖς ὥραις 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 1.15.1; τοῦ ψύχους [prev. work] 5.4.2}}; ὑπακούουσι τῶν τῆς ἀρχῆς παθημάτων οἱ κατὰ τοὺς ὀφθαλμοὺς μύες **feel the effects of**.., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(2).68.}}
**II.6** of ailments, **yield, give way** to a remedy, τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 3.8, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.354: _absolutely_, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προρρητικόν” 2.39, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.122, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.439: _metaphorically_, τὸ μυθῶδες ὑ. λόγῳ 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Theseus” 1; τοῖς ἰσχυρῶς φερομένοις ὑ. ὁ ἀήρ 4th c.BC: Archytas Tarentinus Philosophus 1}}; πληγαῖς ὑ., of metal, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.802b; ὑποχόνδριον ὑπακοῦον}}**yielding to pressure,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 4.45; μὴ εὐθέως ὑπακούοντος εἰς ἐξολκὴν τοῦ ἐμβρύου 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.62, compare 86.}}
**II.7** **concede a point in dispute,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 161b15.}}
**II.8** **correspond,** πᾶσα παραγωγὴ ἐπιρρηματικὴ.. μιᾷ ὑπακούει πτώσει κατὰ τὴν διάλυσιν every adverbial derivative **corresponds** to a case, e.g. οἴκοθι to ἐν οἴκῳ, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Adverbiis;” 206.21}}; **conform** to a theory, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Synt.” 236.14.}}
**III** κοινὸν ὑ. **understand under** the term κοινόν.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 31c, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.23c:}} —_passive_, κοινῶς ὑ. **to be understood** in a general sense, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ ποιημάτων” 5.35.}}
**III.2** in Grammars, **understand** a word omitted, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 22.21 (_passive_): τὸ -όμενον}} what one **has in mind,** the **subject,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pron.” 68.15, +others}}
**III.3** **understand,** with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Mus.” p.72 K., “Po.” 5.9.}}
**IV** _future_ ὑπακούσεται in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.69}}, if correct, must be _passive_, if their **service shall be** lighter; but Scholia gives ὑπακούσονται, whence ξυγκαταστρεψάμενοι (for -οις) is conjectured.

5220:
** ὕπανδρος**, ον,
(ἀνήρ) **under a man, subject to him, married,** γυνή {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|LXX.Num.5.20, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.26.3, NT.Rom.7.2, etc.; τὰς ὑ. τῶν γυναικῶν 2nd c.BC: Polemo Historicus 59; ὕ. γύναια 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pelopidas” 9.}}
**II** **feminine,** ἀγωγὴ οἰκουρὸς καὶ ὕ. a **feminine** mode of life, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 32.10.}}

5221:
** ὑπαντ-άω**,
_Ionic dialect_ **ὑπαντ-έω** {{Refs|“APl.” 4.101}}: _future_ **-ήσομαι**{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 1.20.1, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 149.15, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 10.61}}: _aorist_ **-ήντησα**{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aratus” 34}}, _Doric dialect_ **-άντᾱσα**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 8.59}}:—**come** or **go to meet,** either as a friend, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.2}}; or in arms,{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1.4.22, 4.2.17; εἰς τὰς ὁδοὺς ὑ. 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “pro Euxenippo” 22, compare “SIG” 798.21 (Cyzicus, 1st c.AD)}}; ὑ. τινί {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus [same place], 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.3.15, NT.Matt.8.28, etc.; ὑ. τῇ πόλει πρὸς τὴν χρείαν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus}} [same place]; πρὸς τὸ [βῆμα] {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1630.15 (3rd c.AD)}}: also with _genitive_, ἀνδρῶν ἀγαθῶν παιδὸς ὑ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 719 (_anapaest meter_, assuming variant): —in 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 5.45, the _accusative_ ὄντα (sic codices, ὄντι Schweigh., Mendelss.) refers to σε κατιόντα ὁρῶν}} just before:—later in _middle_, ὑπαντώμενος αὐτοῖς {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 2.5.5, compare 3.11.3, 5.4.5, etc.}}
**2** **meet, encounter,** of a heavenly body, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 132.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **meet,** i. e. **agree to,** ταῖς τιμαῖς {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC: Posidonius Historicus 36 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}; **present oneself at,** τῇ ἀποδόσει {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“Sammelb.” 6.23 (3rd c.AD)}}; πρὸς τὴν ἀπόδοσιν {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“BGU” 614.23 (3rd c.AD).}}
**II.2** **meet,** i.e. **reply** or **object to,** τοῖς ἐμοῖς βουλεύμασι [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 398 (assuming variant, see at {ὕπαντα})}}]; πρός τινα or τι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 10.105, etc.}}; πρός τι ὑ. ὡς.. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 265.4: _absolutely_, εὐαρεστήσεως ὑπαντησομένης}} **come in response, ensue,** Herod._middle_ in {{Refs|“Rh.Mus.” 58.85, compare 100.}}
**II.3** **occur** to one, τῷ ῥήτορι {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 16.4.}}
**II.4** **fall in with,** ἀνωμαλίᾳ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 1.6}}; **correspond with,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Conjunctionibus;” 232.23.}}

5222:
** ὑπάντ-ησις**, εως, ἡ,
**coming to meet,** ἐξῆλθον εἰς ὑπάντησιν αὐτῷ {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.John.12.13, compare 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 11.8.4, 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 4.6 (_plural_); ψήφισμα -ήσεως “SIG” 798.16 (Cyzicus, 1st c.AD).}}
**2** Astrology texts, **encounter** of a heavenly body, in ἄφεσις, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 132 (_plural_).}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **retort, answer,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 11.202}}; πρός τι{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 7.278, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 305.19.}}
**II.2** **counter-treatment,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.41.}}

5223:
** ὕπαρξις**, εως, ἡ,
**existence, reality,** τοῦ θεοῦ {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Pietate - Herkulanische Studien” 114, compare “D.” 3.10}}; opposed to ἀνυπαρξία, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 1.21, compare 3.24}}; opposed to νόησις, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1067c, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.115}}; opposed to ἀναίρεσις, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Conjunctionibus;” 221.17; εἴ τις.. ἐν οὐχ ὑπάρξει τὸν χρόνον λέγοι 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 3.7.13.}}
**2** in Logic, **existence in a subject,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Ammonius Philosophus “in Aristotelis Categorias commentarius - pars” 6.16, +others}}
**3** Grammars, τὰ τῆς ὑπάρξεως ῥήματα, ={ὑπαρκτικὰ ῥ}., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 25.2, compare “Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 2.46.}}
**4** in Mathematics texts, **positive term,** λεῖψις ἐπὶ λεῖψιν πολλαπλασιασθεῖσα ποιεῖ ὕπαρξιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Diophantus Mathematicus 1 “Def.” 9; compare ὑπάρχω 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus IV. 3.}}
**II** **substance,** ἡ τοῦ κέρατος ὕ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 1.129.}}
**II.2** like{τὰ ὑπάρχοντα}, **substance, property,** {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|LXX.Pro.18.11, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.17.11, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ οἰκονομίας” p.69 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus (_plural_), 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 7.8, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 20.71, NT.Heb.10.34, “POxy.” 1274.14 (3rd c.AD); χρημάτων ὕ. Teles p.43 H.}}

5224:
Included with: ** ὑπάρχω**,
_future_
**A** **-ξω**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.109, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 932 (_anapaest meter_):}}_aorist_ ὑπῆρξα (see. below {{Refs|1)}}:—_passive_, _future_ ὑπαρχθήσομαι {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PTeb.” 418.7 (3rd c.AD):}}_perfect_ ὕπηργμαι, _Ionic dialect_ **-αργμαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.11}}:—**begin, take the initiative**:—Constr.:
**A.1** _absolutely_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.286, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1223; ὑπάρχων ἠδίκεις αὐτούς 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 16.44}}; ὁ ὑπάρξας the **beginner** (in a quarrel), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.15, compare 1; ἀμύνεσθαι τοὺς ὑπάρξαντας 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 24.18; ἀμυνομένους, μὴ ὑπάρχοντας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 456e; ὡς οὐχ ὑπάρχων ἀλλὰ τιμωρούμενος 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 358}}:—_middle_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 41c, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 12.41, etc.}}
**A.2** with _genitive_, **take the initiative in, begin,** ἀδίκων ἔργων, ἀδικίης, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.5, 4.1, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.74, etc.; ὑ. τῆς ἐλευθερίας τῇ Ἑλλάδι 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.142, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 237b.}}
**A.3** with _participle_, **take the initiative in** doing, ἐμὲ ὑπῆρξαν ἄδικα ποιεῦντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.8.β, compare 6.133, 9.78}}; ὑπάρχει εὖ (or κακῶς) ποιῶν τινα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.3.23, 5.5.9}}; τοῖς αὐτοῖς ἀμύνεσθαι οἷσπερ καὶ οἱ Λκεδαιμόνιοι ὑπῆρξαν retaliating by the means which the L. **had used first,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.67 (where οἷσπερ is explained by the following ἀποκτείναντες and ἐσβαλόντες).}}
**A.3.b** in _middle_ with _infinitive_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 14.11: with _genitive_, βαδίσεως -ονται [prev. work] 4.34; ἡλίου -ομένου τῆς ἀκμῆς [prev. work] 1.20.}}
**A.4** with _accusative_, ὑ. εὐεργεσίας εἴς τινα or τινι **take the initiative in** [doing] kindnesses to one, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.280, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.26}}; ὑ. τοῦτο (i.e. τὸ εὐνοεῖν) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 927}}:—_passive_, ὑπηρεσίαι ὑπηργμέναι εἰς Φίλιππον αὑτῷ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.109; τὰ παρὰ τῶν θεῶν ὑπηργμένα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.10}}; τὰ ἔκ τινος ὑπαργμένα (_Ionic dialect_ for ὑπηργ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.11; ὑπηργμένων πολλῶν κἀγαθῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1159; οὐδέν μοι ὑπῆρκτο εἰς αὐτόν 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.58; ἀνάξια τῶν εἰς ὑμᾶς ὑπηργμένων 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 21.25}}; ἄξιον τῶν ὑ. equivalent **to what was done for him,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1163b21: _impersonal_, ὑπῆρκτο αὐτοῦ (i.e. τοῦ Πειραιέως)}} **a beginning** of it **had been made,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.93.}}
**B** in _active_ only, **to be the begining,** παιδοβόροι μὲν πρῶτον ὑπῆρξαν μόχθοι τάλανες {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 1068 (_anapaest meter_)}}; πολλῶν κακῶν, μεγάλων ἀχέων, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1582 (variant), “Andr.” 274 (Lyric poetry), compare “HF” 1169.}}
**B.2** **to be already in existence,** πημονῆς δ᾽ ἅλις γ᾽ ὑπάρχει {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1656 (troch.); φοίνισσα δὲ Θρηϊκίων ἀγέλα ταύρων ὑπᾶρχεν}}**was already there,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.205}}; αὗται αἱ νέες τοῖσι Ἀθηναίοισι ὑπῆρχον **already existed,** opposed to to those they were about to build, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.144}}; εἰ τοίνυν σφι χώρη γε μηδεμία ὑπῆρχε if they **had** no country **originally existing,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.15}}; χωρὶς δὲ τούτων οἱ χίλιοι ὑπῆρχον the **original** thousand **existed,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.5.5; ἔδει πρῶτον μὲν ὑπάρχειν πάντων ἰσηγορίαν 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 291 (Lyric poetry); ὑπαρχούσης μὲν τιμῆς, παρούσης δὲ δυνάμεως 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 8.1; τοῦτο δεῖ προσεῖναι, τὰ δ᾽ ἄλλ᾽ ὑπάρχει 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.15, compare 8.53; ταὐτὰ ὑ. αὐτῷ ἅπερ ἐμοί 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.60, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.23}}; ὑμῖν.. ἐλευθερίαν τε ὑπάρχειν καὶ Λακεδαιμονίων ξυμμάχοις κεκλῆσθαι **there is in store** for you.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.9: with _genitive_, οἶκος δ᾽ ὑ. τῶνδε.. ἔχειν}} **there is** store of these things for us to have, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 961 (assuming variant, οἴκοις Pors.): frequently in}}_participle_, ἡ ὑπάρχουσα οὐσία the **existing** property, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.28; τὰ ὑ. ἁμαρτήματα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.92}}; τῆς ὑ. τιμῆς for the **current** price, Syngr. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 35.12}}; οἱ ὑ. πολῖται the **existing** citizens, {{Refs|[prev. author] 18.295}}; τῆς φύσεως ὑ. nature **being what it is,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.4.4}}; also κρησφύγετόν τι ὑπάρχον εἶναι that there should be a refuge **ready prepared,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.124.}}
**B.3** **exist really,** opposed to φαίνομαι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 297b22, “Metaphorical” 1046b10; ἀθεώρητοι τῶν ὑπαρχόντων [prev. author] “GC” 316a9; καταληπτικὴ φαντασία ἡ ἀπὸ τοῦ ὑπάρχοντος “Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 2.25.}}
**B.4** simply, **be,** τοῖσιν ἄγουσιν κλαύμαθ᾽ ὑπάρξει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 932 (_anapaest meter_); ὅθεν εὐμάρει᾽ ὑπάρχοι πόρου [prev. author] “Ph.” 704 (Lyric poetry)}}: and with a predicate, θησαυρὸς ἄν σοι παῖς ὑπῆρχ᾽ οὑμός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1229; τὸ χωρίον καρτερὸν ὑ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.4}}; φύσεως ἀγαθῆς ὑπάρξαι **to be** of a good natural disposition, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 21.11; κἂν σοφὸς ὑπάρχῃ 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 102}}; μέγα ὑ. τοῖς τοιούτοις λόγοις ις of great advantage to them, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.19}}; πολλῶν ὑπάρξει κῦρος ἡμέρα καλῶν, ={κυρώσει πολλὰ καλά}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 919.}}
**B.4.b** τὰ ὑπάρχοντα, much like{τὰ ὑπηργμένα} {{Refs 4th c.BC+|(4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 4 _passive_)}}, a man's **record,** ἀνάξιον τῶν ὑ. τῇ πόλει καὶ πεπραγμένων τοῖς προγόνοις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.49}}; τὰ κάλλιστα τῶν ὑ. your **past record,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 18.95}}; ἡ ὑπάρχουσα αἰσχύνη the disgrace **which has been incurred,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 19.217; τὰ ὑπάρχοντα [αὑτῷ] ἐγκλήματα 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.179.}}
**B.5** sometimes with a _participle_, much like{τυγχάνω, τοιαῦτα [αὐτῷ] ὑπῆρχε ἐὀντα} {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.192; ἐχθρὸς ὑ. ὤν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.38; ὑ. δύναμιν κεκτημένοι [prev. author] 3.7, compare 15.1.}}
**B.6** προγόνων ὑ. τῶν ἐξ Ἰλίου **to be the descendant of**.., {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 2.65.}}
**B.II** like{ὑπόκειμαι} {{Refs|II.2}}**to be laid down, to be taken for granted,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 198d}}; τούτου ὑπάρχοντος, τούτων ὑπαρχόντων, **this being granted,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ti.” 30c, 29b; θέντες ὡς ὑπάρχον [prev. author] “R.” 458a.}}
**B.III** **belong to, fall to** one, **accrue,** ὑπάρξει τοι.. τὰ ἐναντία you **will have,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.109, etc.; τὸ μισεῖσθαι πᾶσιν ὑ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.64}}; τὴν ὑπάρχουσαν ἀπ᾽ ἀλλήλων ἀμφοτέροις [σωτηρίαν] {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.86}}; ἡ ὑπάρχουσα φύσις your **proper** nature, its **normal** condition, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.45}}; τῇ τέχνῃ ὑπάρχειν διδούς assigning **as a property** of art, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 58c, compare “Tht.” 150b, 150c.}}
**B.III.2** of persons, ὑ. τινί **to be devoted to** one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.1.4, “HG” 7.5.5, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.54, etc.}}; καθ᾽ ὑμῶν ὑπάρξων ἐκείνῳ **he will be on** his **side** against you, {{Refs|[prev. work]118, compare 2.14.}}
**B.III.2.b** ἐν παντὶ.. πᾶς χωρίῳ, καὶ ᾧ μὴ ὑπάρχομεν every one in every place, even **outside our sphere of influence** (literal **to which we do** not **belong**), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.87.}}
**B.III.3** in the Logic of {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus ὑπάρχειν}} denotes **the subsistence** of qualities _in a subject, _{{Refs|“Metaphorical” 1025a14}}; ὑ. τινί, ={κατηγορεῖσθαί τινος}, {{Refs|“APr.” 25a13, +others}}; ὑ. κατά τινος{{Refs|[prev. work] 24a27, “Int.” 16b13}}; ἐπί τινος{{Refs|[prev. work] 16a32; ὑ. τινὶ ζῴῳ πεζῷ δίποδι εἶναι “Top.” 109a14}}; ὑπάρξει τι [τῷ πρώτῳ] it **will have predicates,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 5.6.2; ἡ γένεσις τῷ χρόνῳ.. ὑπάρχει 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 142.}}
**B.IV** frequently in _neuter_ _plural_ _participle_, τὰ ὑπάρχοντα,
**B.IV.1** in _signification_ {{Refs|1}}**existing circumstances, presentadvantages,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 191, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.2; ἀπὸ τῶν αἰεὶ ὑ. σφαλέντες 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.18, compare 6.33}}; πρὸς τὰ ὑ.{{Refs|[prev. work] 31; ἐκ τῶν ὑ.}}**under the circumstances, according to one's means,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.4.9, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1288b33; ὡς ἐκ τῶν ὑ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.76, 8.1.}}
**B.IV.2** in _signification_ {{Refs|111}}**possessions, resources,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.70, 144, etc.}}; τὰ ἑκατέροις ὑ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 141; κινδυνεύειν περὶ τῶν ὑ. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.57}}: as a _substantive_, τὰ ὑ. αὐτοῦ {{LXX+NT|NT.Matt.24.47, cf. LXX.Gen.12.5}}; ὑποθέμενος τὰ ὑ. καὶ ὑπάρξοντα **present** and **future resources,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|POxy. 125.22 (6th c.AD), etc.}}
**B.IV.3** in Mathematics texts, ὑπάρχοντα εἴδη **positive** terms, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Diophantus Mathematicus 1 “Def.” 10.}}
**B.V** _impersonal_, ὑπάρχει **the fact is that**.., with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, ὑ. γάρ σε μὴ γνῶναί τινα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1340}}; ὡς ὑ. τοῦ ἔχειν.. as **the case stands** with regard to having, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 516b25; περὶ τοὺς μαστοὺς ὑπεναντίως ὑ. [prev. work] 500a14.}}
**B.V.2** **it is allowed, it is possible,** with _dative_ et _infinitive_, ὑ. ἡμῖν ἐπικρατεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.63, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.19, etc.; ὑ. αὐτῇ εὐδαίμονι εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 81a, compare “Prt.” 345a, “Phdr.” 240b, etc.}}: also without a _dative_, οὐχ ὑ. εἰδέναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.82; ὑ. τὴν αὐτὴν εἶναι μητέρα 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 7.25, etc.: _absolutely_, ὥσπερ ὑπῆρχε}} as well as **was possible,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.109.}}
**B.V.3** in _neuter_ _participle_, ὑπάρχον ὑμῖν πολεμεῖν **since it is allowed** you to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.124, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 217a.}}
**C** **to be** ὕπαρχος or **subordinate colleague,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 36.36; τῷ.. Ἀντωνίνῳ [prev. author] 71.34.}}
**C.II** uncertain in the sense of ἄρχω, **rule;** for {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.87}}, where the {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia is in error, see above 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 111.2b; in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1291b32 ὑπερέχειν is probable reading}}

