**A.3** with _participle_, **take the initiative in** doing, ἐμὲ ὑπῆρξαν ἄδικα ποιεῦντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.8.β, compare 6.133, 9.78}}; ὑπάρχει εὖ (or κακῶς) ποιῶν τινα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.3.23, 5.5.9}}; τοῖς αὐτοῖς ἀμύνεσθαι οἷσπερ καὶ οἱ Λκεδαιμόνιοι ὑπῆρξαν retaliating by the means which the L. **had used first,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.67 (where οἷσπερ is explained by the following ἀποκτείναντες and ἐσβαλόντες).}}
**A.3.b** in _middle_ with _infinitive_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 14.11: with _genitive_, βαδίσεως -ονται [prev. work] 4.34; ἡλίου -ομένου τῆς ἀκμῆς [prev. work] 1.20.}}
**A.4** with _accusative_, ὑ. εὐεργεσίας εἴς τινα or τινι **take the initiative in** [doing] kindnesses to one, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.280, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.26}}; ὑ. τοῦτο (i.e. τὸ εὐνοεῖν) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 927}}:—_passive_, ὑπηρεσίαι ὑπηργμέναι εἰς Φίλιππον αὑτῷ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.109; τὰ παρὰ τῶν θεῶν ὑπηργμένα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.10}}; τὰ ἔκ τινος ὑπαργμένα (_Ionic dialect_ for ὑπηργ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.11; ὑπηργμένων πολλῶν κἀγαθῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1159; οὐδέν μοι ὑπῆρκτο εἰς αὐτόν 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.58; ἀνάξια τῶν εἰς ὑμᾶς ὑπηργμένων 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 21.25}}; ἄξιον τῶν ὑ. equivalent **to what was done for him,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1163b21: _impersonal_, ὑπῆρκτο αὐτοῦ (i.e. τοῦ Πειραιέως)}} **a beginning** of it **had been made,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.93.}}
**B** in _active_ only, **to be the begining,** παιδοβόροι μὲν πρῶτον ὑπῆρξαν μόχθοι τάλανες {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 1068 (_anapaest meter_)}}; πολλῶν κακῶν, μεγάλων ἀχέων, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1582 (variant), “Andr.” 274 (Lyric poetry), compare “HF” 1169.}}
**B.2** **to be already in existence,** πημονῆς δ᾽ ἅλις γ᾽ ὑπάρχει {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1656 (troch.); φοίνισσα δὲ Θρηϊκίων ἀγέλα ταύρων ὑπᾶρχεν}}**was already there,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.205}}; αὗται αἱ νέες τοῖσι Ἀθηναίοισι ὑπῆρχον **already existed,** opposed to to those they were about to build, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.144}}; εἰ τοίνυν σφι χώρη γε μηδεμία ὑπῆρχε if they **had** no country **originally existing,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.15}}; χωρὶς δὲ τούτων οἱ χίλιοι ὑπῆρχον the **original** thousand **existed,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.5.5; ἔδει πρῶτον μὲν ὑπάρχειν πάντων ἰσηγορίαν 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 291 (Lyric poetry); ὑπαρχούσης μὲν τιμῆς, παρούσης δὲ δυνάμεως 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 8.1; τοῦτο δεῖ προσεῖναι, τὰ δ᾽ ἄλλ᾽ ὑπάρχει 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.15, compare 8.53; ταὐτὰ ὑ. αὐτῷ ἅπερ ἐμοί 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.60, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.23}}; ὑμῖν.. ἐλευθερίαν τε ὑπάρχειν καὶ Λακεδαιμονίων ξυμμάχοις κεκλῆσθαι **there is in store** for you.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.9: with _genitive_, οἶκος δ᾽ ὑ. τῶνδε.. ἔχειν}} **there is** store of these things for us to have, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 961 (assuming variant, οἴκοις Pors.): frequently in}}_participle_, ἡ ὑπάρχουσα οὐσία the **existing** property, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.28; τὰ ὑ. ἁμαρτήματα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.92}}; τῆς ὑ. τιμῆς for the **current** price, Syngr. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 35.12}}; οἱ ὑ. πολῖται the **existing** citizens, {{Refs|[prev. author] 18.295}}; τῆς φύσεως ὑ. nature **being what it is,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.4.4}}; also κρησφύγετόν τι ὑπάρχον εἶναι that there should be a refuge **ready prepared,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.124.}}
**B.3** **exist really,** opposed to φαίνομαι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 297b22, “Metaphorical” 1046b10; ἀθεώρητοι τῶν ὑπαρχόντων [prev. author] “GC” 316a9; καταληπτικὴ φαντασία ἡ ἀπὸ τοῦ ὑπάρχοντος “Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 2.25.}}
**B.4** simply, **be,** τοῖσιν ἄγουσιν κλαύμαθ᾽ ὑπάρξει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 932 (_anapaest meter_); ὅθεν εὐμάρει᾽ ὑπάρχοι πόρου [prev. author] “Ph.” 704 (Lyric poetry)}}: and with a predicate, θησαυρὸς ἄν σοι παῖς ὑπῆρχ᾽ οὑμός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1229; τὸ χωρίον καρτερὸν ὑ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.4}}; φύσεως ἀγαθῆς ὑπάρξαι **to be** of a good natural disposition, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 21.11; κἂν σοφὸς ὑπάρχῃ 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 102}}; μέγα ὑ. τοῖς τοιούτοις λόγοις ις of great advantage to them, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.19}}; πολλῶν ὑπάρξει κῦρος ἡμέρα καλῶν, ={κυρώσει πολλὰ καλά}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 919.}}
**B.4.b** τὰ ὑπάρχοντα, much like{τὰ ὑπηργμένα} {{Refs 4th c.BC+|(4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 4 _passive_)}}, a man's **record,** ἀνάξιον τῶν ὑ. τῇ πόλει καὶ πεπραγμένων τοῖς προγόνοις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.49}}; τὰ κάλλιστα τῶν ὑ. your **past record,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 18.95}}; ἡ ὑπάρχουσα αἰσχύνη the disgrace **which has been incurred,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 19.217; τὰ ὑπάρχοντα [αὑτῷ] ἐγκλήματα 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.179.}}
**B.5** sometimes with a _participle_, much like{τυγχάνω, τοιαῦτα [αὐτῷ] ὑπῆρχε ἐὀντα} {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.192; ἐχθρὸς ὑ. ὤν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.38; ὑ. δύναμιν κεκτημένοι [prev. author] 3.7, compare 15.1.}}
**B.6** προγόνων ὑ. τῶν ἐξ Ἰλίου **to be the descendant of**.., {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 2.65.}}
**B.II** like{ὑπόκειμαι} {{Refs|II.2}}**to be laid down, to be taken for granted,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 198d}}; τούτου ὑπάρχοντος, τούτων ὑπαρχόντων, **this being granted,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ti.” 30c, 29b; θέντες ὡς ὑπάρχον [prev. author] “R.” 458a.}}
**B.III** **belong to, fall to** one, **accrue,** ὑπάρξει τοι.. τὰ ἐναντία you **will have,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.109, etc.; τὸ μισεῖσθαι πᾶσιν ὑ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.64}}; τὴν ὑπάρχουσαν ἀπ᾽ ἀλλήλων ἀμφοτέροις [σωτηρίαν] {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.86}}; ἡ ὑπάρχουσα φύσις your **proper** nature, its **normal** condition, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.45}}; τῇ τέχνῃ ὑπάρχειν διδούς assigning **as a property** of art, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 58c, compare “Tht.” 150b, 150c.}}
**B.III.2** of persons, ὑ. τινί **to be devoted to** one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.1.4, “HG” 7.5.5, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.54, etc.}}; καθ᾽ ὑμῶν ὑπάρξων ἐκείνῳ **he will be on** his **side** against you, {{Refs|[prev. work]118, compare 2.14.}}
**B.III.2.b** ἐν παντὶ.. πᾶς χωρίῳ, καὶ ᾧ μὴ ὑπάρχομεν every one in every place, even **outside our sphere of influence** (literal **to which we do** not **belong**), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.87.}}
**B.III.3** in the Logic of {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus ὑπάρχειν}} denotes **the subsistence** of qualities _in a subject, _{{Refs|“Metaphorical” 1025a14}}; ὑ. τινί, ={κατηγορεῖσθαί τινος}, {{Refs|“APr.” 25a13, +others}}; ὑ. κατά τινος{{Refs|[prev. work] 24a27, “Int.” 16b13}}; ἐπί τινος{{Refs|[prev. work] 16a32; ὑ. τινὶ ζῴῳ πεζῷ δίποδι εἶναι “Top.” 109a14}}; ὑπάρξει τι [τῷ πρώτῳ] it **will have predicates,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 5.6.2; ἡ γένεσις τῷ χρόνῳ.. ὑπάρχει 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 142.}}
**B.IV** frequently in _neuter_ _plural_ _participle_, τὰ ὑπάρχοντα,
**B.IV.1** in _signification_ {{Refs|1}}**existing circumstances, presentadvantages,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 191, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.2; ἀπὸ τῶν αἰεὶ ὑ. σφαλέντες 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.18, compare 6.33}}; πρὸς τὰ ὑ.{{Refs|[prev. work] 31; ἐκ τῶν ὑ.}}**under the circumstances, according to one's means,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.4.9, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1288b33; ὡς ἐκ τῶν ὑ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.76, 8.1.}}
**B.IV.2** in _signification_ {{Refs|111}}**possessions, resources,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.70, 144, etc.}}; τὰ ἑκατέροις ὑ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 141; κινδυνεύειν περὶ τῶν ὑ. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.57}}: as a _substantive_, τὰ ὑ. αὐτοῦ {{LXX+NT|NT.Matt.24.47, cf. LXX.Gen.12.5}}; ὑποθέμενος τὰ ὑ. καὶ ὑπάρξοντα **present** and **future resources,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|POxy. 125.22 (6th c.AD), etc.}}
**B.IV.3** in Mathematics texts, ὑπάρχοντα εἴδη **positive** terms, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Diophantus Mathematicus 1 “Def.” 10.}}
**B.V** _impersonal_, ὑπάρχει **the fact is that**.., with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, ὑ. γάρ σε μὴ γνῶναί τινα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1340}}; ὡς ὑ. τοῦ ἔχειν.. as **the case stands** with regard to having, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 516b25; περὶ τοὺς μαστοὺς ὑπεναντίως ὑ. [prev. work] 500a14.}}
**B.V.2** **it is allowed, it is possible,** with _dative_ et _infinitive_, ὑ. ἡμῖν ἐπικρατεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.63, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.19, etc.; ὑ. αὐτῇ εὐδαίμονι εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 81a, compare “Prt.” 345a, “Phdr.” 240b, etc.}}: also without a _dative_, οὐχ ὑ. εἰδέναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.82; ὑ. τὴν αὐτὴν εἶναι μητέρα 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 7.25, etc.: _absolutely_, ὥσπερ ὑπῆρχε}} as well as **was possible,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.109.}}
**B.V.3** in _neuter_ _participle_, ὑπάρχον ὑμῖν πολεμεῖν **since it is allowed** you to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.124, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 217a.}}
**C** **to be** ὕπαρχος or **subordinate colleague,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 36.36; τῷ.. Ἀντωνίνῳ [prev. author] 71.34.}}
**C.II** uncertain in the sense of ἄρχω, **rule;** for {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.87}}, where the {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia is in error, see above 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 111.2b; in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1291b32 ὑπερέχειν is probable reading}}

5226:
** ὑπείκ-ω**,
_Epic dialect_ **ὑποείκω**, with _imperfect_ ὑπόεικον, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.305}}: _future_ ὑπείξω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1362, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 625, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 15.24; ὑπείξομαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.294, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.117}}; _Epic dialect_ also ὑποείξομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.602}}: _aorist 1_ ὑπεῖξα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.7.31}}; _Epic dialect_ ὑπόειξα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.227: compare ὑπεικαθεῖν}}:—**retire, withdraw, depart,** with _genitive_ loci, νεῶν **from** the ships, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.305}}; ὑ. τινὶ ἕδρης **retire from** one's seat **for** another, make room for him, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.42}}; ὑ. τοῦ ἀρχαίου λόγου **draw back** from.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.160}}; ὑ. τινὶ λόγων (conjecture Valck. for λόγῳ) give one the first word, allow him to speak first, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.3.16; πολίταις ὁδῶν καὶ θάκων καὶ λόγων ὑπείκειν [prev. author] “Cyr.” 8.7.10.}}
**2** **yield, give way,** ὑ. τινί {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 7.7.31}}: _absolutely_, of a seaman, ὅστις.. πόδα τείνας ὑπείκει μηδέν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 716}}; of things, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.266; ὅσα δένδρων ὑ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 713; ὑ. ὑγρὰ οὖσα ἡ κνήμη 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 7.6; ὑ. αἱ δάπιδες}}**are soft and yielding,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 8.8.16}}; ἐν ὑπείκοντι in **a yielding substance,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 694b15; πρὸς ἀντιπῖπτον.. καὶ οὐ πρὸς ὑπεῖκον [prev. author] “Pr.” 961b4; καθ᾽ ἅπερ ἂν ὑπείκῃ 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 2.711}}; τὸ ὑπεῖκον, ={οἱ ὑπείκοντες}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 327.}}
**3** with _accusative_, πάροιθε νεμεσσηθεὶς ὑπόειξε χεῖρας ἐμάς **he scaped** my hands, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.227.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **yield, give way, comply,**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 211; θεοῖσιν ὑπείξεαι ἀθανάτοισι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.117, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.602; ἀλλ᾽ ἤτοι μὲν ταῦθ᾽ ὑποείξομεν ἀλλήλοισι 4.62}}; σοὶ πᾶν ἔργον ὑπείξομαι **I will give way** to thee in.., {{Refs|1.294: in Trag., _Attic dialect_, etc., sometimes _absolutely_}}**yield, submit,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 371, “OT” 625, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.127, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 32a, NT.Heb.13.17, etc.}}: sometimes with _dative_, **submit to,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1362; τιμαῖς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 670; γήρᾳ ὑ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 140 (_anapaest meter_); ὑ. θυμουμένοις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 717d, compare “R.” 336e, etc.; ἐπιθυμίαις 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 1.25: with}}_infinitive_, νῷν ὕπεικε τὸν κασίγνητον μολεῖν **concede** to us that.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1184}}; ὑ. δαμῆναι **submit** to be conquered, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.1676}}; but ὑ. μὴ πολεμίζειν **yield,** so as not to.., probable reading {{Refs|[prev. work] 408 (ὑπείξομαι, -ωμαι codices).}}

5227:
** ὑπεναντί-ος**, α, ον
{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1327a17, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Agesilaus” 24}}; but ος, ον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus2.139b}}:— **set over against, meeting,** ἵπποι ὑ. ἀλλήλοισιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 347.}}
**2** **set against, opposite,** of enemies in battle, τοὺς σφίσιν ὑ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.2}}; οἱ ὑ. the **enemy,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.38, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.148, etc.}}
**3** **opposed, opposite, contrary,** γένη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 306e}}; of **contrary** properties, τὸ ὑ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 13}}; ὑπεναντιωτάτοις.. πλείστοις χρώμενον endued with most qualities **most opposed to one another,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 141.2; ἰχθῦς ὑ. αὑτοῖσι 4th-3rd c.BC: Damoxenus Comicus 2.37}}: frequently with _dative_, **opposite** or **contrary to,** ὁ ὑπεναντία τούτοισι λέξας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.50, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.8.24 (_superlative_), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 176a, “Lg.” 810d, 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.28U., “Abh.Berl.Akad.” 1925(5).7 (Cyrene, 3rd c.BC), “SIG” 613.28 (Delph., 2nd c.BC), Wilcken “Chr.” 27.1 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}; so ὑ. πρός τι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1328b41; πρὸς δημοκρατίαν “IG” 22.657.48: as}} _substantive_, τὸ ὑ. τούτου.. πέφυκε is in character the **opposite** of this, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.80}}; τὰ ὑ. τούτων **on the contrary,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.153}}; τὰ ὑ. **incongruities,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1455a26, compare 1461b23.}}
**4** of logical propositions, **subcontrary,** {{Refs 7th c.AD+|7th c.AD: Stephanus Philosophus “in librum Aristotelis de Interpretatione commentarium - pars” 30.38.}} _adverb_ -ίως{{Refs|[prev. work] 32.24.}}
**4.b** in Mathematics texts, **subcontrary,** τομή {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Apollonius Pergaeus Geometra 1.5}}; of a mean, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Archytas Tarentinus Philosophus 2, 3rd c.AD(?): Pappus Mathematicus 84.14, etc.}}
**II** _adverb_ -ίως **in a manner contrary to,** τοῖς νόμοις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.8}}; ὑ. ἔχειν to be **opposed,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 719a28}}; ὑ. κεῖσθαι{{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. work] 719b18; περί τινα ὑ. ὑπάρχει πρός τι [prev. author] “HA” 500a13, +others, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ ποιημάτων” 5.19; ὑ. θεραπεύσεις 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 16.52: also _neuter_ _plural_ ὑπεναντία}} as _adverb_, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“IG” 22.1258.8, 7.2225.36 (Thisbe, 2nd c.BC, senatus consultum).}}

5228:
** ὑπέρ** [ῠ],
_Epic dialect_ also **ὑπείρ**, used by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (for the sake of meter) only in the phrase ὑπεὶρ ἅλα (see. ὑπείρ); 4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus}} **ὁπέρ** (which see): in _Aeolic dialect_ replaced by περί (see. περί {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus V): _preposition_ governing _genitive_ and _accusative_, in 4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus}} also _dative_ (Cf. Sanskrit _upaári_ 'above', Gothic _ufar,_ O{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **ofer** 'over':—from it are formed the _comparative_ and _superlative_ ὑπέρτερος, -τατος, also _adverb_ ὕπερθεν, and Nouns ὑπέρα, ὕπερος.)
**A** WITH GENIT.,
**A.I** of Place, **over;**
**A.I.1** in a state of rest, **over, above,** frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, βάλε.. στέρνον ὑ. μαζοῖο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.528}}; χιτωνίσκους ἐνεδεδύκεσαν ὑ. γονάτων **not reaching to** the knees, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.4.13; ἕστηκε.. ὅσον τ᾽ ὄργυι᾽ ὑ. αἴης 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.327; εἴθ᾽ ὑ. γῆς, εἴτ᾽ ἐπὶ γῆς, εἴθ᾽ ὑπὸ γῆς 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Igne” 1}}; στῆ δ᾽ ἄρ᾽ ὑ. κεφαλῆς stood **over** his head as he lay asleep, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.20, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.803, +others; πασάων ὕ. ἥ γε κάρη ἔχει 6.107; ὑ. πόλιος, ὅθι Ἕρμαιος λόφος ἐστίν, ἦα 16.471}}; ὑ. κεφαλῆς οἱ ἐγίνετο διεξελαύνοντι **over** head, i. e. over the gateway, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.187; ὑ. τῆς ὀροφῆς “IG” 12.373.246; ὑ. τοῦ ἀγάλματος [prev. work]264; ὄρος τὸ ὑ. Τεγέης 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.105}}; τὰ ὑ. κεφαλῆς the higher ground, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 2.20}}; Ἰονίας ὑ. ἁλὸς οἰκέων **on** the Ionian sea, i.e. on its shores, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.65; λιμὴν καὶ πόλις ὑ. αὐτοῦ κεῖται 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.46, compare 6.4, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 40.14}}: of relative geographical position, **above, farther inland,** οἰκέοντες ὑ. Ἁλικαρνησσοῦ μεσόγαιαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.175; ἐξ Αἰθιοπίας τῆς ὑ. Αἰγύπτου 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.48; τοῖς ὑ. Χερρονήσου Θρᾳξίν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.6.2; ὑ. Μασσαλίας 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.14.8, compare 5.73.3, +others}}: in Hellenistic Gr. the _accusative_ is commoner in this sense, see below {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus I.}}
**A.I.1.b** of ships at sea, **off** a place, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.112, 8.95; ναυμαχίην τὴν ὑ. Μιλήτου γενομένην 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.25; ὑ. τούτου (i.e. Φαλήρου) ἀνακωχεύσαντες τὰς νέας [prev. work] 116.}}
**A.I.2** in a state of motion, **over, across,** κῦμα νηὸς ὑ. τοίχων καταβήσεται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.382; τὸν δ᾽ ὑ. οὐδοῦ βάντα προσηύδα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.575; πηδῶντος.. τάφρων ὕ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1279; ὑ. θαλάσσης καὶ χθονὸς ποτωμένοις 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 576}}; ἐκκυβιστᾶν ὑ. [τῶν ξιφῶν] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 2.11.}}
**A.I.3** **over, beyond,** ἐν Κρήτῃ εὐρείῃ τηλοῦ ὑ. πόντου {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.257.}}
**A.II** _metaphorically_, **in defence of, on behalf of,** τεῖχος ἐτειχίσσαντο νεῶν ὕ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.449; ἑκατόμβην ῥέξαι ὑ. Δαναῶν 1.444}}: generally, **for the prosperity** or **safety of,** τὰ ἱερὰ ὑ. τῆς Εὐβοίας θῦσαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12.39.65, compare 45.5; ἱερὰ θυόμενα ὑ. τῆς πόλεως 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.2.13; ἐπιτελεῖν τὰς θυσίας ὑ. τε ὑμῶν καὶ τῶν τέκνων “UPZ” 14.27 (2nd c.BC)}}; in dedications (always with reference to living persons), Σμικύθη μ᾽ ἀνέθηκεν.. εὐξαμένη.. ὑ. παίδων καὶ ἑαυτῆς {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 12.524, compare 22.4403, 42(1).569 (Epid.); Ἀρτέμιδι Σωτείρᾳ ὑ. βασιλέως Πτολεμαίου Ἐπικράτης Ἀθηναῖος “OGI” 18 (Egypt, 3rd c.BC), compare 365 (Amasia, 2nd c.BC), +others; ὑ. τῆς εἰς αἰῶνα διαμονῆς Ἀντωνείνου Καίσαρος [prev. work]702.3 (Egypt, 2nd c.AD); ὑ. τῆς τύχης.. Ἀντωνείνου Σεβαστοῦ Εὐσεβοῦς [prev. work]703.2 (Ptolemais, 2nd c.AD); ὑ. σωτηρίας τοῦ κυρίου ἡμῶν.. Ἀντωνείνου [prev. work]706 (Egypt, 2nd-3rd c.AD)}}; εὑδόντων ὕ. φρούρημα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 705}}; ὑ. τινὸς κινδυνεύειν, μάχεσθαι, βοηθεῖν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.20, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 642c, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.5.6; ἧς ἔθνῃσχ᾽ ὕ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 708; ὑ. γῆς τῆς Ἀθηναίων ναυμαχέειν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.70; ὑ. τῆς Ἑλλάδος ἀμῦναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 692d}}; ἀμυνῶ ὑ. ἱερῶν καὶ ὁσίων Jusj. cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 8.105; νῦν ὑ. πάντων ἀγών 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 405; ὑ. δόξης τελευτήσαντες 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.210, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.93}}; πάνθ᾽ ὑ. ὑμῶν φανήσεται πράξας Χαβρίας, καὶ τὴν τελευτὴν αὐτὴν τοῦ βίου πεποιημένος οὐχ ὑ. ἄλλου τινός **in** your **interests,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.80, compare 83; ὑ. τῆς Ἀσίας στρατηγήσας 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.154}}; of things sought, ὑ. τοῦ νεκροῦ ὠθισμὸς ἐγένετο πολύς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.225}}; ἀφίκετο ὑ. γενεᾶς, ὑ. φωνᾶς, ὑ. τοῦ θησαυροῦ, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 42 (1).121.10,42, 123.11 (Epid., 4th c.BC)}}; γίνωσκέ με πεπορεῦσθαι εἰς Ἡρακλέους πόλιν ὑ. τῆς οἰκίας {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 68.3 (2nd c.BC)}}; sometimes even of the thing to be averted, ἱκέσιον λόχον δουλοσύνας ὕ. **about** slavery, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 111 (Lyric poetry), compare 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.10.}}
**A.II.2** **for, instead of, in the name of,** ὑ. ἑαυτοῦ τι προϊδεῖν **on** his own **behalf,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.141; ὑ. τινὸς ἀποκρίνεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 590a; προλέγειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.7.3; ἐπεὶ οὖν σὺ σιωπᾷς, ἐγὼ λέξω καὶ ὑ. σοῦ καὶ ὑ. ἡμῶν [prev. author] “Cyr.” 3.3.14, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 554}}; ὑ. Ζήνωνος πράσσων **as** Zeno's **representative**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PSI” 4.389.8 (3rd c.BC)}}; ἔγραψεν ὑ. αὐτῶν διὰ τὸ φάσκειν αὐτοὺς μὴ εἰδέναι γράμματα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PGrenf.” 2.17.9 (2nd c.BC)}}; θεάσασθε ὃν τρόπον ὑμεῖς ἐστρατηγηκότες πάντ᾽ ἔσεσθ᾽ ὑ. Φιλίππου as though **by commission from** P., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.6}}; so in other dialects with _accusative_, see below {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus see}}
**A.II.3** in adjurations, with verbs of entreaty, entreat one **as representative of** another, τῶν ὕ. ἐνθάδ᾽ ἐγὼ γουνάζομαι οὐ παρεόντων, i. e. I entreat you as they would if they were here, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.665, compare 660}}; then more _metaphorically_, **by,** λίσσομ᾽ ὑ. ψυχῆς (**as you value** your life) καὶ γούνων σῶν τε τοκήων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|22.338, compare 24.466; λίσσομ᾽ ὑ. θυέων καὶ δαίμονος.. σῆς τ᾽ αὐτοῦ κεφαλῆς καὶ ἑταίρων 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.261; λίσσου᾽ ὑ. μακάρων σέο τ᾽ αὐτῆς ἠδὲ τοκήων 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.701}}; ὑ. ξενίου λίσσεται ὔμμε Διός **in the name of** Zeus, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 7.499.2 (3rd c.BC: Theaetetus Epigrammaticus)}}; so _Aeolic dialect_ περ (see. περί {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus V).}}
**A.II.4** of the cause or motive, **for, because of, by reason of,** ἀλγέων ὕ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 1125 (Lyric poetry); ὑ. παθέων [prev. author] “Hipp.” 159 (Lyric poetry); ἔριδος ὕ. [prev. author] “Andr.” 490 (Lyric poetry)}}; of punishment or reward, **for, on account of,** τοῖσιν ἄγουσιν κλαύμαθ᾽ ὑπάρξει βραδυτῆτος ὕ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 932 (_anapaest meter_), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 11.39, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.43, 4.20, 13.41,42, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.3.4}}; ἀτῆθθαι ὑ. τῶ πατρὸς τὰ πατρώϊα the father's property shall pay the fine **for** the father, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Leg.Gort.” 11.42; ἀποτεισάτω ὁ δεσπότης ὑ. τοῦ δούλου “PHal.” 1.198 (3rd c.BC)}}; τοῦτον (_i.e. _ a runaway slave) ὃς ἂν ἀναγάγῃ, λήψεται ὅσα καὶ ὑ. τοῦ προγεγραμμένου {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 121.24 (2nd c.BC)}}; τὸ κατεσκευασμένον ὑ. τῆς ἡμετέρας σωτηρίας Ἰσιδεῖον **as a thank-offering for..,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|Sammelb. 3926.12 (1st c.BC)}}; ὑ. ὧν ἐτιμήσαμεν αὐτοὺς ταῖς μεγίσταις τιμαῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.57; ἀποδοῦναι χάριν ὑ. ὧν.. ἅπαντας ἀνθρώπους εὐεργέτησεν [prev. author] 4.56}}; of payment, ἡμιωβέλιον ὑ. ἑκάστου {{Refs|“IG” 12.140.2}}; μέτρησον Ποσειδωνίῳ ὑ. Ἡρακλείδου **on account of** H., i.e. debiting H.'s account, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“PFay.” 16 (1st c.BC)}}; μετρήσω ὑ. σοῦ εἰς τὸ δημόσιον **for the credit of** your **account,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|PAmh. 2.88.22 (2nd c.AD)}}; ὑ. λαογραφίας {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Ostr.Bodl.” iii 80 (1st c.AD)}}; ὑ. λόγου ἀννώνης {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“Ostr.” 1479 (3rd c.AD)}}; ὑ. ὧν ἔμαθεν καταβαλεῖν μισθόν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 3.126a, compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 3.39.}}
**A.II.5** ὑ. τοῦ μή with _infinitive_, **for the purpose of preventing** or **avoiding,** ὑ. τοῦ μηδένα.. βιαίῳ θανάτῳ ἀποθνῄσκειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 4.3; ὑ. τοῦ μὴ ποιεῖν τὸ προσταττόμενον 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.64, compare 12.80; τὴν πόλιν ἐκλιπεῖν ὑπέμειναν.. ὑ. τοῦ μὴ τὸ κελευόμενον ποιῆσαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.204: also without μή}} **for the sake of,** ὑ. τοῦτοῖς ἄλλοις ἐπιτάττειν ἐθέλειν ἀποθνῄσκειν to be ready to die **for the sake of**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.94; μὴ τοσαύτην ποιεῖσθαι σπουδὴν ὑ. τοῦ βλάψαι τοὺς πολεμίους ἡλίκην ὑ. τοῦ μηδὲν αὐτοὺς παθεῖν δεινόν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.94.9, compare 5.32.1, 5.86.8}}: this _construction_ is found also in _signification_ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 111.}}
**A.III** **concerning,** ὑ. σέθεν αῐσχε᾽ ἀκούω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.524; κᾶρυξ ἀνέειπέ νιν ἀγγέλλων Ἱέρωνος ὑ. καλλινίκου ἅρμασι 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.32; Σκύθαι μὲν ὧδε ὑ. σφέων τε αὐτῶν καὶ τῆς χώρης τῆς κατύπερθε λέγουσι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.8}}; τὰ λεγόμενα ὑ. ἑκάστων variant in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.123; τοὺς ὑ. τοῦ αἰῶνος φόβους 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Sententiae” 20}}; διαλεχθῆναι, ἀγορεύειν ὑ. τινός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 39e, “Lg.” 776e}}; περὶ μὲν οὖν τούτων τοσαῦτά μοι εἰρήσθω, ὑ. ὧν δέ μοι προσήκει λέγειν.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 24.4, compare 21, 16.20; ὑ. οὗ.. ὁμολογῶ.. διαφέρεσθαι τούτοις 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.31}}; βουλευομένων ὑ. τοῦ ποίαν τινὰ [εἰρήνην ποιητέον] {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] 19.94; ἔγραψάς μοι ὑ. τῶν καμίνων “PCair.Zen.” 273.2 (3rd c.BC)}}; ἐνεκάλουν ὑ. σύκων {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PSI” 6.554.24 (3rd c.BC)}}; ἐπεδώκαμέν σοι ὑπόμνημα ὑ. τοῦ μὴ εἰληφέναι τὴν.. ὄλυραν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 46.4 (2nd c.BC)}}; συλλαλήσαντες ὑ. τοῦ τὴν πόλιν ἐνδοῦναι τοῖς Ῥωμαίοις {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.43.1}}; θροῦς ὑ. τοῦ τὸν Λυκοῦργον ἐκπέμπειν talk **of** sending L., {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.18.5, compare 6}}; γνώμην ὑ. τῆς κοινῆς [δόξης] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.93; ὑ. τῶν τούτου λῃτουργιῶν.. ὡδὶ γιγνώσκω 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.152; ἐκ τῶν ἐμφανῶν ὑ. τῶν ἀφανῶν πιστεύειν 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 4.138b; with verbs expressing emotion, ποίας.. γυναικὸς ἐκφοβεῖσθ᾽ ὕ.; 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 989; εἰ τὰ παρὰ σοὶ καλῶς ἔχει, θάρρει ὑ. ἐκείνων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.1.17; οὐδεὶς ὑ. μου δαιμόνων μηνίεται κατασφαγείσης 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 101 (approaching sense 11.1).}}
**B** WITH ACCUSATIVE
**B.I** of Place in reference to motion, **over, beyond,** frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, e.g. ὑ. ὦμον ἤλυθ᾽ ἀκωκή 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.16, compare 851; ἀλάλησθε.. ὑπεἰρ ἅλα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.73, compare 7.135, +others, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 250, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1145 (Lyric poetry); ὑ. τὸν δρύφακτον ὑπερτιθέμενοι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.22.10}}: without such reference, ὑ. Ἡρακλείας στήλας ἔξω κατοικοῦσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 108e, compare 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 1.6d; τὰς κεφαλὰς ὑ. τὸ ὑγρὸν ὑπερίσχον 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.84.9; τῶν ὑ. τὸ Σαρδῷον πέλαγος τόπων [prev. author] 2.14.6; ὑ. Μασσαλίαν [prev. author] 2.16.1; λόφον κείμενον ὑ. τὴν ὁδόν [prev. author] 2.27.5, compare 3.47.2, +others; τῶν συριῶν ὑ. τὴν σκηνὴν οὐσῶν “PHib.” 1.38.7 (3rd c.BC)}}; οὐλὴ.. ὑ. ὀφρὺν δεξιάν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 76.13 (3rd c.BC)}}; τὸ ὑ. τὸν ἔσχατον.. σπόνδυλον {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.102; ὑ. τὸν οὐρανόν 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 4.135a.}}
**B.II** of Measure, **above, exceeding, beyond,** ὑ. τὸν ἀλαθῆ λόγον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.28; ὑ. τὸ βέλτιστον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 378 (Lyric poetry); ὑ. ἐλπίδα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 366 (Lyric poetry); ὑ. δύναμιν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.16; μεγέθει ὑ. τοὺς ἐν τῇ νηΐ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 488b; ὑ. ἄνθρωπον εἶναι [prev. author] “Lg.” 839d, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Vit.Auct.” 2}}; ὑ. ἡμᾶς **beyond** our powers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 128b; ὑ. τὴν ἀξίαν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 146; ὑ. τὴν οὐσίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 372b}}; ὑ. τὸ ὕδωρ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|compare (ὕδωρ 1.4) 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Pr.Im.” 29.}}
**B.II.b** after _comparative_, **than,** δυνατώτεροι ὑ... {{LXX|LXX.Judg.18.26: so after Posit., τοῖς ἀγαθοῖς ὑ. αὐτόν}} **better than** he,{{LXX|LXX.3Ki.2.32.}}
**B.II.2** of transgression, **in violation of,** ὑ. αἶσαν, opposed to κατ᾽ αἶσαν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.59, +others; ὑ. Διὸς αἶσαν 17.321; ὑ. μοῖραν 20.336}}; ὑ. μόρον (or ὑπέρμορον){{Refs|[prev. work] 30; ὑ. θεόν 17.327; ὑ. ὅρκια 3.299, +others}}
**B.III** of Number, **above, upwards of,** τὰ ὑ. δέκα μνᾶς [ξυμβόλαια] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12.41.23, compare 22.48, +others; ὑ. τεσσεράκοντα ἄνδρας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.64}}; ὑ. τετταράκοντα (i.e. ἔτη) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.4.13; ὑ. τὰ στρατεύσιμα ἔτη γεγονόσι [prev. author] “Cyr.” 1.2.4}}; ὑ. ἥμισυ **more than** half, {{Refs|[prev. work] 3.3.47.}}
**B.IV** of Time, **beyond,** i.e. **before, earlier than,** ὁ ὑ. τὰ Μηδικὰ πόλεμος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.41; ὑ. τὴν φθοράν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 23c.}}
**B.V** in some dialects, in sense {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 11.1,2}}, **on behalf of,** ὑ. τὰν πόλιν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 437 (Delph., 3rd c.BC), +others,compare “IG” 42(1).109iv113 (Epid., 3rd c.BC), 5(2).438-40,442 (Megalopolis, 2nd c.BC), 42(1).380,665 (Epid., 1st c.AD), “IPE” 4.71.10 (Cherson., 2nd c.AD)}}; in sense {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 111}}, **concerning,** ἐπικράνθη μοι ὑ. ὑμᾶς {{LXX|LXX.Ruth.1.13.}}
**C** WITH DAT., only {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus, μαχόμενοι ὑ. τᾷ τᾶς πόλιος ἐλευθερίᾳ}} fighting **for..,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 5(2).16 (Tegea, 3rd c.BC).}}
**D** POSITION: ὑπέρ may follow its _substantive_, but then by anastrophe becomes ὕπερ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.339, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.450, +others, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1444, etc.}}
**E** AS _adverb_., **over-much, above measure,** ὑπὲρ μὲν ἄγαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 627 (Lyric poetry)}}; also written ὑπεράγαν, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 3.2.9, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 3.38, etc.}}; compare ὑπέρφευ: as a predicate, διάκονοι Χριστοῦ εἰσ; ὑπὲρ ἐγώ I am **more** [than they], {{NT|NT.2Cor.11.23.}}
**F** IN COMPOSITION (joined with other words), signifies **over, above,** in all relations, e. g.,
**F.1** of Place, **over, beyond,** as in ὑπεράνω, ὑπέργειος, ὑπερβαίνω, ὑπερπόντιος.
**F.2** of doing a thing **for** or **in defence of,** as in ὑπερμαχέω, ὑπερασπίζω, ὑπεραλγέω.
**F.3** **above measure,** as in ὑπερήφανος, ὑπερφίαλος.

5229:
** ὑπεραίρω**,
**lift** or **raise up over,** εἰς τὸν ἔξω τόπον τὴν τοῦ ἡνιόχου κεφαλήν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 248a; τὴν ὀφρῦν ὑπὲρ τοὺς κροτάφους 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 54}}; ἑρμάτων ὑ. τὸ σκάφος **over** the rocks, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 3.23}}; ὑ. τὸ φθέγμα **raise** it **very high,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Ner.” 9}}:—_middle_, **lift oneself** or **rise above,** πάντων {{Refs|Anonymus in Rhetores Graeci 1.632}} W.: _absolutely_, **to be lifted up,** {{NT|NT.2Cor.12.7}}; **give oneself airs, be coy,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Aristaenetus Rhetor 1.17, 2.6}}; **exalt oneself above,** ἐπὶ θεόν {{NT|NT.2Thes.2.4.}}
**2** **heighten,** ναῦν τοίχοις {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 3.35.}}
**II** _intransitive_,
**II.1** with _accusative_, **jump over,** τειχία {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitum magistro” 8.3}}; **cross,** Ἄλπεις {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.23.1; ὑ. τὸ πέλαγος}}**pass over,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.28.1}}; ὑ. τὴν ἄκραν **double** the cape, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.54.7}}; κάμψαντες τὸν Πάχυνον ὑ. [τὸ πέλαγος] εἰς.. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.25.8; also τὸν Καταράκτην “OGI” 654.6 (Egypt, 1st c.BC)}}: _absolutely_, **cross the sea,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.47.2}}: as naval and military term, **outflank,** τὸ λαιὸν τῶν πολεμίων {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.50.6, compare 3.73.7}}, etc.: without a sense of motion, **rise above,** τὸ ὕδωρ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.8.10; τὸ μέγεθος τοῦ δένδρου [prev. author] “CP” 5.14.9.}}
**II.2** **excel,** τινι **in** a thing, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.220}}: with _accusative_, **excel,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] 60.21, Aristeas 16, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Heroicus” 2.19; τοὺς πρὸ ἑαυτοῦ ἡγεμονεύσαντας “SIG” 877 A 5 (Delph., 2nd-3rd c.AD)}}; νοῦν ὑπεραίρει {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.7.22.}}
**II.3** **overshoot, go beyond,** μήθ᾽ ὑπεράρας μήθ᾽ ὑποκάμψας καιρόν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 786 (_anapaest meter_); ὑ. τὸν ὡρισμένον καιρόν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.14.11; τὴν συνήθειαν [prev. author] 28.14.2}}; **exceed,** ὑ. τῆς οὐσίας τὸ μέγεθος ὁ τῶν τέκνων ἀριθμός {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1266b11; οὗ ἡ πρόνοια τὰς πάντων εὐχὰς οὐκ ἐπλήρωσε μόνον ἀλλὰ καὶ ὑπερῆρε “BMus.Inscription” 894.8 (Halic., 1st c.AD)}}; πυγωνιαῖον ἢ μικρὸν ὑπεραῖρον a little **more,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.6.8}}; τῶν ὑπεραιρόντων ἱερέων the priests **in excess** (of a certain number), {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“BGU” 1.16 (3rd c.AD)}}; τῶν ὑπεραιρούντων (sic) τὸν ἀριθμὸν τῶν ἱερέων {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PLond.” 2.347.6 (3rd c.AD)}}; τὸ ὑπεραῖρον ἀργύριον the money (received) **in excess**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 976.27 (Samos, 2nd c.BC)}}; τοσοῦτον ἐν [τοῖς δαπανηθεῖσιν] ὑπερῆρεν [αὐτόν] he so far **exceeded** him in his expenditure, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 37.8}}; λόγος ὑπεραίρων τοῖς ὀνόμασι καὶ ταῖς γνώμαις **overdone,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 8.6}}; τὸ ὑπεραῖρον **exaggeration,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 16.12.9.}}
**III** with _genitive_, **pass beyond, double** a cape, τοῦ ἀκρωτηρίου {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 3.24}}; **rise above,** τῆς γῆς {{Refs|[prev. author] “Her.” 19.16.}}
**III.2** **transcend, exceed,** μήθ᾽ ὑπεραίροντα τῶν εἰθισμένων ὄγκων (τὸν εἰθ. ὄγκον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes) μήτ᾽ ἐλλείποντα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 717d, compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 75.13 (with _genitive_), etc.; ὑ. τῷ μεγέθει τινῶν 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 20.91, etc.}}; **overcome,** τέχνῃ τοῦ ῥοθίου {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 7.26.}}
**III.3** with _genitive_, **despise,**{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|[prev. work] 1.2, 3rd c.AD: Philostratus Junior Sophista “Imagines” 7.}}
**IV** **overflow,** τὰ ἀγγεῖα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mirabilia” 835a32}}: _absolutely_, of a stream, ὑ. εἰς τὰ χωρία {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 55.10.}}
**IV.2** ὑ. ὑπέρ τι **project** beyond.., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Tactica” 12.9}}; οἱ ἐν τῷ τρίτῳ ζυγῷ ὑ. τοὺς πρωτοστάτας πήχεις σ, i.e. their σάρισαι **project beyond**.., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Tactica - Griechische Kriegsschriftsteller” 14.4}}; ὑπεραίρειν ἔξω τὰ βλέφαρα **project beyond** the eyelids, of a tumour, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 7.36}}, compare 15; **overlap,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Aristarchus Samius Astronomus 8.}}
**V** Ὑπεραίρων, οντος, ὁ, **Most Excellent,** = Latin _Exsuperatorius,_ name given to December by Commodus, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 72.15.}}

5231:
** ὑπεράνω** [ᾰ],
_adverb_ **above,** opposed to ὑποκάτω, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 588.31 (Milet., 2nd c.BC)}}; οἰκεῖν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DDeor.” 4.2, etc.}}; **above the horizon,** {{LXX+4th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra “Phaenomena” p.8 M.: mostly with _genitive_, ὑ. τούτων [τῶν μορίων] σχίζεται [ἡ φλέψ] 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 513b32, compare “PSI” 6.631.6 (3rd c.BC), LXX.Eze.43.15, +others}}; ὑ. γίγνεσθαί τινος to get **the upper hand of,**{{Refs 1st c.BC+|Teles p.44 H., 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.10b, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Morte - Papiri Ercolanesi” 34; ὑ. τεθεῖσθαι πάντων [prev. author] “Piet.” 102}}; ποιεῖν or ποιεῖσθαί τινα or τι ὑ. τινός, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.98e, 6b}}; πάντων ὑ. ποιεῖν act **more nobly** than all others, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 7.128.}}
**2** οἱ ὑ. πλεονασμοί **excessive** repetitions, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 12.24.1}}; but μίαν ὑ. ποιότητα one **supreme** quality, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Meno Medicus “Iatrika” 14.18.}}
**3** of time, **further back,** ἐκ τῶν ὑ. χρόνων {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“SIG” 742.58 (Ephesus, 1st c.BC).}}
**4** **above,** in a document, [ψηφίσματα] ὑ. γεγραμμένα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. work]591.2 (Lampsacus, 2nd c.BC).}}

5232:
** ὑπεραυξ-άνω**
and **ὑπεραύξ-ω**, **increase above measure**:—_passive_, **to be so increased,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 14.226}}; **become over-powerful,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.24, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 79.15.}}
**2** _passive_ also, **grow above,** ὑπεραύξονται τῶν ἀμπέλων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1282.}}
**II** _intransitive_ in _active_, **grow** or **abound exceedingly,** ὑπεραυξήσας (of a fish) Callisth. cited in {{NT+5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.36.16; ὑπεραυξάνει ἡ πίστις ὑμῶν NT.2Thes.1.3.}}

5233:
** ὑπερβαίνω**,
_future_ **-βήσομαι**{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 94:}}_aorist 2_ ὑπερέβην, _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ὑπέρβᾰσαν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.469}}:—**step over, mount, scale,** with _accusative_, τεῖχος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” [same place]; οὐδόν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.80; τείχη 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 654, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.20; γεῖσα τειχέων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1180; τάφρους [prev. author] “Rh.” 111}}; ὑ. τοὺς οὔρους **cross** the boundaries, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.108}}; τὰ ὄρεα, Αἷμον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 4.25, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.96}}; δόμους **step over** the threshold of the house, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 382 codices; δῶμα [prev. author] “Ion” 514 (troch., assuming variant); ὑ. τέγος ὡς τοὺς γείτονας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.53}}; ὑ. τὴν οἰκίαν τινός, of burglars, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 796.2 (2nd c.BC); but more usually ὑ. εἰς τὴν οἰκίαν [prev. work] 793vi21 (2nd c.BC),compare “BGU” 1007.10 (3rd c.BC), “PSI” 4.396.4 (3rd c.BC)}} (the usage with _genitive_ is more than uncertain; in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.54 the best codices have ἐπέβησαν}}; in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 1049 Kirchhoff restored ὑπεκβᾶσ᾽}}; in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Ion” 220 2nd-3rd c.AD: Hermes Trismegistus supplied βᾱλόν): _absolutely_, ὑ. εἰς τὴν τῶν Θηβαίων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.4.59; τῶν [ἡδονῶν] εἰς τὸ ἐπέκεινα ὑ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 587c}}; of rivers, **overflow,** ἐς τὴν χώρην, ἐς τὰς ἀρούρας, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.13, 14; εἰ ἐθελήσει ὑπερβῆναι ὁ ποταμὸς ταύτῃ [prev. work] 99.}}
**2** **overstep, transgress,** μέτρα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus [same place]; οὐ θέμιν οὐδὲ δίκαν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 1.5; νόμους τοὺς Περσέων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.83, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 449, +others; τοὺς ὅρκους 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 11.2; τὸν τῶν ἀναγκαίων ὅρον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 373d; τῆς εἱμαρμένης ὅρον “IG” 12(7).53.32 (Amorgos, 3rd c.AD)}}; τἀληθές **exceed** the truth, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ ποιημάτων” 5.24}}: _absolutely_, **transgress, trespass, sin,** ὅτε κέν τις ὑπερβήῃ (_Epic dialect_ _aorist_ _subjunctive_) καὶ ἁμάρτῃ {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.501; ὑ. καὶ ἁμαρτάνοντες 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 366a, cf. NT.1Thes.4.6.}}
**3** **pass** or **go beyond,** τοὺς προσεχέας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.89}}; **leave out, omit,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 528d, +others, 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 3p.63U., 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.592, etc.; ὑ. τι τῷ λόγῳ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.38; ὑ. τὸ σαφὲς εἰπεῖν [prev. author] 60.31}}; **pass over,** i. e. leave unmolested, the next heir, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 3.57}}; ὑ. τῆς οὐσίας **omit** part of it, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Posteriora” 91b27.}}
**4** **jump across** an intervening space, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 3.9.}}
**5** of Time, **pass by, elapse,** ὑπερβάντων τῶν τῆς συμπαθείας χρόνων {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.41.}}
**II** **go beyond,** ὑπερβὰς ἑβδομήκοντα [ἔτη] **after passing** the age of seventy, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 755b}}; ὑ. τοῦτο **go beyond** this, in their demands, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.15.6}}; **transcend,** τὸν νοῦν {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.7.39}}: _absolutely_, **dies** ὑπερβαίνοντες **supernumerary** days in the calendar, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD: Macrobius Grammaticus “Sat.” 1.13.10.}}
**II.2** **surpass, outdo,** πάσῃ παρὰ πάντας ἀνθρώπους ὑ. ἀρετῇ {{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 24d; ὑ. ἢ γνῶσιν σαφηνείᾳ ἢ ἄγνοιαν ἀσαφείᾳ [prev. author] “R.” 478c: _absolutely_, uncertain reading in 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1015.}}
**III** **stand over. shield, protect,** with _dative_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 1.710.}}
**IV** in _perfect_, **to be higher than,** δύο [ἐσχάρας] ὑπερβεβηκυίας τὴν ἐν τῷ μεταφρένῳ ἐσχάραν {{Refs 7th c.AD+|7th c.AD: Paulus Aegineta Medicus 6.44.}}
**B** Causal in _aorist_ 1, **put over,** ὑπερβησάτω ἐπὶ τὰς δεξιὰς πλευρὰς τὴν κνήμην, as a direction to one mounting a horse, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 7.2.}}

5234:
** ὑπερβάλλω**,
_future_ -βαλῶ, _Epic dialect_ **-βαλέω**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.597}}: _Epic dialect_ _aorist 2_ ὑπειρέβαλον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.637}}:—**throw over** or **beyond a mark, overshoot,** ὑπέρβαλε σήματα πάντων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.843; τόσσον παντὸς ἀγῶνος (i.e. σήματα) ὑπέρβαλε [prev. work] 847}}; δουρὶ ὑ. Φυλῆα **beat** him **in throwing** with it, {{Refs|[prev. work] 637.}}
**2** ὅτε μέλλοι ἄκρον [λόφον] ὑπερβαλέειν **force** the stone **over** the top, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” [prev. cited]}}
**3** _intransitive_, **run beyond, overrun** the scent, of hounds, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 6.20.}}
**4** **outstrip** or **pass,** in racing, τινας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 716.}}
**5** _middle_, **throw** a stone **over** one's head, {{Refs|“SIG” 1071 (from Olympia).}}
**II** in various _metaphorically_ senses:
