**III.4** **exceed,** i. e. overlap, a base, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 6.29; compare ὑπερβολή IV.—Note, the case that follows is almost always the _accusative_; the _genitive_ occurs in a few exceptional instances, see above 11.2 b, 111.1 and 2.}}
**B** _middle_, with _perfect_ _passive_, = {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 11}}, **outdo, overcome, conquer,** τινα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.124, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 758 (Lyric poetry), “Nu.” 1035; τὴν βασιλέος δύναμιν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.24; μάχῃ ὑ. τινά 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 691; φίλτροις ὑ. τινά 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 584, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 413}}: _absolutely_, **to be conqueror,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.9, 7.168.}}
**B.2** **exceed, surpass,** τινα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.342, etc.; τοὺς ἀπ᾽ αἰῶνος “OGI” 542.11 (Ancyra, 2nd c.AD)}}; πάντας τῷ ὕψεϊ καὶ τῷ μεγάθεϊ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.175, compare 110; τινὰ ἀναιδείᾳ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 409}}; θωπείαις{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 890; εἴς τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 115d; ἔν τινι 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 1.1.2.}}
**B.2.b** δόσι χρημάτων ὑ. **surpass all,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.61; ἀρετῇ [prev. author] 9.71}}; ὑπερβαλλόμενος πλήθεϊ with **overpowering** numbers, {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.21:}}_perfect_ _participle_ _passive_, ὑπερβεβλημένη γυνή an **excellent, surpassing** woman, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 153; φύσις ὑπερβεβλ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 558b; ταφῆς τῆς μὲν ὑπερβεβλ., τῆς δὲ ἐλλειπούσης [prev. author] “Lg.” 719d: with _genitive_, γόγγροι τῶν παρ᾽ ἡμῖν ὑπερβεβλ. κατὰ τὸ μέγεθος 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 3.2.7.}}
**B.3** **overbid, outbid** (see. above {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 11.4), τινὰ χρήμασιν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 232c, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.3.32.}}
**B.4** **outflank,** τὸ κέρας τῶν πολεμίων {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Asclepiodotus Tacticus “Tact.” 10.2, compare18.}}
**B.II** **put off, postpone,** τὴν ἀπόδοσιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.9; τὴν συμβολήν [prev. author] 9.45; εἰς ἄλλον καιρόν 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.223S.}}; but ἢν ὑπερβάλωνται ἐκείνην τὴν ἡμέραν.. συμβολὴν μὴ ποιεύμενοι if **they let** that day **pass** without fighting, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.51}}: _absolutely_, **delay, linger,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.71, 76, 7.206; εἰς αὖθις ὑπερβαλέσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 254d, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica ad Alexandrum” 1420a8, 1438b6.}}

5236:
** ὑπερβολ-ή**, ἡ,
(ὑπερβάλλω) **a throwing beyond** others, δίσκων ὑπερβολαῖς {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Philostrati majoris imagines” 2.19}}: in _intransitive_ sense, **altitude** of a star, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 342b32.}}
**2** **overshooting, superiority,** χερῶν ὑπερβολαῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 434; στρατιᾶς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.31.}}
**3** **excess,** τοῦ μεγέθεος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Archytas Tarentinus Philosophus 1}}; opposed to ἔλλειψις or ἔνδεια, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 356a, 357a, 357b; θερμασίης 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης” 2.65; ὑ. δισσὴ.., τῷ ποσῷ καὶ τῷ ποιῷ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 668b14}}; ὑπερβολὴν τῆς ἐπιθυμίας ἔχειν with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.33, etc.: hence in various phrases, χρημάτων ὑπερβολῇ.. πρίασθαι}} at an **extravagant** price, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 232}}; ἐπέφερον τὴν ὑ. τοῦ καινοῦσθαι pushed on their **extravagance** in revolutionizing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.82}}; οὐκ ἔχον ἐστὶν ὑπερβολήν it can go no further, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.119, compare 25.54; ἃ μηδὲ πιθανὰς τὰς ὑ. ἔχει 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἥρως” 62}}; so οὐδεμίαν or μηδεμίαν ὑ. λείπειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.5,110, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.25; οὐδεμίαν ὑ. καταλείπων φιλοτιμίας “SIG” 545.13 (Delph., 3rd c.BC)}}; εἴ τις ὑ. τούτου if there is **aught beyond** (worse than) this, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.66, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.42}}; ταῦτ᾽ οὐχ ὑ.; is not this **the extreme, the last degree?** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.38}}; ὑπερβολὴν ποιήσομαι I will put **an extreme case,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 19.332}}; τοσαύτην ὑ. ποιεῖσθαι ὥστε.. to go so **far** that.., {{Refs|[prev. author] 18.190: followed by a _genitive_, ὑ. ποιεῖσθαι ἐκείνων τῆς αὑτοῦ βδελυρίας}} to carry his own rascality **beyond** theirs, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 22.52, compare 23.201, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.22, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 14.38}}; ὑ. ποιεῖν τῆς τιμῆς to **raise** the price, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1259a26; εἰς ὑ. εὐδαιμονίας ἥκειν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 11.14; τοσαύτας ὑ. δωρειῶν παρές χηται 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.141}}; ὑ. ἀνοίας ἔχειν Polystr.p.27 W.
**4** with _preposition_ in Adverbial phrases, ={ὑπερβαλλόντως}, εἰς ὑπερβολήν **in excess, exceedingly;** εἰς ὑ. ἄμεινον [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 494; ἀγαθὸς εἰς ὑ. 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 80.11; ἐς ὑ. ἐκθερμαίνεσθαι 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης” 2.65: with _genitive_, κτήσαιτ᾽ ἂν ὄλβον εἰς ὑ. πατρός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 282.6 (variant{εἰς ὑπεκτροφὴν πάτρας})}}]; **far beyond,** τοῦ πρόσθεν εἰς ὑ. πανοῦργος, i. e. **far more** wicked, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Hipp.” 939, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 61.33; ἀναλίσκειν πάντα εἰς ὑπερβολάς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 326d:— ἐξ ὑπερβολῆς 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 8.15.8:}} —καθ᾽ ὑπερβολὰν τοξεύσας **with surpassing** aim, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1196 (Lyric poetry); καθ᾽ ὑ. ἐπαινεῖν}}**extravagantly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.11}}; οἱ καθ᾽ ὑ. ἐν ἐνδείᾳ ὄντες in **extremity** of need, {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1295b18; αἱ καθ᾽ ὑ. ἡδοναί [prev. author] “EN” 1151a12; τὸ καθ᾽ ὑ. τραχύ 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Po.Herc.” 994.35; καθ᾽ ὑ. ἁμαρτωλός NT.Rom.7.13: with _genitive_, καθ᾽ ὑ. φιλοδοξίας “OGI” 472.9 (Didyma, 1st c.AD)}}:—so in _dative_, εὐτελὴς ὑπερβολῇ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 615; παχεῖ᾽ ὑ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 41; ὑ. ἀγαθός 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 625a29, etc.}}
**5** **preeminence, perfection,** without any notion of excess, δι᾽ ἀρετῆς ὑπερβολήν {{Refs|[prev. author] “EN” 1145a24, compare “Rh.” 1367b1, “Pol.” 1284a4}}; ἡ ὑ. τῆς φιλίας **the best and noblest kind** of friendship, {{Refs|[prev. author] “EN” 1166b1}}; but ἡ καθ᾽ ὑ. φιλία, ={ἡ καθ᾽ ὑπεροχήν}, {{Refs|[prev. author] “EE” 1238b18.}}
**6** **overstrained phrase, hyperbole,** ὑπερβολὰς εἰπεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.88; οἱ πρὸς ὑπερβολὴν πεπονημένοι λόγοι [prev. work]11; ὑπερβολὰς εἰπεῖν}} make **strong statements,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.35, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.64}}; as a figure of speech, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1413a29, 4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 52, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 3.2.9; πρὸς -ὴν εἰρημένος [prev. author] 1.2.33.}}
**7** τὸ καθ᾽ ὑπερβολήν **the superlative degree,** in Adjectives, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 134b24}}; τιθέναι ὑπερβολῇ{{Refs|[prev. work] 139a9; καθ᾽ ὑ. εἰπεῖν [prev. author] “Cael.” 281a16.}}
**8** **overbid** at auction, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 2p.44 (3rd c.BC).}}
**II** **crossing over, passage** of mountains, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.25, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.34.6, etc.}}
**II.2** in _singular_ or _plural_, **place of passage, mountain-pass,** with or without τοῦ ὄρους, τῶν ὀρέων, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.5.18, 4.1.21, 4.4.18}}; ὑ. τοῦ Ταύρου Wilcken {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Chr.” 1 ii 14 (3rd c.BC)}}; αἱ τῶν Ἄλπεων ὑ. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.39.10; αἱ Ἄλπιαι ὑ. 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 7.1.5; ἡ κατὰ τὸν Αἷμον ὑ. 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 19.73.}}
**III** (from _middle_) **delay,** τοῦ κακοῦ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.112, cf. Decrees cited in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.29, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 14.9.8; [τῆς κρίσεως] ὑ. λαβούσης “PEnteux.” 65.3 (3rd c.BC).}}
**IV** the conic section called **hyperbola,** because the square of the ordinate is equal to a rectangle with height equal to the abscissa applied to the parameter (as base) but **exceeding** (ὑπερβάλλον), i. e. overlapping, that base, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Biographus Perg. “Con.” 1.12, 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in primum Euclidis librum commentarius” p.419F.}}

5237:
** ὑπερορ-άω**,
_future_ -όψομαι: _aorist_ ὑπερεῖδον, _infinitive_ -ῐδεῖν: _aorist_ _passive_ ὑπερώφθην:— **look over, look down upon,** with _accusative_, τὴν θάλασσαν ὑπερορῶντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.36.}}
**II** **overlook, take no notice of,** τοὺς πονηροὺς ὑπερορᾷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.77; τὴν ὕβριν ὑπερεώρακε 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.116}}; οὐκ ὀλίγα τῶν προσόδων ὑ. **remit**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“OGI” 56.16 (Canopus, 3rd c.BC)}}; δι᾽ ὄνειρον.. ὑπεριδεῖν τὸ συμφέρον {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.4}}: with _participle_, οὐχ ὑπεροψόμενοί τινας ἀφαιρεθέντας {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 5.52.}}
**II.2** **despise, disdain,** ὑπεριδὼν Ἴωνας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.69, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ κακιῶν ί” p.27 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus; λόγους ὑπεριδεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.62; σφῶν τὸ πλῆθος ὑπεριδών [prev. author] 5.6, compare 6.11; ὑπερείδετε τὴν ἐμὴν ὁμιλίαν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 8.7; πλὴν ἀρετῆς πάντα ὑ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 120e; πάντα τἀνθρώπινα ὑπερεώρα πρὸς τὴν παρὰ τῶν θεῶν ξυμβουλίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.3.4}}:—_passive_, ἡ Λακεδαίμων κακῶς ἤκουσε καὶ ὑπερώφθη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.28, compare 7.42; ὑπ᾽ ἐκείνων ὑπερορᾶσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 232d.}}
**II.2.b** less frequently with _genitive_, ὑπερορῶ τῆς ἀπολογίας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.3.4; ὑμῶν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.338; τῶν νόμων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.9; πενίας 5th c.BC: Gorgias Rhetor et Sophista “Pal.” 32}}; τῶν μὲν ζῴων φροντίσαι, τῶν δ᾽ οὕτω τιμίων (i.e. τῶν ἄστρων) ὑ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 290a32; ὑπερεῖδε τῶν ἀνθρωπείων ἀγαθῶν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Demon.” 3, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.108,312.}}

5242:
** ὑπερέχω**,
_Epic dialect_ **ὑπειρ-** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus (see. below 1.2)}}: _Epic dialect_ _imperfect_ ὑπείρεχον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.426:}}_aorist_ ὑπερέσχον, and in _poetry_ form -έσχεθον, {{Refs|11.735, 24.374}}: _future_ **-έξω**{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 60.6 (3rd c.BC), 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}:—**hold over,** σπλάγχνα.. ὑπείρεχον Ἡφαίστοιο **held** them **over** the fire, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.426; μου τὸ σκιάδειον ὑπέρεχε 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1508; ἡμῶν ὑπερεῖχε τὴν χύτραν [prev. author] “Eq.” 1176}}; ὑπερέχοντα τὸν αὐλὸν τῆς θαλάσσης **holding** it **up out of** the sea, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 537b1.}}
**2** ὑ. χεῖρά (χεῖράς) τινος **hold** one's hand **over** him, **so as to protect,** μάλα γάρ ἑθεν εὐρύοπα Ζεὺς χεῖρα ἑὴν ὑπερέσχε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.420, 687; τις.. ἐμεῖο θεῶν ὑπερέσχεθε χεῖρα 24.374; Ζεὺς τῆσδε πόληος ὑπειρέχοι.. χεῖρα 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 757; so πόλεως ἵν᾽ ὑπερέχοιεν ἀλκάν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 215 (Lyric poetry), compare “fragment” 199.7: with _dative_ _person_, οἱ.. ὑπείρεχε χεῖρας Ἀπόλλων 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.433; αἴ κ᾽ ὔμμιν ὑπέρσχῃ χεῖρα Κρονίων 4.249, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.184.}}
**3** **hold above,** ὑ. τὸ ῥύγχος, ὅπως ἀναπνέῃ, of the dolphin, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 589b11, compare 566b15, 599b27, +others; ὑ. ὀφρύν}}**elevate,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|Anthologia Graeca 5.298 (6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus).}}
**II** _intransitive_, **to be above, rise above the horizon,** εὖτ᾽ ἀστὴρ ὑπερέσχε φαάντατος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.93}}; αὐτῆς [Αἰγύπτου] εἶναι οὐδὲν ὑπερέχον no part of it **was above water,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.4}}; ὕδωρ,.. ὃ μόλις ὑπερέχοντες ἐπεραιώθησαν which they crossed, **with their heads** only just **above** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.23}}; ἕψεται ἄχρι ἂν ὑπερέχῃ τὸ ὕδωρ till it **sticks out above** the water, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 3.7}}; but ἐπιχέας ὕδωρ ὥστε ὑπερέχειν till it **covers** (i.e. the contents of the vessel), {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.87; τὸ κέρας τὸ ἕτερον ἢ καὶ ἀμφότερα ὑπερέχοντα}}**projecting above the ground,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.41}}; γεῖσον.. ὑπερέχον τρία ἡμιπόδια **projecting** a foot and a half, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“IG” 22.1668.34, compare 7.3073.71 (Lebad., 2nd c.BC): with _genitive_, ὑπερέσχεθε γαίης}} **rose above, overlooked** the earth, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.735; ὄμμ᾽ ὑπερσχὸν ἴτυος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1384; [σταυροὺς] οὐχ ὑπερέχοντας τῆς θαλάσσης 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.25; σκεύη ὑπερέχοντα τοῦ τειχίου 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 514c, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.5.7; ὤφθη.. ὁ δεξιὸς ὀφθαλμὸς ὑπερέχειν θατέρου παμπόλλῳ δή τινι 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(2).301.}}
**II.2** **overtop, be prominent above,** στάντων μὲν Μενέλαος ὑπείρεχεν εὐρέας ὤμους, i. e. stood (head and) shoulders above them, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.210; ὅκως τινὰ ἴδοι τῶν ἀσταχύων ὑπερέχοντα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.92.ζ,compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1284a37; φιλέει ὁ θεὸς τὰ ὑπερέχοντα πάντα κολούειν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.10.έ,compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.2.17}}; [ᾡ] ὁ πρῶτος ὅρος ὑ. τοῦ δευτέρου.. μέρει by the fraction by which the first term **exceeds** the second, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Archytas Tarentinus Philosophus 2: τὸ ὑπερέχον}} the **excess,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Diophantus Mathematicus 1.6.}}
**II.3** in military phrase, **outflank,** τῶν πολεμίων ὑ. τῷ κέρατι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.2.18, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.107.}}
**II.4** _metaphorically_, with _accusative_, **overtop, excel, outdo,** βροτῶν πάντων ὑπερσχὼν ὄλβον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 709 (troch.); σωφροσύνῃ πάντας ὑ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1365 (_anapaest meter_); πελταστικῷ εἰκὸς ὑ. τὴν ἡμετέραν δύναμιν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.1.9.}}
**II.4.b** with _genitive_, πάντων ὑ. μεγέθει καὶ ἀρετῇ {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 24e, compare “Prm.” 150e, “Grg.” 475c; ὑ. τῶν πολλῶν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.206, cf. NT.Phil.2.3; ἁπάντων ὑπερέχουσι τῶν κακῶν 4th c.BC: Anaxilas Comicus 22.7 (troch.).}}
**II.4.c** _absolutely_, **prevail,** θεῶν ὑπερέσχε νόος {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 202}}; οἱ ὑπερσχόντες the **more powerful,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 215; τῶν πόλεων αἱ ὑπερέχουσαι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.95}}; οἱ ὑπερέχοντες **those in authority,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 6.78, compare 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 61.30, +others}} ἐπειδὰν ἡ θάλαττα ὑπέρσχῃ **has prevailed,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.69}}; ἐν τοῖς πολεμίοις ὑ. **excel** in.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 642}}; ἐνδέχεται.. μὴ τοσοῦτον ὑ. τῷ ποσῷ, ὅσον λείπεσθαι τῷ ποιῷ **exceed** so much.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1296b23}}; ὑπὲρ ὧν πλειονάκι ἐντετευχυιῶν ὑπερέχων ἡμᾶς ἀπράκτους καθίστησι **being too strong for** us, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Sammelb.” 4638.18 (2nd c.BC)}}; πᾶν κρύφιον οὐχ ὑπερεῖχε σέ **was beyond** thee (i. e. thy comprehension), {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Theodotion LXX.Eze.28.3.}}
**II.4.d** _passive_, **to be outdone,** ὑπό τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 102c, 102d; τὴν δύναμιν τοῦ ὑπερέχειν καὶ ὑπερέχεσθαι [prev. author] “Prm.” 150d; κατὰ πλοῦτον ὑπερέχειν κατ᾽ ἀρετὴν δ᾽ ὑπερέχεσθαι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1281a7, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.805.}}
**II.5** in Logic, **have a wider extension,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Posteriora” 99a24, compare “Rh.” 1363b8 (_active_ and _passive_).}}
**II.6** ἐπὶ τοῖς ὑπερέχουσι δανεῖσαι to lend on the security of **excess value,** of a second mortgage, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 364.33 (Ephesus, 3rd c.BC).}}
**III** with _genitive_ _of things_, **rise above, be able to bear,** τῆς ἀντλίας [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 17; τῶν ἀναλωμάτων 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.80 (variant for{ὑπερεῖδον}).}}]
**IV** **have over,** ὑπερέχει he **has in hand,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Zenon Papyri du Caire 292.498, compare 790.25 (3rd c.BC); ὑπερέξομεν πρὸς τὸ διὰ χερός [prev. work]355.93 (3rd c.BC).}} —Cf. ὑπερίσχω.

5244:
** ὑπερήφᾰν-ος**, ον,
_Doric dialect_ **ὑπερά-** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.28, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 16.49}}:—mostly in bad sense, **overweening, arrogant,** {{LXX+8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 149, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus [same places], 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 405 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.196, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.9, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ κακιῶν ί” p.10 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, etc.; Κύριος ὑπερηφάνοις ἀντιτάσσεται LXX.Pro.3.34; ὑβρισταὶ καὶ ὑ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1390b33}}; -ώτεροι.. καὶ ἀλογιστότεροι{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1391a33; οἰκίαι -ώτεραι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 13.30.}} _adverb_, -νως ἔχειν bear oneself **proudly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 399b, “Tht.” 175b}}; ὑ. ζῶντες living **sumptuously, prodigally,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.152, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 691a; ὀψωνεῖν.. οὐχὶ μετρίως.., ἀλλ᾽ ὑ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 32.20}}; of a dish, ὑ. ὄζειν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 261.6}} (but also, **insolently, brutally,** μαστιγοῦν τινα probably in {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 80.4 (3rd c.BC)).}}—This sense appears in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus in the}}_participle_ ὑπερηφανέων (which see).
**2** rarely in good sense, **magnificent, splendid,** σοφία, ἔργον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 96a, “Smp.” 217e; ὑ. τι διαπραττομένη [prev. author] “Grg.” 511d; ποτήρια χρυσᾶ.., ὑπερήφανα 4th-3rd c.BC: Philippides Comicus 27; πράξεις ὑ. τὸ μέγεθος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Fabius Maximus” 26}}; **sublime,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 3}}. _adverb_ -νως, ἀγωνιζόμενος {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Agesilaus” 34.}}

5246:
** ὑπέρογκ-ος**, ον,
**of excessive bulk** or **size,** γενομένης τῆς κνήμης ὑ. **swelled to a great size,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.4.58; [μαστοὶ] οἱ ὑ. 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.88; πιμελὴς καὶ ὑ. 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista}}**tim.**15; δύναμις ὑ., opposed to ταπεινή, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.23; τὰ ὑ. τῶν βελῶν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Audibilibus” 802b34.}}
**2** **immoderate, excessive,** οὐσίαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 317c}}; τιμαί, εὐτυχίαι, etc., {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.820f, “Aem.” 34, etc.; φρόνημα [prev. author] “Luc.” 21}}; τὰ ὑ., opposed to τὰ ἐλλείποντα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 728e}}; of style, **ponderous, verbose,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.7a (but also ὑπέρογκα λαλεῖν}} talk 'big', {{NT|NT.Jude.16, cf. NT.2Pet.2.18)}}: generally, **exceedingly great,** πρᾶγμα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMort.” 23.2}}. _adverb_ **-κως**{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.103, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demetrius” 30}}: _neuter_ as _adverb_, ὑπέρογκον φρονεῖν {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “Protrepticus” 14.}}
**3** **difficult,** {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.13.2.}}

5247:
** ὑπεροχ-ή**, ἡ,
(ὑπερέχω {{Refs|11)}} **projection, prominence,** οὐ κνῖσα κρούει πινὸς ὑπεροχὰς ἄκρας; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Ephippus Comicus 3, compare “Geoponica” 9.10.4}}; αἱ ὑ. τῶν βουνῶν, τῶν ὀρῶν, their **prominent points,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.10.10, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.936a}}; **top** of an upright beam, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Athenaeus Mechanicus 17.4}}; ὑ. λιθοειδεῖς, of the mastoid processes of the skull, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Rufus Medicus “περὶ ὀνομασίας” 139}}; τὰς ὑ. αὐτῶν (i.e. τῶν μαστῶν) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.55}}: _absolutely_, **an eminence,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.104.3.}}
**2** **rising** of a star, ἀνατολὴν εἶναι.. ὑ. ἄστρου ὑπὲρ γῆς {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.200}}; **raising,** τῆς ἑαυτῶν κεφαλῆς {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 5.8.3.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **pre-eminence, superiority,** ὑπεροχῆς ἐπιθυμεῖ ἡ νεότης, ἡ δὲ νίκη ὑ. τις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1389a13; ἡ ἰσχὺς καὶ ἡ ὑ. [prev. author] “Pol.” 1297b18; τὴν ὑ. ἀπονέμειν τοῖς ἀρίστοις [prev. work] 1293b41}}; τὴν ὑ. τῆς πολιτείας λαμβάνειν **superiority in** the government, {{Refs|[prev. work] 1296a31}}; διὰ τὴν ὑ. τοῦ πλήθους because of **superiority** in multitude,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1293a4; ἡ ἐν τῷ ἐπιτηδεύματι ὑ. “IG” 22.3800: _plural_, πρὸς τὰς ὑ. οὕτω διακεῖσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.16; διαφέρεσθαι ἐν τῷ ποσῷ καὶ ταις ὑ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1323a35.}}
**II.2** like{ὑπερβολή}, **excess,** opposed to ἔλλειψις (defect), in many senses, as in Arithm., one of the ἀριθμοῦ ᾗ ἀριθμὸς πάθη, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] “Metaphorical” 1004b12, compare 3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ ἑλίκων” 11, “Aequil.” 1.2, 3rd c.AD: Diophantus Mathematicus 1.6, +others}}; ἐν ἴσῃ ὑ., of an arithmetical progression, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.AD(?): Pappus Mathematicus 76.21, +others, compare 4th c.BC: Archytas Tarentinus Philosophus 2, 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “in Ptolemaei Harmonica” p.266 W.}}; **excess,** of a sum of money, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 976.66 (Samos, 2nd c.BC)}}; in Physics, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 187a16, 189b10, “HA” 486b8, +others; διαφέρειν καθ᾽ ὑπεροχήν [prev. author] “PA” 644a17, +others; τάχος τὸ ὑ. [ἔχον] κινήσεως [prev. author] “Metaphorical” 1052b30; ἡ κατὰ τὴν ἀρετὴν ὑ. [prev. author] “EN” 1098a11, compare “Rh.” 1368a25}}; τῶν ἠθῶν (i.e. τοῦ Επικούρου) {{Refs|“IG” 22.1099.27 (Epist. Plotinae), compare 42(1).86.18 (Epid., probably)}}; φιλίας εἶδος τὸ καθ᾽ ὑ., where one **exceeds** the other in rank, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1158b12, compare 1161a20: _plural_, κατὰ πλούτων ὑπεροχάς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 711d; οἱ ἐν ὑπεροχαῖς εὐτυχημάτων ὄντες 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1295b14.}}
**II.3** alone, **supremacy, authority, dignity,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.64.1; τὴν Σελεύκου τοῦ βασιλέως ὑ. 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 187.4; δαιμόνων ὑ. “OGI” 383.75 (Nemrud Dagh, 1st c.BC)}}; οἱ ἐν ὑπεροχαῖς νεανίσκοι {{NT+2nd c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.41; οἱ ἐν ὑπεροχῇ ὄντες “PTeb.” 734.24 (2nd c.BC), NT.1Tim.2.2; ἀνὴρ ἐν ὑ. κείμενος LXX.2Mac.3.11.}}
**II.4** of language, **periphrasis. prolixity,** opposed to ἔλλειψις, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 283c.}}
**II.5** as a title, **Excellency,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Justinianus Imperator “Novellae” 25.5; ἡ ὑμετέρα Ὑ. “POxy.” 130.20 (6th c.AD).}}

5252:
** ὑπερφρον-έω**,
(ὑπέρφρων) **to be over-proud, have high thoughts,** μηδ᾽ ὑπερφρόνει {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1039, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.18.7; μὴ ὑ. παρ᾽ ὃ δεῖ φρονεῖν, ἀλλὰ φρονεῖν εἰς τὸ σωφρονεῖν NT.Rom.12.3}}: with _dative_ modi, **to be proud in** or **of** a thing, πλούτῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.199; τὸν λόγον, ᾧ ὑπερπεφρόνηκας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.104a.}}
**2** with _accusative_, **overlook, look down upon, despise,** ὑπερφρονήσας τὸν παρόντα δαίμονα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 825; τοὺς θεούς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 226; πέφυκε ἄνθρωπος τὸ.. θεραπεῦον ὑπερφρονεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.39; ἑτέρους Aristeas 122}}:—_passive_, **to be despised,** ὑπὸ τῶν εὐπραγούντων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.16.}}
**3** with _genitive_, **think slightly of,** δαιμόνων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1325; τῶν καθεστώτων νόμων 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1400; τοῦ ἐπιτηδεύματος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 258b.}}
**II** **surpass in knowledge,** ὑ. ἱστορίᾳ τὸν δῆμον (variant for{περιφρ-}) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.141: with _accusative_ _cognate_, πάντα ὑ.}} **excel in** all **wisdom,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιστολαί” 17.}}

5253:
** ὑπερῷον**,
_Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ **ὑπερ-ώϊον**, τό, **the upper part of the house,** where the women resided, παρθένος αἰδοίη ὑπερώϊον εἰσαναβᾶσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.514; εἰς ὑπερῷ᾽ ἀναβάς 16.184, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.362}}; ὑπερωϊόθεν φρεσὶ σύνθετο θέσπιν ἀοιδὴν.. Πηνελόπεια **from her chamber** she heard it, {{Refs|[prev. work] 328; approached by a κλῖμαξ, [prev. work] 330: so in later Gr.}}**upper chamber** or **story,** {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Acts.1.13, “Supp.epigram” 2.754 (Syria, 2nd c.AD), “POxy.” 2146.7 (3rd c.AD).}}
**2** **attic, garret,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1001, “Pl.” 811, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 17, “IG” 22.1638.27; ἄνωθ᾽ ἐξ ὑ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 698 (_anapaest meter_)}}; of **a spare room,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.14.}}

5254:
** ὑπέχω**,
_future_ ὑφέξω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 552, etc.:}}_aorist_ ὑπέσχον, _poetry_ also ὑπέσχεθον, _Aeolic dialect_ _infinitive_ ὐποσκέθην {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 12(2).526 A 40 (Eresus, 4th c.BC)}}:—**hold under,**
**a** **put** a mare **under** or **to** a horse, ὑποσχὼν θήλεας ἵππους {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.269.}}
**b** of **holding out** the hand **to receive** something, ὑπέσχεθε χεῖρα {{Refs|7.188}}; προτείνειν καὶ ὑ. [τὴν χεῖρα], to receive bribes, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.255; ὑ. χρυσίῳ τὴν χεῖρα 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 309}}: proverbial of a greedy person, ὑ. τὴν χεῖρα ἀποθνήσκων {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD: Diogenianus Paroemiographus 3.12.}}
**c** ῥημάτων ὑ. οὖας **lend** an attentive ear, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 37.14; σὺ δὲ μείλιχον οὖας ὑπόσχες 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Hymni” 7.52; ὑ. τὰ ὦτά τινι 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 2.230 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, etc.}}
**d** **hold** a cup **under** another vessel, while something is poured into it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.151, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1063, “Pax” 431, compare 909: also _intransitive_, ὑ. κρουνοῖς}} **stand under** shower baths, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Antyllus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 10.3.10.}}
**e** **put under,** like wax under a seal, τι ταῖς αἰσθήσεσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 191d.}}
**f** ὑ. μαστόν, of mother or nurse giving suck, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1372; νηπίοις θηλὴν ὑ. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Romulus” 21.}}
**2** **supply, afford, furnish,** νεφέλην {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 199.7 (ὑπερς χών Casaubon)}}; πλοῦτος ὑ. μέριμναν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.54; πάντα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 841}}; ὑ. τὸ αὐτὸ τοῖς ἐναντίοις (i.e. fear) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.21}}; ὑ. ἑαυτόν **submit** oneself to another, so as to be at his disposal or follow his advice, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.44, variant in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 399b: also with}}_infinitive_, ὑπόσχες Σωκράτει ἐξελέγξαι **allow** Socrates to examine you, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Grg.” 497b.}}
**II** **uphold, support,** τοὺς ὤμους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.72.}}
**II.2** **undergo, suffer,** τήνδ᾽ ἄτην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1274 (_anapaest meter_); ζημίαν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1308; κακόν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 196}}; τιμωρίαν τινός **for** a thing, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.81, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.221, 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 91, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 716b; πυρὸς αἰωνίου δίκην NT.Jude.7; κόλασιν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.198d, etc.}}; also τούτων τὰς αἰτίας **to be subject to** accusation for.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.67; τούτων.. οὐκ ἂν δικαίως τὴν αἰτίαν ὑπέχοιμι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 33b; ψόγον ἀμουσίας ὑ. [prev. author] “R.” 403c.}}
**II.3** in law-phrases, ὑ. δίκας [τινός] **to have to give** an account of a thing, or **suffer** a penalty, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.118; δίκην ὑπόσχες αἵματος.. Εὐμενίσι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1649; ὑ. φόνου δίκας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 872c (poetry also φόνον μητρὸς ὑφέξω}} in the same sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 1318 (_anapaest meter_)); δίκην ὑ. τῶν πεπραγμένων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.95; ὑφέξειν τὴν δίκην 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 552; ὑφέξω τοῖς κακίοσιν δίκην 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1253; ὑ. τῇ πόλει δίκην 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 99a}}; τοιάνδε δίκην ὑφέξειν **undergo** such a trial, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.53, compare “IG” 12(2) [same place]}}; τοῖς χρήμασι τὰς σίκας ὑ. **to have to pay** the penalty with one's property, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 20.17}}; ὑ. δίκας, ἐὰν.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.77; ὑ. κρίσιν περὶ ὧν ἄν τις ἐγκαλῇ [prev. author] 21.125:}} — ὑ. καὶ λαμβάνειν τὸ δίκαιον ἐπὶ τῶν χρηματιστῶν, i.e. to have the right of suing or being sued, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 5.213, +others (2nd c.BC)}}; λαμβάνω ἐγὼ καὶ ὑπέχω τὸ δίκαιον ὑπὲρ τοῦ Πολυκλείτου {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PEnteux.” 8.6 (3rd c.BC); ἐπαναγκάσαι αὐτὸν τὸ δίκαιον ἡμῖν ὑποσχεῖν [prev. work] 59.11 (3rd c.BC).}}
**II.3.b** ἐμοὶ λόγον ὑποσχέτω **let him render** account to me, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 338d; οὐδενὶ θέλων ὑπέχειν λόγον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.4.9; ὑ. τῇ πόλει περὶ τοῦ βίου λόγον 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.37; ὑ. εὐθύνας [ἀρχῆς] Lys.. 24.26, compare 9.11, 30.3.}}
**II.4** **sustain, maintain,** λόγον an argument, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1354a5, “Metaphorical” 1011a22, +others; ὑπόθεσιν [prev. author] “Top.” 158a31; θέσιν καὶ ὁρισμόν [prev. work] 160b14. (For the}}_middle_, see at {ὑπισχνέομαι}.)

5255:
** ὑπήκοος**, ον,
(ἀκοή) **hearkening,** ἀμφοτέροισι perhaps **answering** with both gifts, {{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.46 (Antip. Thess.)}}; **a hearer, scholar,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 4.44, 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de vita Pythagorica” 26.121.}}
**II** **obeying, subject,** with _genitive_, Πέρσας Μήδων ὑπηκόους ἐποίησε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.102, compare 4.167, 7.111, 149, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 234, 242 (both troch.), 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.78, 6.20, etc.; ὑ. τῶν νόμων 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1102a10.}}
**II.2** with _dative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 287, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.4.22; ἅπαντα τῷ πλουτεῖν ὑπήκοα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 146; γλῶττα ὑ. τῷ λογισμῷ 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.90b}}; also ναυσὶ καὶ οὐ φόρῳ ὑ. **liable to furnish**.. (compare ὑποτελής), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.57}}; τροφὴ ὑ. τῇ πέψει **easy of** digestion, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.661b}}; ὕλη ὑ. phlegm **easily brought up,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|7th c.AD: Stephanus Medicus “in Hp.” 1.181 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}
**III** _absolutely_ as _substantive_, ὑπήκοοι, οἱ, **subjects,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.1.36, etc.}}; ἡ ὑ. (i.e. χώρα) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 36.53}}; τὸ ὑ. ={οἱ ὑ}., τὸ ὑ. τῶν ξυμμάχων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.69, compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 37.25, etc.}}; **the subject allies** of Athens were called ὑπήκοοι, opposed to αὐτόνομοι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.57, compare 6.22, 8.2.}}

5256:
** ὑπηρετ-έω**,
_future_ **-ήσω**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor (see. below)}}, etc.: _pluperfect_ ὑπηρετήκειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.3.9}}:—properly **do service on board ship, as a rower** (compare ὑπηρέτης, ὑπηρεσία), {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 524.33,47 (Praesus, 3rd c.BC)}}:—_passive_, πλοῖον ὑπὸ δύο ἀνθρώπων ὑπηρετεῖσθαι δυνάμενον {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 2.55.}}
**II** **to be a servant, do service,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 996, “Ph.” 990}}; opposed to ἄρχω, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 518 (troch.); τοὺς διὰ φόβον ὑ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 1.38.}}
**II.2** with _dative_, **minister to, serve,** τῷ παρόντι δαίμονι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1306, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1708, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.108, etc.}}; ὑ. τῷ χρηστηρίῳ **submit to** its ruling, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.41, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 914a; ἔργοις ἀνοσίοις ὑ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 283}}; [νόμῳ, λόγῳ], {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.19}}; ὑ. τοῖς τρόποις **humour** his ways, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1432}}; τῷδ᾽ ὑ. λόγῳ **second, support** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 588; ὢν ἄνθρωπος ἀνθρώπου τύχαις ὑπηρετήσω 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 150.}}
**II.3** ὑ. τινὶ εἴς or πρός τι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.109, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 8.7, etc.}}; also ὑ. τινί τι **serve** one in a thing, οἷς σὺ ταῦθ᾽ ὑπηρετεῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1024, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 979, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 196c, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.1.20, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.138, 59.35.}}
**II.3.b** in financial sense, τὸν δὲ ταμίαν εἰς τὸ ἀνάλωμα ὑπηρετῆσαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Supp.epigram” 1.351.30 (Samos, 4th c.BC), compare 363.43 ([prev. passage], 3rd c.BC), “Inscription Prien.” 3 (4th c.BC), 18 (3rd c.BC), +others}}
**II.3.c** at Athens, **serve as** ὑπηρέτης of the Council, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.70.}}
**II.3.d** in the Mithraic cult, **to be a** ὑπηρέτης or **servitor,** {{Refs|Rev.Hist.Rel. 109.63 (from Rome).}}
**II.3.e** **render military service**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 180.14, 613.23 (both 2nd c.AD).}}
**II.4** _absolutely_, **serve, be subordinate,** opposed to προστάττω, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 129a13; ἡ ὑπηρετοῦσα ἐπιστήμη [prev. author] “Metaphorical” 982b5: with _neuter_ _plural_ of}} _adjective_, etc., τὰ λοίφ᾽ ὑ. **help in** what remains to be done, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 15; ὑ. τὰ περὶ τὸν πόλεμον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 467a}}; and with _cognate_ _accusative_, ὑ. τὰς διακονικὰς πράξεις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1277a36}}:—_passive_, **to be done as service,** τὰ ἀπ᾽ ἡμέων ἐς ὑμέας ὑπηρετέεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.139}}; χρὴ σὴ τό γε ἐμὸν ὑπηρετέεσθαι **that** my **service should be rendered,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.108; ἢν τὰ παρ᾽ ὑμῶν ὑπηρετῆται 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.63; τὸ πρᾶγμα τὸ ὑπηρετηθέν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Eudemia” 1243a16, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.2.34.}}—The _middle_ occurs in late texts, as {{Refs 1st c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 7.19, +others, and 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 1.11, uncertain in “Supp.epigram” 1.327.19 (Callatis, 1st c.AD)}}; τὸ -ούμενον the **retinue,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Memnon Historicus 2.4}}; _future_ **-ήσομαι**{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 58.24 (3rd c.AD)}}; but in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1306, ὑπηρετοίην was rightly restored by Musgrave and Elmsl. for -οίμην.}}

5257:
** ὑπηρέτ-ης**, ου, ὁ,
(ἐρέτης) _Doric dialect_ **ὑπηρέτας** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 42(1).122.40,112 (Epid., 4th c.BC), “SIG” 1000.31 (Cos, 1st c.BC)}}:— **rower,** τοὶ ὑ. τᾶν μακρᾶν ναῶν {{Refs|“SIG” [prev. cited] (uncertain meaning).}}
**II** **underling, servant, attendant,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.63, 5.111; δοῦλοι καὶ πάντες ὑ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 289c}}; ὑ. [τῆς πόλεως], opposed to ἄρχων, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “R.” 552b; ἡ πόλις εἰς ὑπηρέτου σχῆμα.. προελήλυθεν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.210}}; τῶν ἰατρῶν, τῶν δικαστῶν ὑ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 720a, 873b}}; used in Trag. and _Attic dialect_ to express all kinds of **subordinate** relations, as Hermes is ὑ. θεῶν, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 954, compare 983}}; the Delphians are Φοίβου ὑπηρέται, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 712}}; Neoptolemus is ὑ. to Odysseus, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 53; the αὐλός is ὑ. to the Chorus, Pratin.Lyric poetry 1.7}}: sometimes with _dative_, τῷ θεῷ ὑ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 773e}}; τοῖς νόμοις{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 715c, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1287a21; also οἱ περὶ τυράννους καὶ πόλεις ὑ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 426}}; opposed to οἰκέτης, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.10.3: with _genitive_ objecti, ὑ. παντὸς ἔργου}} **a helper in** every work, {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 1.9.18.}}
**II.2** at Athens,
**II.2.a** **the servant who attended each man-at-arms** (ὁπλίτης) to carry his baggage, rations, and shield, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.17}}; sometimes light-armed as slingers or bowmen, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1186.}}
**II.2.b** ὁ τῶν ἕνδεκα ὑ. the **assistant** of the Eleven, employed in executions of state-criminals, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 116b, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.3.54, 2.4.8.}}
**II.2.c** a **petty officer** of the Council, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 12.879 (_plural_), “Hesperia” 3.63 (4th c.BC).}}
**II.3** _plural_, **staff-officers in immediate attendance on the general, aides-de-camp, adjutants,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.4.4, 6.2.13, etc.: _sing._, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 50.31}}; also, **officer attached to** τάξις, σύνταγμα, or ἑκατονταρχία, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Asclepiodotus Tacticus “Tact.” 2.9, 6.3, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Tactica - Griechische Kriegsschriftsteller” 9.4, 16.2, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Tactica” 10.4, 14.4.}}
**II.4** **servitor** in the cult of Mithras, {{Refs|“Rev.Hist.Rel.” 109.64 (from Rome).}}

5258:
** ὕπνος**, ὁ,
**sleep, slumber,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.245, +others (see. below)}}; of **the sleep of death,** κοιμήσατο χάλκεον ὕ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.241; Κάλχανθ᾽ ὕ. θανάτοιο κάλυψεν 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Fragmenta” 160 codices 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus; ὕπνῳ καὶ καμάτῳ ἀρημένος 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.2, compare 12.281; τάπητες μαλακώτεροι ὕπνω 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.125, compare 5.51.}} — Special phrases:
**I** of going to **sleep,** μιν ἐπήλυθε.. ὕ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.793; ἐπὶ.. ὕ. ὄρουσεν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.232; ἱκάνει 1.610; ἔχει 10.4, etc.; τὸν ὕ. ἔμαρπτε 23.62, +others; ᾕρει 24.4, +others; λαμβάνει 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 767; opposed to ὕπνος ἀνῆκέ τινα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.71, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.551, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 310d: of persons, ὕπνον ἀωτεῖν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.159, etc.; ὕπνου δῶρον ἕλοντο 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.481, etc.; λαβεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 223b; κοιμᾶσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 6.7; ἡδὺν ὕπνον καθεύδειν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Kith.fragment” 1.5; ὕπνου τυχεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 713; μικρὸν ὕπνου λαχών 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.1.11; ὕπνου λαχεῖν μέρος 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 218}}; ἐν ὕπνῳ or ὕπνῳ πεσεῖν to fall a-**sleep,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 4(3).23(41), 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 68; εἰς ὕπνον πεσεῖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 826}}; οὐχ ὕπνῳ γ᾽ ἐνδόντα (so Badham for εὕδοντά) μ᾽ ἐξεγείρετε {{Refs|[prev. author] “OT” 65}}; also ὕπνῳ δεδμημένος, δαμείς, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.2, 14.353, etc.}}; νικώμενος, κρατηθεῖσ᾽, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 290, “Eu.” 148 (Lyric poetry); κάτοχος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 978 (Lyric poetry); σκεδάσαι.. ἀπὸ βλεφάρων ὕπνον [prev. work] 991 (Lyric poetry).}}
**I.2** of waking from sleep, ἐγεῖραί τινα ἐξ ὕπνου {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.44, etc.}}: of the sleeper, ἀνόρουσε, ἔγρετο, ἐξ ὕ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.162, 2.41; ἐξ ὕ. στῆναι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 277; ἀπολακτίσασ᾽ ὕπνον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 141; ἀποσείσασθαι 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 6.}}
**I.3** with Preps., when the _plural_ also is not uncommon, ἐν ὕπνῳ in **sleep,** in **a dream,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 44, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 476c}}; ἐν τοῖς ὕ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 572b, “Sph.” 266b, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.21, “PCair.Zen.” 34.5 (3rd c.BC)}}; καθ᾽ ὕπνον ὄντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 970 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 800a}}; καθ᾽ ὕπνον, κατὰ τοὺς ὕπνους, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.717e,555b, “Alex.” 50}}; περὶ πρῶτον ὕ. about one's first **sleep,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 31, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.2; περὶ πρώτους ὕ. 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 13; ἀπὸ πρώτου ὕ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.43; διὰ μέσων τῶν ὕ. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Themistocles” 28; ἐκ τῶν ὕ. ἐγείρεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 330e}}: _plural_, **dreams,** ὕ. ἀγένητοι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 1.22.}}
**II** **Sleep,** as a god, twinbrother of Death, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.231, 16.672, 682; _accusative_ to 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 212}}, son of Night without father. [ῠ by nature, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 3, “Ag.” 14, 912, etc.}}; ῡ by position in _Epic dialect_, etc.] (Cf. Sanskrit _svápati_ 'sleep', _substantive_ **svápnas** 'sleep, dream'; Latin _somnus, sopor,_ etc.)

5259:
** ὑπό** [ῠ],
_preposition_ with _genitive_, _dative_, and _accusative_: _Aeolic dialect_ **ὐπά** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 39}}; _Boeotian dialect_ **ὑπά** Ἀρχ.Δελτ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|14 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus ii 19 (Thespiae, 3rd c.BC)}}; _Ionic dialect_ ηυπύ only in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 14.871 (Cumae, 5th c.BC); 4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus}} **ὁπύ** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Schwyzer” 664.15,21 (Orchom. 4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus, 4th c.BC)}}; in _Epic dialect_ **ὑπαί** (also {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 12.139)}}: this is found in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} only six times as a well-attested reading (ὑ. πόδα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.824, ὑ. δέ 3.217, 11.417, 12.149, ὑ. δείους 10.376, 15.4)}}; elsewhere (before λ ν ρ ϝ) it is weakly attested as see l. for ὑπὸ (?~X ¯), e.g. ποσσὶ δ᾽ ὑπὸ (variant{ὑπαὶ}) λιπαροῖσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.44, +others}}; but ὑπαὶ νεφέων is given by most codices in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.625, 16.375 (see. Allen ed. maj.), and ὑπαὶ νεφέεσσι Anonymus cited in 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.38e; also in compounds, ὑπαιδείδοικα “hymnus ad Mercurium” 165, ὑπαιφοινίσσω (which see)}}; it is not frequently in Trag. Poets, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 892, 944, 1164 (Lyric poetry), “Eu.” 417, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 711, 1418 (Lyric poetry), “Inach.” in “PTeb.” 692 ii5 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 1188 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 970 (_paratragoedia_). (With ὑπό}} (ὕπο) cf. Sanskrit _úpa_ 'towards, near to, etc.', Gothic _uf_ 'under'.)
**A** WITH GENITIVE,
**A.I** of Place, with Verbs of motion, **from under,** αὖτις ἀναστήσονται ὑ. ζόφου they will rise again **from under** the gloom, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.56; ὑ. χθονὸς ἧκε φόωσδε 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 669; ῥέει κρήνη ὑ. σπείους 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.141, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 230b; ὄσσε δεινὸν ὑ. βλεφάρων ἐξεφάανθεν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.17}}; ἐσιδόντες ὑπαὶ χειμῶνος αἴγλαν **from under** the storm-cloud, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 12.139}}; especially of rescuing **from under** another's power, after the Verbs ἐρύεσθαι, ἁρπάζειν, ῥύεσθαι, ἐρύειν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.248, 13.198, 17.224, 235; ἤγαγεν ὑμέτερόνδ᾽ ἀνδροκτασίης ὕ. λυγρῆς}}**from the consequences of,** {{Refs|23.86}}; also ἵππους μὲν λῦσαν ὑ. ζυγοῦ **from under** the yoke,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8.543, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.39}}; ὑπ᾽ ἀρνειοῦ λυόμην I loosed myself **from under** the ram, {{Refs|9.463}}; σπλάγχνων ὕπο ματέρος μόλεν, i.e. was born, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.35, compare “O.” 6.43}}; rarely in Trag., ὑ. πτερῶν σπάσας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 441; περᾷ γὰρ ἥδ᾽ ὑ. σκηνῆς πόδα [prev. author] “Hec.” 53; once in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, τὰς δέ οἱ ἵππους ὑ. τοῦ ἅρματος νεμομένας ἀφανισθῆναι 4.8; αἴ τις ὑ. τῶν νομίων τῶν ἐπιϝοικων ἀνχωρέῃ “SIG” 47.27 (Locris, 5th c.BC)}}; compare ὑπέκ.
**A.I.2** of the object **under** which a thing is or is placed, **under, beneath,** with collateral sense of motion, as μοχλὸν ὑ. σποδοῦ ἤλασα πολλῆς thrust it **in under** the embers, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.375; ὑ. στέρνοιο τυχήσας 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.106; τοὺς μὲν ὑ. χθονὸς εὐρυοδείης πέμψαν 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 717}}: also without the sense of motion, ὑπ᾽ ἀνθερεῶνος ὀχεὺς τέτατο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.372; βάθιστον ὑ. χθονός ἐστι βέρεθρον 8.14; ἐτέθαπτο ὑ. χθονός 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.52; κεκευθὼς πολεμίας ὑ. χθονός 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 588; ὑπ᾽ ἀγκῶνος βέλη 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.83; νέρθεν ὑπ᾽ ἐγκεφάλοιο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.347; τὰ ὑ. γῆς δικαιωτήρια 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 249a; δεξιὰν ὑφ᾽ εἵματος κρύπτειν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 342; φέρειν ζώνης ὕπο [prev. work] 762: Thom.Mag. p.375 R. says that ὑ.}} = **under** takes _genitive_ in _Attic dialect_, _accusative_ in 'Hellenic' Greek; κατακρύψας ὑ. κόπρου, which is variant in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.329 for ὑ. κόπρῳ}} is called by {{Refs|12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1631.36 Ἀττικώτερον, ὁποῖον καὶ τὸ φέρειν τι ὑ. κόλπου ἢ ὑ. μάλης (see. κόλπος, μάλη)}}; but in _Attic dialect_ Prose, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, and the Koine ὑ. with _genitive_ in significations 1.1, 2}} is almost limited to these and a few other phrases, especially ὑ. γῆ; it is not found at all in {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus, LXX}}, Ptolemaic papyri, and **NT;** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus has ὑ. ἁμάξης}} (= **from under**) {{Refs|“An.” 6.4.22, 25}}; the Orators have only ὑ. μάλης, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator “Fragmenta” 54, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 29.12}}; ὑ. γῆς is found in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 18b, “Mx.” 246d, “R.” 414d, +others, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 352b6, +others, 2nd c.BC: Hipparchus Astronomicus 2.2.45, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.18.10}} (ὑ. τῆς γῆς), {{Refs|21.28.3, 10.}}
**A.I.2.b** _metaphorically_, ἀρετῶσι δὲ λαοὶ ὑπ᾽ αὐτοῦ **under** his rule, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.114.}}
**A.II** of Cause or Agency, frequently with _passive_ Verbs, and with _intransitive_ Verbs in _passive_ sense, μή πως τάχ᾽ ὑπ᾽ αὐτοῦ δουρὶ δαμήῃς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.436, compare 4.479; ἡνιόχοιο ἐν κονίῃσι πεσόντος ὑφ᾽ Ἕκτορος 17.428}}; εὖτ᾽ ἂν πολλοὶ ὑφ᾽ Ἕκτορος θνῂσκοντες πίπτωσι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|1.242; τὸν.. τοκέα ὑ. τοῦ.. παιδὸς ἀποθνῄσκειν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.137; οἵαις ὑπ᾽ αὐτοῦ πημοναῖσι κάμπτομαι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 308, compare “Th.” 7, +others; πέλεκυς.. ὅς τ᾽ εἶσιν διὰ δουρὸς ὑπ᾽ ἀνέρος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.61; ὑπ᾽ Ἀχαιῶν.. φοβέοντο.. ἀπὸ νηῶν 16.303; πάσχειν δὲ κακῶς ἐχθρὸν ὑπ᾽ ἐχθρῶν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1042 (_anapaest meter_); ὑ. τοῦ Μήδου δεινότερα τούτων πάσχοντες 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.77; ἐκπεσόντες ὑ. τοῦ πλήθους [prev. author] 4.66; ἀναστάτων Καμαριναίων γενομένων ὑ. Συρακοσίων [prev. author] 6.5; ὑφ᾽ ὑμῶν αὐτῶν καὶ μὴ ὑ. τῶν πολεμίων τοῦτο παθεῖν [prev. author] 4.64}}; κλύοντές ἐσμεν αἰσχίστους λόγους.. τοῦδ᾽ ὑπ᾽ ἀνδρὸς ἀρτίως we have been called shameful names **by**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1321}}; κακῶς ὑ. τῶν πολιτῶν ἀκούειν to be ill spoken of **by**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.77, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 304e, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.7.23}}; of a subordinate agent, ὑ. κήρυκος προαγορεύειν, ἀπειπεῖν κηρύκων ὕπο, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.98, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 737, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.32; ἐμῶν ὑπ᾽ ἀγγέλων.. πορεύεται 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 391; ὑ. ἀγγέλων πέμπων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 66a: sometimes with a verbal}} _substantive_, τὸ ὑ. νόμου ἐπίταγμα (i. e. ἐπιταττόμενον){{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “R.” 359a; ἐκφορὰ φίλων ὕπο 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 1029; ἡ ὑπ᾽ ἀρετῆς Ἡρακλέους παίδευσις 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.34; ἡ ὑ. πάντων τιμή [prev. author] “Cyr.” 3.3.2; Ἥρας δεσμοὶ ὑ. ὑέος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 378d}}; so ἄτρωτον ἦν ὑ. στύγους (={οὐ τετρωμένον}) probably in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 532.}}
**A.II.2** also in pregnant phrases, not only of the immediate act of the agent, but also of its further result, ὅθ᾽ ὑ. λιγέων ἀνέμων σπέρχωσιν ἄελλαι hasten driven on **by** them, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.334}}; ὑφ᾽ Ἕκτορος.. φεύγοντες fleeing **before** him,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|18.149, χάσσονται ὑπ᾽ ἔγχεος 13.153, compare 7.64, 11.119, 424, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.320, 7.263, +others; πράγματα εἶχον ὑ. λῃστῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.1.5}}; ἔπαινον, αἰτίαν ἔχειν ὑ. τινῶν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.78, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 99; οὐκέτι ἀποχωρεῖν οἷόν τ᾽ ἦν ὑ. τῶν ἱππέων 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.78, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1084.}}
**A.II.3** frequently of things as well as persons, ὡς διάκειμαι ὑ. τῆς νόσου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.77; κεῖμαι νούσου ὕ. στυγερᾶς “IG” 42(1).125.8 (Epid., 3rd c.BC)}}; χαλεπῶς ἔχειν ὑ. τραυμάτων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 142b; ὑ. δόρατος πλαγείς “IG” 42(1).122.64 (Epid., 4th c.BC); ὑ. ἔχιος φῦμα [prev. work]123.4 ([prev. passage], 4th c.BC); ἰάθη ὑ. ὄφιος [prev. work]121.113 ([prev. passage], 4th c.BC)}}; κατεσκεύασαν τὰς πύλας κλείεσθαι ὑ. σφύρας τε μεγάλης καὶ κτύπου παμμεγέθους γιγνομένου {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 20.4}}: of the agency of feelings, passions, etc., ἀνόρουσ᾽ ὑ. χάρματος {{Refs|“hymnus ad Cererem” 371}}; ἐνδακρύειν, ἀνολολύξαι χαρᾶς ὕπο, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 541, 587; μαίνεται.. ὑφ᾽ ἡδονῆς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1153; χλωρὸς ὑπαὶ δείους 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.376; ὑ. δέους ἔρρηξε φωνήν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.85, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.33; οὐ δυνατὸν τὸν δῆμον ἐσόμενον ὑ. τῶν κακῶν καρτερεῖν [prev. author] 4.66; ὑ. κακοῦ ἀγρυπνίῃσι εἴχετο 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.129; ὑπ᾽ ἄλγους 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 183; ὑπ᾽ ὀργῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1083; ὑ. λύπης 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1073: hence ὑπό is used even with active Verbs, where a passive word may be supplied, e.g. ὑ. ἀρετῆς καὶ προθυμίης συνεπλήρουν τὰς νέας}} **from** courage, i. e. impelled **by** courage, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.1; ὤρυσσον ὑ. μαστίγων [prev. author] 7.22, compare 56}}; οὐδὲ σέ γε δόλος ἔσχ᾽ ὑ. χειρὸς ἐμᾶς **by** my agency, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1118 (Lyric poetry)}}; αἰ μήτις αὐτὸς δοίη, μὴ ὑπ᾽ ἀνάγκας not **under** compulsion, {{Refs|“GDI” 5128.5 (from Vaxos).}}
**A.II.4** ὑπό frequently serves to denote the attendant or accompanying circumstances, νέφος ἐρχόμενον κατὰ πόντον ὑ. Ζεφύροιο ἰωῆς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.276, compare 16.591, etc.: sometimes with}}_participle_ added, ἀμφὶ δὲ νῆες σμερδαλέον κονάβησαν ἀϋσάντων ὑπ᾽ Ἀχαιῶν **at** their shouting, i.e. when they shouted, {{Refs|2.334, 16.277; ἴαχε σάλπιγξ ἄστυ περιπλομένων δηΐων ὕ. 18.220.}}
**A.II.5** of **accompanying** music, to give the time, κώμαζον ὑπ᾽ αὐλοῦ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 281, compare 278; ᾄδων ὑπ᾽ αὐλητῆρος 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 123, compare 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 825, 5th c.BC: Charon Historicus “fragment” 9; πίνειν ὑ. σάλπιγγος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1001}}: generally, of anything **attendant,** δαΐδων ὕ. λαμπομενάων ἠγίνεον **by** torchlight, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.492, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 639 (Lyric poetry), “Ion” 1474 (Lyric poetry); καταθάψομεν.. ὑ. κλαυθμῶν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1554 (_anapaest meter_); ὑπ᾽ εὐκλείας θανεῖν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1299; εἴσειμ᾽ ὑπαὶ πτερύγων κιχλᾶν καὶ κοψίχων 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 970}}; ὑπ᾽ εὐφήμου βοῆς θῦσαι offer a sacrifice **accompanied by** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 630}}; ὑ. φανοῦ πορεύεσθαι **by** lantern-light, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 5.7}}; ὑ. πομπῆς ἐξάγειν τινά **in** or **with** solemn procession, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.45, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 1030}}; ὑ. βίης βήξας coughing **with** violence, violently, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.107}}; ἐτόξευον ὑ. μαστίγων, i.e. they shot and lashed, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.4.25: see below 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 11.4, C. IV. 1.}}
**A.II.6** ὑ. Ἑλλανοδικᾶν, ={ἐπί} with _genitive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 171 (Olympia, 4th c.BC).}}
**A.II.7** in Mathematics texts, ἡ ὑ. ΘΔΗ **the angle** ΘΔΗ (={ἡ ὑ. τῶν ΘΔ, ΔΗ περιεχομένη γωνία}), {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Hypotyposis astronomicarum positionum” 2.26}}; but also τὸ ὑ. τῶν ΑΓ, ΓΒ **the rectangle contained by** ΑΓ, ΓΒ, ={ΑΓ χ ΓΒ}, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 2.4.}}
**A.II.8** ναῦλον ὄνων γ εἰς τὴν πόλιν ὑ. οἴνου **laden with** wine, Papyrus in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Hermes” 28.163 (2nd c.AD), compare [prev. work]479, and infr. C. IV. 2.}}
**B** WITH DATIVE (especially in Poets, never in {{LXX|LXX (LXX.Job.12.5 is uncertain reading)}} or **NT,** not common in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus, Ptolemaic papyri, or 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus)}}, of Position **under,** ὑ. ποσσί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.784, +others; ὑ. πλατανίστῳ [prev. work] 307, compare 18.558}}; ὑ. Τμώλῳ **at** its **foot,**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|2.866, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.186; Βερύσιοι ὑ. τῇ Ἴδῃ “IG” 12.191.11, compare 373.118, +others; ὑ. τῇ ἀκροπόλι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.105}}; τῶν θανόντων ὑπ᾽ Ἰλίῳ **under** its walls, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 764, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 860; πέτρῃ ὕ. γλαφυρῇ εὗδον, Βορέω ὑπ᾽ ἰωγῇ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.533; ὑ. τοῖς ὄρεσιν ἔχειν τὰς πηγάς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 350b27; ὑ. πέτρᾳ παῖς “IG” 42(1).122.19 (Epid., 4th c.BC); ὑ. τῷ ναῷ ἀστραγαλίζοντος αὐτοῦ [prev. work]121.25 ([prev. passage], 4th c.BC)}}; ηυπὺ τῇ κλίνῃ τούτῃ ληνὸς (or Λῆνος) ηύπυ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work]14.871 (Cumae, 5th c.BC)}}; στρουθοὶ ὑ. τῇ τραπέζῃ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Michel” 832.33 (Samos, 4th c.BC)}}; ὑ. τῇ μασχάλῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 11; χέλυν δ᾽ ὑ. μασχάλῃ εἶχεν “hymnus ad Mercurium” 242; ὑ. ταῖς μασχάλαις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 688b5, 14}}; ὁ ὑ. τῇ γῇ ἀήρ **under** the earth, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cael.” 295a28}}; ἐὰν ὑ. σοὶ κατακλινῇ lies **next below** you, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 222e}}; ὑφ᾽ ἅρμασι **under,** i.e. **yoked to,** the chariot, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.402, 18.244; εἶχε μάχαιραν ὑφ᾽ αὑτῷ παρεσκευασμένος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 8.20.6 codices, compare “POxy.” 1800 “fragment” 2.36 (“Vit.Aesop.”); ὑ. τοῖς χιτωνίσκοις περιζώματα φοροῦσιν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 12.26a. 4, compare 13.7.9}}; τά τε θηρία καὶ τὰς ὑπ᾽ αὐτοῖς σχεδίας **under** them, on which they stood, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.46.8; τῆς γῆς τῆς ὑ. τῷ κόσμῳ κειμένης 4th-3rd c.BC: Timaeus Historicus cited in [same work] 12.25.7; οἱ ὑ. τῇ ἄρκτῳ, τῇ μεσημβρία, οἰκοῦντες 4th c.AD: Adamantius Physiognomonicus 2.31, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 940a37, “Phgn.” 806b16; ὑ. τῷ μετώπῳ ὀφρύες [prev. author] “HA” 491b14; ὑ. τῷ γενείῳ 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 34.10.9; τὰ ὑ. τοῖς ὕδασι καὶ ὑμέσι καὶ ὑέλοις 2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus}} “*Deff.” 135.12; ὑ. τῷ δέρματι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(2).102.}}
**B.2** with Verbs of motion, where rest or position follows, εἷσαν ὑ. φηγῷ set [him] **down under** it, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.693; ἔζευξαν ὑφ᾽ ἅρμασιν.. ἵππους 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.478, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.782; ὑ. δ᾽ ἄξοσι.. ἔπιπτον 16.378, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.1.37; δέμνι᾽ ὑπ᾽ αἰθούσῃ θέμεναι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.644.}}
**B.3** in such phrases as ὑ. χερσί τινος ἁλῶναι, δαμῆναι, {{Refs|2.374, 860, +others}}; ἐμῇς ὑ. χερσὶ δάμασσον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|3.352; ὑ. δουρὶ δαμῆναι 5.653, etc.; ἔκπεσον ἵππων Ἀτρεΐδεω ὑ. χερσί 11.180; ὤλετο.. ὑ. γαμφηλῇσι λέοντος 16.489; πέπληγμαι δ᾽ ὑπαὶ δάκει φοινίῳ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1164 (Lyric poetry); ἐν κονίῃσι πέσοιεν ὑπ᾽ ἀνδράσι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.453; ὑ. τινὶ κτείνεσθαι 16.490.}}
**B.4** **behind,** ὑ. φάλαγγι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Asclepiodotus Tacticus “Tact.” 6.1}}; **under the cover** or **protection of,** ὑ. τούτῳ τῷ φράγματι τοὺς ὑπορύσσοντας εἶναι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 37.9; ὑ. ταῖς αὑτῶν ἀσφαλείαις 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.57.8, 4.12.10, 16.6.1.}}
**B.II** of the person **under whose hand, power,** or **influence,** or the thing **by** or **through which** a thing is done, ὑπ᾽ Ἀργείοισι φέβοντο fled **before** them, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.121; frequently in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus with _intransitive_ or _passive_ Verbs, ἐφόβηθεν ὑφ᾽ Ἕκτορι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.637; ὁρμηθέντες ὑ. πληγῇσιν ἱμάσθλης 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.82; βῆ.. θεῶν ὑ. πομπῇ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.171; ὦρτο δὲ κῦμα πνοιῇ ὕπο 23.215; ὑ. λαίλαπι βέβριθε χθών 16.384; τεκεῖν, τεκέσθαι ὑ. τινί, 2.714, 728, 742}}; ἀτῆθαι ὑ. τῷ μεμφομένῳ {{Refs|“GDI” 4994.8 (from Crete)}}; ὁ χρησμὸς ὁ γεγονὼς ὑ. τοῖ Ἀπόλλωνι {{Refs|“Inscription Magn.” 38.5, compare 12,31,52.}}
**B.II.2** expressing **subjection** or **dependence,** ὑ. τινί **under** one's **power,** δέδμητο δὲ λαὸς ὑπ᾽ αὐτῷ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.305, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.156; ὑπ᾽ ἀνδράσιν οἶκον ἔχουσιν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.68}}; εἶναι ὑ. τισί to be **subordinate, subject to** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.32}}; ὑ. Χείρωνι τεθραμμένος **under the eye of**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 391c}}; ἔχειν ὑφ᾽ ἑαυτῷ have **under** one, **at** one's **command,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.26; τὰ θηρία τὰ ὑ. τοῖς ἀνθρώποις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 563c; ὑ. τινὶ στρατεύσασθαι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cicero” 44}}: in pregnant sense, ἵνα.. πάντα ὑ. Πέρσῃσι γένηται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.11, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.64; ὑπ᾽ ἑωυτῷ ποιήσασθαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.157; κινδυνεύσαιμ᾽ ἂν ὑ. τῇ δυσχερεστάτῃ γενέσθαι τύχῃ 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 24.6; ὑ. τῷ Μακεδόνι ταττομένων 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.11.4; τοὺς τραφέντας ὑ. τούτοις [prev. author] 6.7.2.}}
**B.II.3** of the subordination of things coming **under** a class, αἱ ὑ. ταῖς τέχναις ἐργασίαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 205c; τὸ ὑ. ταῖς γεωμετρίαις [prev. author] “R.” 511b; ὄργανα.. τὰ ὑ. τῇ μουσικῇ [prev. author] “Hp.Ma.” 295d.}}
**B.II.4** as in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 11.5, ὑπ᾽ αὐλητῆρι πρόσθ᾽ ἔκιον}} advanced **to the music of** the flute-player, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 283}}; ὑπ᾽ αὐλῷ, ὑ. κήρυκι καὶ θεολόγῳ, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DDeor.” 2.2, “Alex.” 19; ὑ. μάστιξι διορύττειν τὸν Ἄθω 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.470e}}: generally, of attendant circumstances, ἐξ ἁλὸς εἶσι.. πνοιῇ ὕπο Ζεφύροιο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.402}}; ὑ. ῥάβδοις καὶ πελέκεσι κατιών **escorted by** the lictors, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Publicola” 10}}; ὑ. σκότῳ, νυκτί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1030 (Lyric poetry), 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.1022, etc.; λάμπει δ᾽ ὑ. μαρμαρυγαῖς ὁ χρυσός 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 3.17; αἰθομένα δᾲς ὑ. ξανθαἵσι πεύκαις 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 79; ὑ. φωτὶ πολλῷ προσῄει 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Galba” 14; ὑ. λαμπάσιν ἡμμέναις 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 10.41}}; ὑ. πολλῷ στρατῷ **escorted by** a great host, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Nicolaus Damascenus Historicus 10J.; ὑ. δικαιοσύνῃ διαγαγεῖν τὸν βίον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 335d.}} — ὑπό has no sense with _dative_ which it has not also with _genitive_; but all its senses with _genitive_ do not belong to the _dative_:—later ὑπό with _dative_ is found as a mere _periphrastic_ of the _dative_, στέφος.. αὐτὸς ὑφ᾽ ἡμετέραις πλεξάμενος παλάμαις {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 5.73 (2nd-3rd c.AD: Rufinus Epigrammaticus), compare 85 (4th-5th c.AD: Claudianus Epigrammaticus); λέων ὑπ᾽ ἄκοντι τετυμμένος 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.26, compare 4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 2.131.}}
**C** WITH ACCUSATIVE, of Place; to express motion **towards and under** an object, ὑ. σπέος ἤλασε μῆλα drove them **under,** i.e. **into,** the cave, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.279; ὑ. ζυγὸν ἤγαγεν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.383}}; σεῦ ὕστερος εἶμ᾽ ὑ. γαῖαν, i.e. shall die, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.333; νέεσθαι ὑ. ζόφον 23.51, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.335}}; κατακρύπτειν τινὰ ὑ. τὴν αὐτὴν θύρην **under shelter** of it, i.e. **behind** it, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.12; πάϊς ὣς ὑ. μητέρα δύσκεν εἰς Αἴαντα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.271; ὅκως ἔωσι ὑ. τὸν πεζὸν στρατὸν τὸν σφέτερον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.96; ὑ. τὸν πρῶτον λόχον τῶν ὁπλιτῶν τὸν πρῶτον λόχον τῶν ψιλῶν τετάχθαι 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Tactica - Griechische Kriegsschriftsteller” 15.2}}; of coming **close up under** a lofty citadel, ἤλθεθ᾽ ὑ. Τροίην **up to** T., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.146; ὅτ᾽ ἔμελλεν ὑ. πτόλιν αἰπύ τε τεῖχος ἵξεσθαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.181; παυρότερον λαὸν ἀγαγόνθ᾽ ὑ. τεῖχος ἄρειον 4.407; ὑ. τὰ τείχη φεύγειν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.74.11; ὑ. τὰς ἴλας φεύγειν [prev. author] 3.65.7, compare 3.105.6, 11.21.5, +others}}; ὑ. ταὐτὸ στέγος εἰσελθεῖν {{Refs|“GDI” 3536 B 3 (from Cnidus)}}; πᾶν ὃ ἐὰν ἔλθῃ.. ὑ. τὴν ῥάβδον {{LXX|LXX.Lev.27.32, cf. LXX.Deu.4.11, +others}}; so ὑ. δικαστήριον ὑπαχθείς, ἀγαγόντες, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.72, 104 (compare ὑπάγειν ὑ. τοὺς ἐφόρους [prev. work] 82) probably refers to the elevated seats of the judges in court, compare ὑπάγω A ΙΙ.}}
**C.2** of Position or Extension **under** an object, without sense of motion, Ἀρκαδίην ὑ. Κυλλήνης ὄρος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.603, compare 824, etc.; ἰκριώσασι ὑ. τὴν ὀροφήν “IG” 12.374.76; ἐργασαμένοις τὸ ἄνθεμον ὑ. τὴν ἀσπίδα [prev. work]371.9; τὰ μὲν ὑ. τὸν λόφον καὶ τὰμ φάραγγα “Inscription Prien.” 37.162 (2nd c.BC)}}; ἀνθέντω ὑ. τὸν ναὸν τᾶς Δάματρος {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“IG” 5(1).1498.13 (_locative_ uncertain, 2nd c.BC)}}; ὅσσοι ἔασιν ὑπ᾽ ἠῶ τ᾽ ἠέλιόν τε everywhere **under** the sun, {{LXX+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.267; ὑπ᾽ αὐγὰς ἠελίοιο φοιτῶσι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.181; τῶν ὑ. τοῦτον τὸν ἥλιον.. ἀνθρώπων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.270; τὰ ὑ. τὴν ἄρκτον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.10, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 362a17; οἴκησις ἡ λεγομένη ὑ. τὸν πόλον 1st c.BC: Geminus Astronomicus 5.38, compare 16.21, +others; ὑ. τὸν οὐρανόν LXX.Exo.17.14, +others, “UPZ” 106.14 (1st c.BC)}}; τὸ ὑ. τὴν ἀκρόπολιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.17; ὁ ὑ. γῆν λεγόμενος εἶναι θεός 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.114, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.259}}; ὑ. γῆν is more frequently than ὑ. γῆς in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus, “Mete.” 349b29, +others, in 2nd c.BC: Hipparchus Astronomicus, 1.3.10, +others, and entirely supersedes ὑ. γῆς in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, 2.124, 125, 127, 148, 150, 3.102, 4.195, 7.114, and 1st c.BC: Geminus Astronomicus, 2.19, +others}}; it is found also in {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 21.28.11, etc.}}; ὑ. γῆν **the nadir,** opposed to μεσουράνημα, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PLond.” 1.98r. 49, 110.33 (1st-2nd c.AD)}}; also ἄγχε δέ μιν.. ἱμὰς ἁπαλὴν ὑ. δειρήν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.371; Τρῶες.. πτῶσσον ὑ. κρημνούς 21.26; ἀγέροντο.. ἄλσος ὕ. σκιερόν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.278; τρωφεὶς ὑ. τὸν ὀφθαλμόν “IG” 42(1).122.120 (Epid., 4th c.BC)}}; οὐλὴ ὑπ᾽ ὀφθαλμὸν δεξιόν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen” 76.13 (3rd c.BC)}}; ὑ. τὸ μέρος τοῦ ἐνοφειλομένου ὑπογραψάτω ὅσον ἰδίᾳ ἔχει {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PRev.Laws” 19.2 (3rd c.BC)}}; κείμενος ὑ. τὸν ὀμφαλόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.7, compare 67, +others; ὑ. τὰς πύλας ἵππων πόδες φαίνονται 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.10; μὴ ὑποτιθέναι κύλικα ὑ. τὴν κλίνην “IG” 12(5).593 A 21 (Ceos, 5th c.BC); ὑ. τὸν ὀδόν [prev. work]42(1).102.249 (Epid., 4th c.BC)}}; καταψύξατε ὑ. τὸ δένδρον {{LXX|LXX.Gen.18.4}}; ὑ. τὸν λέβητα{{LXX|LXX.Eccl.7.7(6)}}; ὑ. τοὺς πόδας{{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.Lam.3.34; εἰς τοὺς ὑ. πόδα χωρεῖ τόπους 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 5.75 (see. πούς 1.6 g)}}; ἡ ὑ. πόδα (i.e. γραμμή) the **base** of a triangle, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus}} “*Mens.” 55; also ὑπ᾽ αὐγὰς.. λεύσσουσαι πέπλους **holding** them **up to** the light, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1154}}; also ὑ. τὸν ὀφθαλμόν **close to** the eye, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 874a9; ὑποκειμένης τῆς Εὐβοίας ὑ. τὴν Ἀττικήν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.108; ὑπ᾽ αὐτὴν ἐσχάτην στήλην ἔχων ἔχριμπτ᾽ ἀεὶ σύριγγα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 720; εἰ θεωρήσειεν ὑπ᾽ αὐγὰς τὸν ἀνθρώπειον βίον 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “Protrepticus” 8 (compare αὐγή 1)}}: of subordinate position. κατακλίνεσθαι ὑ. τινά {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Symp.” 9}}; τίς ὑ. τίν; who is **next to** whom, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Onosander (Onasander) Tacticus 10.2.}}
